WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.310
- Welcome to The Huberman Lab Podcast

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.481
for everyday life.

00:00:06.481 --> 00:00:09.300
[upbeat guitar music]

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.410
I'm Andrew Huberman

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.110
and I'm a professor of
neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.060
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.080
Today, we are talking about
obsessive-compulsive disorder

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.560
or OCD.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.550
We are also going to talk

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.910
about obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder which,

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.890
as you will soon learn, is distinct

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.110
from obsessive-compulsive disorder.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.450
In fact, many people that
refer to themselves or others

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.180
as obsessive or compulsive
or quote-unquote,

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.260
having OCD or OCD about
this or OCD about that

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.410
do not have clinically diagnosable OCD,

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:43.050
rather, many people have
obsessive-compulsive

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.940
personality disorder.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.520
However, there are many
people in the world

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.940
that have actual OCD,
and for those people,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.650
there's a tremendous amount of suffering.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.980
In fact, OCD turns out to be number seven

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.680
on the list of most
debilitating illnesses,

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.100
not just psychiatric
illnesses, but of all illnesses

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.220
which is remarkable and
somewhat frightening.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.760
The good news is thanks to
the fields of psychiatry,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.340
psychology, and science in general,

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.040
there are now excellent
treatments for OCD.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.440
We're going to talk about
those treatments today.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.960
Those treatments range
from behavioral therapies,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.260
to drug therapies, and brain stimulation,

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.890
and even some of the more
holistic or natural therapies.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.110
As you'll soon learn, for certain people,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.900
they may want to focus more
on the behavioral therapies,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.260
whereas for others, more
on the drug-based therapies

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.910
and so on and so forth.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.650
One extremely interesting
and important thing

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.120
I learned from this episode

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.910
is that the particular
sequence that behavioral

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.910
and/or drug and/or holistic
therapies are applied

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.160
is extremely important.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:48.720
In fact, the outcomes of studies

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.640
often depend on whether not people start

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:51.990
on drug treatment and then follow

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.810
with cognitive behavioral
treatment or vice versa.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.340
We're going to go into all those details

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.160
and how they relate to
different types of OCD,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.480
because it turns out there are indeed

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.940
different types of
obsessions and compulsions,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.110
and the age of onset for
OCD, and so on and so forth.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.330
What I can assure you is
by the end of this episode,

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.160
you'll have a much greater understanding

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.680
of what OCD is and what it isn't

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.740
and what obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder is

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.060
and what it is not.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:21.720
And you'll have a rich array
of different therapy options

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.990
to explore in yourself or in others

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:26.280
that are suffering from OCD.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.560
And if neither you or others that you know

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.420
suffer from OCD or obsessive-compulsive

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.880
personality disorder,
the information covered

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.520
in today's episode will
also provide insight

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.380
into how the brain and nervous system

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.930
translate thought into action generally.

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:42.180
And also, you're going to learn a lot

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.820
about goal-directed behavior generally.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.320
My hope is that by the end of the episode

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.990
you'll both understand a
lot about this disease state

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.710
that we call OCD, you will
have access to information

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.750
that will allow you to
direct treatments to yourself

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.670
or others in better ways,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.800
and that you will gain greater insight

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.260
into how you function and
how human beings function

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.130
in general.

