00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.730
Let's talk about OCD or
obsessive-compulsive disorder.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:34.860
First of all, as the name suggests,

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:37.620
OCD includes thoughts or obsessions

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.560
and compulsions which are actions.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:43.470
The obsessions and the
compulsions are often linked.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:44.790
In fact, most of the time,

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:46.710
the obsessions and the
compulsions are linked

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:49.470
such that the compulsion, the behavior,

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:52.680
is designed to relieve the obsession.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.570
However, one of the hallmark themes

00:08:54.570 --> 00:08:56.610
of obsessive-compulsive disorder

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.160
is that the obsessions are intrusive.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:00.930
People don't want to have them.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.160
They don't enjoy having them.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:04.020
They just seem to pop into people's minds

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.720
and they seem to pop into
their mind recurrently.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:11.520
And the compulsions, unlike
other sorts of behaviors,

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.640
provide brief relief to the obsession,

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.870
but then very quickly reinforce
or strengthen the obsession.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.000
This is a very key theme to realize

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.530
about obsessive-compulsive disorder

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:24.030
so I'm just going to repeat it again.

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:26.850
These two features, first,
the fact that the obsessions

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:29.940
are intrusive and recurrent,

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:32.880
as well as the fact that the
compulsions, the behaviors,

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.750
provide, if anything, only
brief relief for the obsessions,

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.610
but in most cases simply serve to make

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:42.000
the obsessions stronger
are the hallmark features

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.710
of obsessive-compulsive disorder.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.180
And it turns out to be very important

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:47.550
to keep these in mind as we go forward,

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:50.520
not just because they define
obsessive-compulsive disorder,

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.290
but they also define
the sorts of treatments

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.970
that will and will not work

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:55.680
for obsessive-compulsive disorder.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.270
And then once you understand a little bit

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:58.800
about the neural circuitry underlying

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:00.150
obsessive-compulsive disorder,

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:02.040
which we'll talk about in a few moments,

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.780
then you will clearly understand

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.120
why being a quote-unquote,
obsessive person

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.460
or having obsessive-compulsive personality

00:10:08.460 --> 00:10:10.710
is not the same as OCD.

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.630
In fact, we can leap ahead a little bit

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:14.700
and compare and contrast OCD

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:17.160
with obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:20.160
along one very particular set of features.

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.170
Again, I'll go into this
in more detail later,

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:25.860
but it's fair to say
that OCD is characterized

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.530
by these recurrent and
intrusive obsessions.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:30.510
And as I mentioned before,
the fact that those obsessions

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:33.090
get stronger as a function of people

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:34.530
performing certain behaviors.

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.600
So unlike an itch that you feel,

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.340
and then you scratch
it and it feels better,

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:40.590
OCD is more like an itch that
you feel, you scratch it,

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:42.870
and the itch intensifies.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.510
That contour or that pattern of behaviors

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.880
and thoughts interacting is very different

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.340
than obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder,

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:54.060
which mainly involves a sense
of delayed gratification

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.550
that people want and somewhat enjoy

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:58.350
because it allows them to function better

00:10:58.350 --> 00:10:59.880
or more in line with how they would like

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.290
to show up in the world.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:04.410
So again, OCD has mainly
to do with obsessions

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:05.550
that are intrusive and recurrent,

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.980
whereas obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.260
does not have that
intrusive feature to it.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:12.450
People do not mind, or in fact,

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:15.630
often invite or like the
particular patterns of thought

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.570
that lead them to be compulsive
along certain dimensions.

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:20.280
So leaving aside obsessive-compulsive

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:22.320
personality disorder for the moment,

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.750
let's focus a bit more on OCD and define

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:26.880
how it tends to show up in the world.

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:31.170
First of all, OCD is extremely common.

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:33.510
In fact, current estimates
are that anywhere

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:36.750
from 2.5% to as high as 3

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:41.010
or even 4% of people suffer from true OCD,

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.890
that is an astonishingly high number.

00:11:43.890 --> 00:11:45.900
Now, the reason the range is so big,

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.200
2.5% all the way up to
3, or maybe even 4%,

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:52.230
is that a lot of the features of OCD

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:56.190
go unnoticed both in
the clinician's office

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:58.890
and simply because people don't report it

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:00.330
and don't talk about it.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.160
In fact, it is possible to have recurrent

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.650
and intrusive obsessions and not engage

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.540
in the sorts of behaviors
that would ever allow

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:09.330
people to notice that somebody has OCD.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:12.480
That can be because some
of the intrusive thoughts

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.430
don't actually lead to overt behaviors

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.840
like hand washing or checking

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.280
that other people would notice.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.980
It can also be because people learn

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:22.590
to disguise or hide their obsessions

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:24.330
and their compulsions out of shame

00:12:24.330 --> 00:12:27.660
or fear of looking strange
or whatever it might be

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.610
such that they have these obsessive

00:12:29.610 --> 00:12:32.580
and intrusive thoughts, and
they do little micro-behaviors

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.770
like they might tap their
fingers on their thigh

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.410
as a way to avoid, at
least in their own mind,

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.180
something catastrophic happening.

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:40.850
That might seem crazy to you,

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:43.140
it might seem bizarre, but
this is the sort of thing

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.450
that operates in a lot of people.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:46.980
And I really want to emphasize this

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:50.100
because the clinical
literature that are out there

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:54.060
really point to the fact
that many people have OCD,

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.160
full blown OCD, and never report it

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.960
because of the kind of shame and hiding

00:12:57.960 --> 00:12:59.520
associated with it.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.310
Another thing to point out is that OCD

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:04.740
is extremely debilitating.

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.150
I mentioned this a few minutes ago,

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:09.030
but OCD is currently
listed as number seven

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:11.610
in terms of the most
debilitating illnesses,

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:15.030
not just mental illnesses or disorders,

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:16.710
but all types of illnesses

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:19.530
including things like asthma
and cancer, et cetera.

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:23.340
So you can imagine with that
standing at number seven,

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.650
that it is both extremely common

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:27.120
and extremely debilitating.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.640
And as a consequence, it's now realized

00:13:29.640 --> 00:13:33.990
that many hours, days,
weeks, months, or even years

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.050
of work performance or showing up at work

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.780
of relational interactions really suffer

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.490
as a consequence of people having OCD.

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:43.800
So this is a vital problem
that the scientific

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:46.440
and psychiatric and psychological
communities understand.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.560
And it's one of the reasons
that I'm doing this podcast.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:52.410
And of course, I received
a ton of interest in OCD

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:55.047
because of this incredibly
high incidence of OCD

00:13:55.047 --> 00:13:56.970
and how debilitating it is.

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.180
We could go really deep into
why it's so debilitating.

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:01.410
I don't want to spend
too much time on that

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:03.150
because I think most of
that is pretty obvious,

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:04.530
but some of it is not.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:06.690
For instance, one of the
things that makes OCD

00:14:06.690 --> 00:14:08.370
so debilitating is, of course,

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.200
the shame that we talked about before.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:13.020
But it's also the fact that
when people are focusing

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:14.640
on their obsessions and their compulsions,

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:16.590
they're not able to focus on other things.

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:18.000
That's simply the way
that the brain works.

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.430
We're not able to focus on
too many things at once.

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:23.610
The other thing is that
OCD takes a lot of time

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:25.350
out of people's lives.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.720
With recurrent intrusive
thoughts happening

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:30.060
at very high frequency, or
even at moderate frequency,

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.850
people are spending a lot of
time thinking about this stuff

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:34.770
and they're thinking about the behaviors

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:36.870
they need to engage in, and
then engaging in the behaviors,

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:37.890
which as I mentioned before,

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:39.207
just serve to strengthen the compulsions

00:14:39.207 --> 00:14:41.580
and so they're not actually
doing the other things

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.350
that make us functional human beings

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.720
like commuting to work or doing homework

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:48.030
or doing work or listening
when people are talking

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:49.710
or interacting or sports or working out,

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:52.740
all the things that make
for a rich quality life

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:56.040
are taken over by OCD in many cases.

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:57.840
So while that might be obvious to some,

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.550
I'm not sure that it's
obvious to everybody

00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:02.550
just how much time OCD can occupy.

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:05.250
Another thing you'll
soon learn is that sadly,

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.890
a lot of the obsessions
and compulsions in OCD

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:09.720
often relate to taboo topics.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:12.750
And that's because the
general categories of OCD

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:15.480
fall into three different bins,

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.850
checking obsessions and compulsions,

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.250
repetition obsessions and compulsions,

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:22.410
and order obsessions and compulsions.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.000
The checking ones are somewhat obvious,

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.310
checking the stove or checking the locks,

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:27.720
which I think we all tend to do.

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:30.510
I'm somebody typically
I'll head off to the car

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:32.097
to commute to work and I'll think,

00:15:32.097 --> 00:15:33.150
did I lock the front door,

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:34.590
and I'll go back once,

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:36.840
but I won't go back twice or 50 times.

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:40.320
People with OCD will often
go back 20 or 30 times

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.510
before they'll actually allow
themselves to drive off.

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:44.790
And then it's a real challenge for them

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.340
to continue to drive off
and discard with the idea

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:48.570
that they didn't check the stove

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:49.650
or they didn't check the locks

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:52.320
or they didn't check
something else critical.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.690
Repetition obsessions and compulsions,

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:57.870
obviously can dovetail
with the the checking ones,

00:15:57.870 --> 00:16:00.030
but those tend to be
things like counting off

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:02.250
of a certain number of numbers,

00:16:02.250 --> 00:16:04.110
like one, two, three,
four, five, six, seven,

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:05.520
seven, six, five, four, three, two, one.

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:08.400
People perform that repeatedly,
repeatedly, repeatedly,

00:16:08.400 --> 00:16:09.853
or feel that they have to.

00:16:09.853 --> 00:16:13.230
I remember years ago
watching a documentary about

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:15.000
the band, The Ramones, right?

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.380
Most people heard of The Ramones, right?

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:18.180
Jeans, T-shirts, aviator glasses,

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:19.740
everyone had to change
their last name to Ramone.

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:21.000
They weren't actually all related

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:22.260
to one another, by the way.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:23.520
You had to change your
last name to Ramone.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:25.590
The Ramones had one band member

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:29.490
who was admittedly and known
to others as having OCD.

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:32.070
And during that documentary,
which I forget the name,

00:16:32.070 --> 00:16:33.420
I think it was called,

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:34.410
can't remember, anyway,

00:16:34.410 --> 00:16:37.140
can't remember, hippocampal lapse there,

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:38.580
but in this documentary,

00:16:38.580 --> 00:16:41.760
the band members describe Joey Ramone

00:16:41.760 --> 00:16:44.970
as leaving hotels, walking down the stairs

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:46.110
to the parking lot, but then having

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:47.910
to walk up and down them
seven or eight times,

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:49.530
and sometimes getting out of the van again

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.197
and walking up and down
them seven or eight times

00:16:51.197 --> 00:16:53.610
and it always had to be a
certain number of times,

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:55.080
given a certain number of stairs.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.700
This appears, quote-unquote, crazy,

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.010
but of course, we don't want
to think of this as crazy.

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:03.240
This is somebody who very
likely had full blown OCD.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.190
Now that particular
example, believe it or not,

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.860
is not all that uncommon.

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:09.720
It just so happens that that example

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.670
entailed certain compulsions and behaviors

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:14.820
that were overt and that
other people could see.

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:16.890
And you can imagine how
that would prevent somebody

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.570
from moving about their daily life easily.

00:17:18.570 --> 00:17:20.370
A lot of people, as I mentioned before,

00:17:20.370 --> 00:17:22.770
have obsessions and
compulsions that they hide

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:24.300
and they do these little micro behaviors,

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.430
or they'll just count off in their head

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.650
as opposed to generating
some sort of walking up

00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:31.440
and downstairs or tapping
or things of that sort.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:33.000
So we have checking, we have repetition,

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.650
and then there's order.

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:37.080
Order oftentimes is thought of

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.480
as putting cleanliness

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.490
or making sure everything is aligned

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.740
and perfect and orderly.

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:45.390
And oftentimes that is the case,

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:46.740
but there are other forms of order

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:48.990
that people with OCD can focus on

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:51.720
in a obsessive and compulsive way.

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:54.690
Things like incompleteness, the idea that

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.370
one can't walk away from something

00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:58.650
or stop doing something
because something's not right

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:00.570
or complete in that picture.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:02.970
It could be the way the table is set.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.100
It could be the way that
something's written on a page.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:06.780
It could be an email.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:09.690
Again, now we're still talking
about OCD, the disorder.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.790
We're not talking about
obsessive-compulsive

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:13.860
personality disorder.

00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.200
I'm aware of, well, I'll just be direct,

00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:18.510
several colleagues of mine
and it's just remarkable,

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:20.280
the order in their emails.

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:23.310
Every email is perfect,
punctuated, perfect,

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:25.920
grammar, perfect,
everything's spaced perfect.

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:26.820
Do they have OCD?

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:27.990
Well, they might, they might not.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:31.350
How would I know unless
they disclose that to me.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:33.600
But they might have obsessive-compulsive

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:36.180
personality disorder, or
they just might be able

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:37.920
to generate a lot of order

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:39.210
and they have a lot of discipline

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:40.980
around the way they write,
and the way they present

00:18:40.980 --> 00:18:43.680
any communication with anybody at all.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:47.880
So if somebody has a OCD
that's in the domain of order,

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:51.000
it could be incompleteness
and the constant feeling

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:52.890
of something not being completed

00:18:52.890 --> 00:18:54.270
and a need to complete it.

00:18:54.270 --> 00:18:56.190
It can also be in terms of symmetry,

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.740
that everything be aligned
in symmetric in some way.

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.920
This could be seen perhaps in young kids.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:04.050
This is one example that
I read in the literature

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:06.600
of children that need to
arrange their stuffed animals

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:09.570
in exact same order every day

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:12.270
and in a particular order to the point

00:19:12.270 --> 00:19:14.640
where if you were to move
the little stuffed frog

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:15.930
over next to the stuffed rabbit,

00:19:15.930 --> 00:19:19.680
that the child would have
an anxiety reaction to that

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:23.520
and feel literally
compelled, driven to fix that

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.400
maybe even multiple times
over and over again.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:27.960
We'll talk about OCD in children

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:29.400
versus adults in a little bit.

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:31.650
And then the other aspect of order,

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:33.900
which is a little bit less than intuitive,

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:35.610
is this notion of disgust,

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:37.470
this idea that something is contaminated.

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:41.280
So we often think about OCD
and hand washing behavior

00:19:41.280 --> 00:19:42.810
in response to people feeling

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:44.250
that something is contaminated,

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:46.290
a space, a towel, et cetera,

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.510
or even simply somebody else's hand

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:50.820
and so they're unwilling
to shake somebody's hand.

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:53.520
You can imagine how these
different bins of obsessions

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:56.820
and compulsions, checking
repetition and order

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.130
be extremely debilitating
depending on how severe

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.410
they are and how many
different domains of life

00:20:01.410 --> 00:20:02.790
they show up in.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.710
Because oftentimes in movies

00:20:04.710 --> 00:20:06.660
and even the way I'm describing it now

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:09.420
it sounds as if, okay, well
somebody has to check the locks

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.220
but they don't have to
also check the stove,

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.940
or somebody has the need to
count to seven back and forth

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:17.400
up to seven and down to seven seven times

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:20.370
seven times a day or
something of that sort

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:23.220
where they need symmetry in
very specific domains of life.

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:24.870
But it turns out that this recurrent

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:27.270
and intrusive aspect of obsessions

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:29.220
leads people with OCD

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:31.950
to have checking repetition

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:35.460
and/or order compulsions everywhere.

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:38.130
So whether or not somebody
is at work or in school

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:39.510
or trying to engage in sport

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:40.950
or trying to engage in relationship

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:43.620
or just something simple
like walking down the street,

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:45.930
the obsessions are so
intrusive that they show up

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:48.750
and they compel people to
do things in that domain

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.150
independent of whether or not

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.100
they happen to be in
one location or another.

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:55.737
In other words, the thought patterns

00:20:55.737 --> 00:20:57.930
and the behaviors take
over the environment

00:20:57.930 --> 00:20:59.280
as opposed to the environment

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.500
driving the thought
patterns and behaviors.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:03.150
So it therefore becomes impossible

00:21:03.150 --> 00:21:05.250
to ever find a room that's clean enough,

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:07.800
to find a bed that's made well enough,

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:10.770
to find anything that's done well enough

00:21:10.770 --> 00:21:13.200
to remove the obsession.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:14.937
And I know I've said
it multiple times now,

00:21:14.937 --> 00:21:18.030
but I'm going to say it many
times throughout this episode

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:21.960
in a somewhat obsessive,
but I believe justified way

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:25.620
that every time that one
engages in the compulsion

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:26.820
related to the obsession,

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.820
the obsession simply becomes stronger.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:31.350
So you can imagine what a powerful

00:21:31.350 --> 00:21:33.390
and debilitating loop that really is.

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:34.770
So let's drill a little bit deeper

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:36.510
into how the obsessions and compulsions

00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:37.890
relate to one another.

00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:39.987
If we were to draw a line
between the obsessions

00:21:39.987 --> 00:21:43.740
and the compulsions, that
line could be described

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.720
as anxiety.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:47.880
Now, we need to define what anxiety is

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:49.920
and to be quite honest,

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:52.470
most of psychology and science can't agree

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:54.120
on exactly what anxiety is.

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:56.250
Typically the way we think about fear

00:21:56.250 --> 00:21:59.070
is that it's a heightened
state of autonomic arousal,

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:00.990
so increased heart rate,
increased breathing,

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:03.720
sweating, et cetera, in
response to an immediate

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:06.360
and present threat or perceived threat.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.580
Whereas anxiety, generally speaking

00:22:08.580 --> 00:22:09.990
in the scientific literature,

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:13.740
relates to the same
sorts of thought patterns

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:16.380
and somatic bodily responses, heart rate,

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:17.580
breathing, et cetera,

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:20.730
but without a clear and present danger

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:23.010
being in the environment or right there.

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:25.860
So that's the way that we're
going to talk about anxiety now.

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.620
And anxiety is really
what binds the obsessions

00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:30.480
and compulsions such
that someone will have

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:31.710
an intrusive thought.

00:22:31.710 --> 00:22:34.200
So for instance, someone
will have the thought that

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:36.930
if they turn left on any street,

00:22:36.930 --> 00:22:38.610
that something bad will happen.

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:39.540
Okay, that's an obsession.

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:41.430
It's actually not all that uncommon.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.590
Now, how bad and what the specificity

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.420
of that bad thing really is will vary.

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:47.430
Some people will think, if I turn left,

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:48.840
something generally bad will happen,

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:50.190
it just makes me feel anxious,

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:52.110
So they always insist on going right.

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:55.230
Whereas other people will
think if I turn left,

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:58.200
so and so will die, or I will die,

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.450
or something terrible will happen,

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:02.670
I'll get a disease or someone
else will get a disease

00:23:02.670 --> 00:23:04.650
or I'll be cursing myself or somebody else

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:06.630
in some very specific way.

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:10.860
This is unfortunately quite
common in people with OCD.

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:13.560
So they have this feeling

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:14.970
and the feeling can be generally

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:17.220
or specifically related
to a particular outcome.

00:23:17.220 --> 00:23:20.070
But beneath that is a feeling of anxiety,

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:22.080
a quickening of the heartbeat,

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:23.550
a quickening of breathing,

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:26.310
a narrowing of one's visual focus.

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:28.110
I've talked about this
before in another podcast,

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:29.760
the Master Stress, another podcast

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:31.440
but if you haven't heard those,

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:32.580
let me just briefly describe

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:34.650
that when we are in a state of increased

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:36.480
so-called autonomic arousal, alertness,

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:38.190
stress, et cetera,

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:40.440
our visual field literally narrows,

00:23:40.440 --> 00:23:42.960
the aperture of our
visual field gets smaller

00:23:42.960 --> 00:23:44.220
and that's because of the relationship

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:45.600
between the autonomic nervous system

00:23:45.600 --> 00:23:48.660
and your visual system, so
you start seeing the world

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:50.670
through sort of soda straw view

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:52.080
or through binocular-like view,

00:23:52.080 --> 00:23:53.610
as opposed to seeing the big picture.

00:23:53.610 --> 00:23:54.630
Why is that important?

00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:58.800
Well, it literally sharpens
and narrows your focus

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:00.810
toward the very thing that the obsessions

00:24:00.810 --> 00:24:02.100
and the compulsions are focused on.

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:04.230
So the person walking
down the street who sees

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:06.240
the opportunity to go left or right

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:09.660
will only see the bad decision,

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:12.540
their visual field narrows very tightly

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:15.210
along that possibility
of taking a left turn.

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:17.640
And I know as I describe this
seems totally irrational,

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:20.460
but I want to emphasize
that the person with OCD

00:24:20.460 --> 00:24:22.440
knows it's irrational.

00:24:22.440 --> 00:24:25.890
They might feel crazy because
they're having these thoughts,

00:24:25.890 --> 00:24:27.990
but they know it makes no sense whatsoever

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:30.870
that left somehow would
be different than right

00:24:30.870 --> 00:24:33.270
in terms of outcomes in
this particular case,

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:36.090
and yet it feels as if it would.

00:24:36.090 --> 00:24:38.490
In fact, in some cases it
feels as if they went left,

00:24:38.490 --> 00:24:40.830
they would have a full blown panic attack.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:44.010
So the idea here is that the
obsessions and compulsions

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:47.190
are bound by anxiety, but then by taking

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:50.430
a right-hand turn, again, in
this one particular example,

00:24:50.430 --> 00:24:53.910
by taking a right-hand
turn, there's a very brief,

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:57.690
I should mention, very
brief relief of that anxiety

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:01.590
at the time of the decision
to go right, not left

00:25:01.590 --> 00:25:05.100
and there's an additional drop in anxiety

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:07.380
while one takes the right-hand turn

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:09.630
as opposed to the left-hand turn.

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:11.700
And then as I alluded to before,

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:14.310
there's a reinforcement of the compulsion.

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:16.140
In other words, by going right,

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.960
it doesn't create a situation in the brain

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:21.900
and psychology of the person
that, oh, you know what,

00:25:21.900 --> 00:25:25.110
I'm not anxious anymore, left
would've probably been okay.

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:28.470
It reinforces the idea that
right made me feel better,

00:25:28.470 --> 00:25:30.270
or turning right made me feel better,

00:25:30.270 --> 00:25:32.700
and going left would've
been that much worse.

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:35.700
Again, it reinforces the
obsession even further.

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:40.230
And again, we could swap out
right turns and left turns

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:41.910
with something like hand washing,

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:44.310
the feeling that something is contaminated

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:45.780
and the need to wash one's hands

00:25:45.780 --> 00:25:48.060
even though one already washed their hands

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:50.160
20, 30, 50 times prior.

00:25:50.160 --> 00:25:51.630
And we're actually going
to go back to that example

00:25:51.630 --> 00:25:53.640
a little bit later when we talk about

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:55.530
one particular category of therapies

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:58.620
that are very effective
in many people for OCD

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:01.680
which are the cognitive
behavioral and exposure therapies.

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:02.880
I think some of you have heard

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:04.950
of cognitive behavioral
and exposure therapies,

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:07.110
but the way they're used to treat OCD

00:26:07.110 --> 00:26:09.630
is very much different
than the way they're used

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:12.390
to treat other sorts of anxiety disorders

00:26:12.390 --> 00:26:14.700
and other sorts of disorders generally.

00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:19.230
So it's fair to say that up
to 70% of people with OCD

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:22.920
have some sort of anxiety
or elevated anxiety,

00:26:22.920 --> 00:26:25.170
either directly related to the OCD

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:26.880
or indirectly related to the OCD

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:28.530
and it's really hard to tease those apart

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:31.680
because OCD can create its own anxiety,

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:34.740
as I mentioned before, it can
even increase its own anxiety.

00:26:34.740 --> 00:26:37.710
And there's also an issue of depression.

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:40.500
Having OCD can be very depressing,

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:43.590
especially if some of these
OCD thoughts and behaviors

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:45.300
start to really impede people's ability

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:46.230
to function in life.

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:47.760
At work, and school, and relationship,

00:26:47.760 --> 00:26:49.830
they can start feeling
less optimistic about life.

00:26:49.830 --> 00:26:52.590
And in fact, some people can
become suicidally depressed.

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:55.380
That's how bad OCD can be for us.

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:57.660
So we have to be careful when saying

00:26:57.660 --> 00:27:00.570
that 70% of people with
OCD also have anxiety

00:27:00.570 --> 00:27:02.850
or X number of people with
OCD are also depressed

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:04.170
because we don't know whether or not

00:27:04.170 --> 00:27:06.750
the depression led the OCD
or the other way around

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:07.890
or whether or not they're operating,

00:27:07.890 --> 00:27:09.840
as we say in science, in parallel.

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:11.880
Some of the drug treatments
for OCD and depression

00:27:11.880 --> 00:27:14.340
and anxiety can tease some of that apart

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:15.630
and we'll talk about that,

00:27:15.630 --> 00:27:17.130
but I think it's fair to say that

00:27:17.130 --> 00:27:21.000
what binds the obsessions
and compulsions is anxiety,

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:22.820
that there's a feeling of,

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:25.080
or I should say an
urgent feeling of a need

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:27.210
to get rid of the obsession.

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:29.190
And the person feels as if the only way

00:27:29.190 --> 00:27:30.630
they can do that is to engage

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:32.400
in a particular compulsive behavior.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:33.750
Some people are probably wondering

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:36.330
if there's a genetic component to OCD

00:27:36.330 --> 00:27:37.980
and indeed there is,

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:40.560
although the nature of
it isn't exactly clear.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:43.170
And oftentimes when
people hear that something

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:44.610
has a genetic component,

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:48.210
they think it's always directly
inherited from a parent,

00:27:48.210 --> 00:27:49.680
and that's not always the case.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:52.740
There can be genes that
surface in siblings

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:54.510
or genes that surface in children

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:56.580
that are not readily apparent

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:58.080
in terms of what we call a phenotype.

00:27:58.080 --> 00:27:59.340
So you have a genotype, the gene,

00:27:59.340 --> 00:28:01.470
and then you have a
phenotype, the way it shows up

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:04.230
as a body form or like eye color

00:28:04.230 --> 00:28:06.870
or how it shows up in terms of a behavior

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:08.880
or behavioral pattern.

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.820
Based on twin studies where researchers

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:14.430
have examined identical
twins, fraternal twins,

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:17.880
even identical twins that
share the same sack in utero,

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:19.680
the what we call monochorionic,

00:28:19.680 --> 00:28:22.290
so sitting in the same
little bag during pregnancy

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:23.760
or in different little bags,

00:28:23.760 --> 00:28:24.840
you can see different levels

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:26.760
of what's called genetic concordance.

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:29.400
But if we were to just
sort of cut a broad swath

00:28:29.400 --> 00:28:31.140
through all of the genetic data,

00:28:31.140 --> 00:28:35.340
it's fair to say that about
40 to 50% of OCD cases

00:28:35.340 --> 00:28:36.780
have some genetic component,

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:40.230
some mutation or some
inherited aspect that's genetic

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:41.070
and that one could point to

00:28:41.070 --> 00:28:43.200
if they got their genome mapped.

00:28:43.200 --> 00:28:44.310
Now, while that's interesting,

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:46.590
I don't think it's terribly
useful for most people.

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:48.960
First of all, you can't
really control your genes,

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:50.760
at least at this point in history,

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:53.220
even though there are things
like epigenetic control

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:55.110
and people are very
excited about technologies

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:57.220
like CRISPR for modifying the genome

00:28:58.110 --> 00:28:59.643
in humans at some point,

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.640
most people can't control
their genetics, right?

00:29:02.640 --> 00:29:04.770
You can't pick who your
parents were as they say.

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:07.170
So just know that there
is a genetic component

00:29:07.170 --> 00:29:10.830
in about half of people
with OCD, but not always.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:13.290
Now as is typical for this podcast,

00:29:13.290 --> 00:29:15.960
I want to focus on some
of the neural mechanisms

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.150
and chemical systems in the brain and body

00:29:18.150 --> 00:29:20.760
that generate
obsessive-compulsive disorder.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:23.070
In fact, if you've watched
this podcast before,

00:29:23.070 --> 00:29:24.600
listen to this podcast before,

00:29:24.600 --> 00:29:26.310
this is always how I structure things.

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:28.560
First, we introduce a topic

00:29:28.560 --> 00:29:30.390
and we explore that topic in detail

00:29:30.390 --> 00:29:32.610
and really define what
it is and what it isn't.

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:35.190
And then it's very
important that we focus on

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:36.990
what is known and what is not known

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:40.170
about the biological
mechanisms that generate

00:29:40.170 --> 00:29:41.760
whatever that thing happens
to be, in this case,

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:44.247
OCD and obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder.

00:29:44.247 --> 00:29:46.980
Now I want to emphasize
that even if you don't have

00:29:46.980 --> 00:29:48.780
a background in biology,

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:50.760
I will make this information
accessible to you.

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:52.860
And I also want to emphasize
that for those of you

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:54.450
that are interested in treatments

00:29:54.450 --> 00:29:58.350
and are anxiously awaiting
the description of things

00:29:58.350 --> 00:30:02.040
that can help with OCD, I encourage you,

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:04.650
if you will, to please try and digest

00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:06.660
some of the material about
the underlying mechanisms

00:30:06.660 --> 00:30:08.760
because understanding
even just a little bit

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:11.250
of those biological
mechanisms can really help

00:30:11.250 --> 00:30:13.980
shed light on why particular drug

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:15.087
and behavioral treatments

00:30:15.087 --> 00:30:18.120
and other sorts of treatments
work and don't work.

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:20.970
This is especially
important in the case of OCD

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:22.680
where it turns out that the order

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:24.480
and type of treatment can really vary

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:26.040
according to individual,

00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:29.010
and that's something really
special and important about OCD

00:30:29.010 --> 00:30:31.200
that we really can't say
for a number of the other

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:32.760
sorts of disorders that we've described

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:34.350
on previous podcasts.

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:35.700
So let's take a step back

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:37.680
and look at the neural circuitry.

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:40.800
What's going on in the brain
and body of people with OCD?

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:43.410
Why the intrusive recurrent thoughts?

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:44.430
Why the compulsions?

00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:48.390
Why is that whole system bound by anxiety?

00:30:48.390 --> 00:30:50.460
And in some ways in thinking about that,

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:51.840
I want you to keep in mind

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:55.623
that the brain has two main functions.

00:30:56.460 --> 00:30:58.800
The brain's main functions are

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:00.900
to take care of all
the housekeeping stuff,

00:31:00.900 --> 00:31:02.040
make sure digestion works,

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:03.030
make sure the heart beats,

00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:05.190
make sure you keep
breathing no matter what,

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:08.220
make sure that you can see, you can hear,

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:09.450
you can smell, et cetera,

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:12.180
the basic stuff, and then
there's an enormous amount

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:14.490
of brain real estate that's designed

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:16.950
to allow you to predict
what's going to happen next,

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:17.920
either in the immediate future

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:20.100
or in the long-term future.

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:23.030
And largely that's done based
on your knowledge of the past.

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:24.480
So you also have memory systems.

00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:27.000
And of course you have
systems in the brain and body

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:29.790
that are designed to bind what's happening

00:31:29.790 --> 00:31:31.860
at the housekeeping level,
like your heart rate,

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:34.350
to your anticipation of
what's going to happen next.

00:31:34.350 --> 00:31:36.090
So if you're thinking about
something very fearful,

00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:37.650
your body will have one type of reaction.

00:31:37.650 --> 00:31:39.300
If you're thinking about
something very pleasant

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:41.910
and relaxing, your body will
have another type of reaction.

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:44.940
So whenever I hear about
the brain-body distinction,

00:31:44.940 --> 00:31:47.010
I have to just remind everybody

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:48.420
that there really is no distinction

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:50.520
between brain and body
when you think about it

00:31:50.520 --> 00:31:51.480
through the nervous system.

00:31:51.480 --> 00:31:54.030
The nervous system is the brain,
the eyes, the spinal cord,

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.410
but of course all their connections

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:56.550
with all the organs of the body

00:31:56.550 --> 00:31:58.260
and the connections of
all the organs of the body

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:00.330
with the brain, the
spinal cord, et cetera.

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:02.880
So as I describe these neural circuits,

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:03.870
I don't want you to think of them

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:05.760
as just things happening in the head,

00:32:05.760 --> 00:32:07.800
they are certainly happening in the head,

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:10.710
in fact, the circuits all
described most in detail

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:13.290
do exist within the confines
of your cranial vault,

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:15.030
that's nerd speak for skull,

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:19.170
but those circuits are
driving particular predictions

00:32:19.170 --> 00:32:21.420
and therefore particular biases

00:32:21.420 --> 00:32:23.490
towards particular actions in your body.

00:32:23.490 --> 00:32:25.020
They're creating a state of readiness

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:28.710
or a state of desire to
check or desire to count

00:32:28.710 --> 00:32:32.100
or desire to avoid et cetera, et cetera.

00:32:32.100 --> 00:32:33.450
So what are these circuits?

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.480
Well, there's been a lot
of wonderful research

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:38.760
exploring the neural circuit's underlying

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:40.800
obsessive-compulsive disorder

00:32:40.800 --> 00:32:41.940
and that's mainly been accomplished

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:43.500
through a couple of methods.

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:45.990
Most of those methods
when applied in humans

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:50.700
involve getting some look
into which brain areas

00:32:50.700 --> 00:32:53.070
are active when people
are having obsessions

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:55.770
and when people are
engaging in compulsions.

00:32:55.770 --> 00:32:57.390
Now that might seem simple to do,

00:32:57.390 --> 00:33:00.510
but of course your brain is
housed inside the cranial vault.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:01.800
And in order to look inside it,

00:33:01.800 --> 00:33:04.110
you have to use things like
magnetic resonance imaging,

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:07.530
which is just fancy technology
for looking at blood flow,

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:11.040
which relates to activation
of neurons, nerve cells,

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:14.040
or things lik PET, P-E-T, imaging,

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:15.690
which has nothing to do with the verb pet

00:33:15.690 --> 00:33:17.610
and has nothing to do with your house pet,

00:33:17.610 --> 00:33:20.460
has everything to do with
positron emission tomography,

00:33:20.460 --> 00:33:22.140
which is just another way of seeing

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:23.520
which brain areas are active

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:25.440
and then you can also
use PET to figure out

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:27.180
what sorts of neurochemicals are active,

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:29.220
like dopamine, et cetera.

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:32.640
Many studies, we can fairly say dozens

00:33:32.640 --> 00:33:33.810
if not hundreds of studies,

00:33:33.810 --> 00:33:36.630
have now identified a particular circuit

00:33:36.630 --> 00:33:39.480
or loop of brain areas
that are interconnected

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:43.263
and very active in
obsessive-compulsive disorder.

00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:47.370
That loop includes the cortex,

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:50.910
which is kind of the outer
shell of the human brain.

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:53.730
The lumpy stuff, as
it's sometimes appears,

00:33:53.730 --> 00:33:55.200
if the skull is removed.

00:33:55.200 --> 00:33:57.510
And it involves an area
called the striatum

00:33:57.510 --> 00:33:59.220
which is involved in action selection

00:33:59.220 --> 00:34:00.870
and holding back action.

00:34:00.870 --> 00:34:03.180
The striatum is involved
in what's commonly called

00:34:03.180 --> 00:34:05.460
go and no-go types of behaviors.

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:07.320
So every type of behavior
like picking up a pen

00:34:07.320 --> 00:34:11.430
or a mug of coffee involves
a go type function.

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:13.530
It involves generating an action.

00:34:13.530 --> 00:34:16.140
But every time I resist an action,

00:34:16.140 --> 00:34:17.730
my nervous system is also doing that

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:20.880
using this brain structure, the striatum,

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:24.060
which includes, among other
things, the basal ganglia.

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:25.170
We've talked about that before.

00:34:25.170 --> 00:34:27.180
I'm not trying to overload
you with terminology here,

00:34:27.180 --> 00:34:29.520
but I know some people are
interested in terminology.

00:34:29.520 --> 00:34:31.470
So you have go behaviors
and you have no-go,

00:34:31.470 --> 00:34:34.923
resisting of behaviors,
not going toward behavior.

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:39.030
The cortex and the striatum
are in this intricate

00:34:39.030 --> 00:34:39.990
back and forth talk.

00:34:39.990 --> 00:34:41.370
It's really loops of connections.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:43.110
The cortex doesn't tell
the striatum what to do,

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.090
the striatum doesn't tell
the cortex what to do.

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:45.990
They're in a crosstalk.

00:34:45.990 --> 00:34:47.430
Like any good relationship, there's a lot

00:34:47.430 --> 00:34:49.030
of back and forth communication.

00:34:49.890 --> 00:34:53.700
There's a third element in
this cortico-striatal loop

00:34:53.700 --> 00:34:56.070
as it's called, and that's the thalamus.

00:34:56.070 --> 00:34:57.450
Now, the thalamus is not a structure

00:34:57.450 --> 00:34:59.610
I've talked a lot about
before on this podcast,

00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:02.040
but it's one of my favorite
structures to think about

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:03.630
and teach about in neuroanatomy,

00:35:03.630 --> 00:35:05.520
which I teach back at Stanford

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:07.470
and I've taught for many years elsewhere

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:09.540
because the thalamus is this incredible

00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:12.360
egg-like structure in
the center of your brain

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:14.520
that has different channels through it.

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:17.400
Channels for relaying visual information

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:20.550
or auditory information
or touch information

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.970
from your environment up into your cortex,

00:35:23.970 --> 00:35:26.160
and as a consequence,
making certain things

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:28.620
that are happening to you and around you

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:30.600
apparent to you, making you aware of them,

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:34.440
making you perceive them
and suppressing others.

00:35:34.440 --> 00:35:35.580
So for instance, right now,

00:35:35.580 --> 00:35:37.170
if you're hearing me say this,

00:35:37.170 --> 00:35:40.440
your thalamus has what are
called auditory nuclei,

00:35:40.440 --> 00:35:41.850
there's collections of neurons

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:44.970
that respond to sound waves

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:46.980
that are of course coming
in through your ears,

00:35:46.980 --> 00:35:49.713
and your thalamus is active in a way

00:35:49.713 --> 00:35:52.320
that those particular
regions of your thalamus

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:55.980
are allowed, literally permitted
to pass the information

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:58.620
coming from your ears
through some other steps

00:35:58.620 --> 00:36:00.630
but then to your thalamus,
your auditory thalamus,

00:36:00.630 --> 00:36:02.190
then up to your cortex and you can hear

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:03.900
what I'm saying right now.

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:06.840
At the same time, your
thalamus is surrounded

00:36:06.840 --> 00:36:07.920
by a kind of a shell,

00:36:07.920 --> 00:36:09.990
something called the
thalamic reticular nucleus.

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:11.430
Again, you don't have
to remember the names,

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:13.200
but this thalamic reticular nucleus,

00:36:13.200 --> 00:36:15.750
also sometimes called the
reticular thalamic nucleus,

00:36:15.750 --> 00:36:18.240
this is, believe it or not, a
subject of debate in science.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:19.920
There are people that
literally hated each other,

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:20.880
probably still hate each other,

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:22.920
even though one of them
is dead for decades,

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:24.930
because they would argue it
was thalamic reticular nucleus,

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:27.210
the other was reticular thalamic nucleus.

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:28.620
Anyway, these are scientists,

00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:31.050
they're people, they tend to debate.

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:32.580
but the thalamic reticular nucleus,

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:34.380
as I'm going to call it,

00:36:34.380 --> 00:36:36.330
serves as a sort of gate

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:38.790
as to which information
is allowed to pass through

00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:41.850
up to your conscious
experience, and which is not.

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:44.190
And that gating mechanism
is strongly regulated

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:45.930
by the chemical GABA.

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:50.460
GABA is a neurotransmitter
that is inhibitory, as we say,

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:53.730
it serves to shut down
or suppress the activity

00:36:53.730 --> 00:36:55.020
of other neurons.

00:36:55.020 --> 00:36:58.650
So the thalamic reticular
nucleus is really saying,

00:36:58.650 --> 00:37:02.190
no, touch information
cannot come in right now.

00:37:02.190 --> 00:37:04.860
You should not be
thinking about the contact

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:06.960
of the back of your legs with the chair

00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:07.890
that you're sitting on, Andrew,

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:09.540
you should be thinking about
what you're trying to say

00:37:09.540 --> 00:37:11.220
and what you're hearing
and how your voice sounds

00:37:11.220 --> 00:37:13.920
and what you see in
front of you, et cetera.

00:37:13.920 --> 00:37:17.310
Whereas if I'm about to get
an injection from a doctor

00:37:17.310 --> 00:37:19.560
or I'm in pain, or I'm in pleasure,

00:37:19.560 --> 00:37:22.230
I'm going to think about
my somatic sensation

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:23.490
at the level of touch

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:24.990
and I'm probably going to think less

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:26.760
about smells in the room,

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:28.590
although I might also think
about smells in the room

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:30.360
or what I'm seeing and what I'm hearing.

00:37:30.360 --> 00:37:33.360
We can combine all these
different sensory modalities,

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:34.950
but the thalamic reticular nucleus

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:36.990
really allows us to funnel,

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:40.080
to direct particular categories
of sensory experience

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:41.430
into our conscious awareness

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:44.823
and suppress other categories
of sensory experience.

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:48.330
In addition, the thalamic
reticular nucleus

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:52.020
plays a critical role in which thoughts

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:54.840
are allowed to pass up to
our conscious perception

00:37:54.840 --> 00:37:56.730
and which ones are not,

00:37:56.730 --> 00:37:59.400
so much so that some neuroscientists

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:01.530
and indeed some neurophilosophers,

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:02.670
if you want call them that,

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:04.780
have theorized or philosophized that

00:38:05.850 --> 00:38:07.170
the thalamic reticular nucleus

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:09.360
is actually involved in our consciousness.

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:10.710
Now, consciousness isn't a topic

00:38:10.710 --> 00:38:12.330
that I really want to
talk about this episode

00:38:12.330 --> 00:38:14.670
and it's a very kind of mushy-murky,

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:17.160
as we say in science, it's a shmooey term

00:38:17.160 --> 00:38:18.930
because it doesn't really
have clear definitions

00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:20.670
so arguments about it often get lost

00:38:20.670 --> 00:38:23.010
in the fact that people are
arguing about different things.

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:24.450
But when I say consciousness,

00:38:24.450 --> 00:38:26.280
what I mean is conscious awareness.

00:38:26.280 --> 00:38:28.800
So let's zoom out and
take a look at the circuit

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:30.450
that we've got and that we now know

00:38:30.450 --> 00:38:33.000
based on neuroimaging studies is

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:35.130
intimately involved in
generating obsessions

00:38:35.130 --> 00:38:36.750
and compulsions in OCD.

00:38:36.750 --> 00:38:39.240
We have a cortex or neocortex,

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:41.430
which is involved in
perception and understanding

00:38:41.430 --> 00:38:42.570
of what's happening.

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:44.760
We have the striatum and basal ganglia,

00:38:44.760 --> 00:38:47.460
which are involved in
generating behaviors, go,

00:38:47.460 --> 00:38:49.500
and suppressing behaviors, no-go.

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:51.900
And we have the thalamus which collects

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:54.390
all of our sensory experience in parallel,

00:38:54.390 --> 00:38:56.580
hearing, touch, smell, et cetera,

00:38:56.580 --> 00:38:58.410
not so much smell through the
thalamus, I should mention,

00:38:58.410 --> 00:39:00.840
but the other senses that is.

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:04.320
And then that thalamus is encased

00:39:04.320 --> 00:39:06.930
by the thalamic reticular
nucleus, which serves

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:09.210
as a kind of a guard
saying you can pass through

00:39:09.210 --> 00:39:11.160
and you can pass through,
but you, you, you

00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:13.290
can't pass through up to conscious

00:39:13.290 --> 00:39:15.270
understanding and perception.

00:39:15.270 --> 00:39:18.790
So that loop, this
cortico-striatal-thalamic loop,

00:39:18.790 --> 00:39:21.900
cortico-striatal-thalamic
loop is the circuit

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:24.600
thought to underlie OCD,

00:39:24.600 --> 00:39:26.400
and dysfunction in that circuit

00:39:26.400 --> 00:39:28.290
is what's thought to underlie OCD.

00:39:28.290 --> 00:39:29.940
Now, again, this circuit
exists in all of us

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:31.260
and it can operate in healthy ways,

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:33.450
or it can operate in ways
that make us feel unhealthy

00:39:33.450 --> 00:39:36.510
or even suffer from full blown OCD.

00:39:36.510 --> 00:39:39.660
How do we know that this
circuit is involved in OCD?

00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:42.810
Well there, we can look to
some really interesting studies

00:39:42.810 --> 00:39:46.710
that involve bringing human
subjects into the laboratory

00:39:46.710 --> 00:39:49.020
and generating their
obsessions and compulsions

00:39:49.020 --> 00:39:50.610
and then imaging their brain using

00:39:50.610 --> 00:39:53.700
any variety of techniques
that we talked about before.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:55.230
What would such an experiment look like?

00:39:55.230 --> 00:39:57.630
Well, in order to do
that sort of experiment,

00:39:57.630 --> 00:40:00.720
first of all, you need people who have OCD

00:40:00.720 --> 00:40:02.910
and of course you need
control subjects that don't,

00:40:02.910 --> 00:40:05.160
and you need to be able to reliably evoke

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:06.780
the obsessions and the compulsions.

00:40:06.780 --> 00:40:10.110
Now, it turns out this is
most easily, or I should say

00:40:10.110 --> 00:40:11.850
most simply done, 'cause it can't be easy

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:12.900
for the people with OCD,

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:15.240
but this is most straightforward,

00:40:15.240 --> 00:40:16.320
that's the word I was looking for,

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:19.680
most straightforward when
looking at the category

00:40:19.680 --> 00:40:22.230
of obsessions and compulsions that relate

00:40:22.230 --> 00:40:25.110
to order and cleanliness.

00:40:25.110 --> 00:40:27.570
So what they do typically
is bring subjects

00:40:27.570 --> 00:40:30.450
into the laboratory who have a obsession

00:40:30.450 --> 00:40:32.580
about germs and contamination

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:34.920
and a compulsion to hand wash,

00:40:34.920 --> 00:40:37.680
and they give these
people, believe it or not,

00:40:37.680 --> 00:40:41.010
a sweaty towel that contains the sweat

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:43.030
and the odor and the

00:40:43.920 --> 00:40:46.500
liquid, basically, from
somebody else's hands.

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:48.090
In fact, they'll sometimes have someone

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:50.850
wipe their own sweat off
the back of their neck

00:40:50.850 --> 00:40:52.740
and put it on the towel
and then they'll put it

00:40:52.740 --> 00:40:54.660
in front of the person,
which as you can imagine

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:57.990
for someone with OCD is
incredibly anxiety-provoking

00:40:57.990 --> 00:41:01.353
and almost always evokes
these obsessions about,

00:41:01.353 --> 00:41:04.410
ugh, this is really, this is really bad.

00:41:04.410 --> 00:41:06.591
This is really bad, I need to clean,

00:41:06.591 --> 00:41:07.424
I need to clean. I need to clean.

00:41:07.424 --> 00:41:08.670
Now they're doing all this while someone

00:41:08.670 --> 00:41:10.560
is in a brain scanner or

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:11.400
while they're being imaged

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:13.350
for positron emission tomography.

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:14.310
And then they can also look at

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:17.280
the patterns of activation in the brain

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:19.020
while the person is doing hand washing.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:20.580
Although sometimes the apparati

00:41:20.580 --> 00:41:21.840
associated with these imaging studies

00:41:21.840 --> 00:41:23.340
make it hard to do a lot of movement,

00:41:23.340 --> 00:41:25.350
they can do these sorts of studies.

00:41:25.350 --> 00:41:27.090
They have done these sorts of studies

00:41:27.090 --> 00:41:30.270
in many subjects using
different variations

00:41:30.270 --> 00:41:31.380
of what I just described.

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:34.255
And low and behold what lights up?

00:41:34.255 --> 00:41:35.250
And when I say lights up,

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:37.740
what sorts of brain regions

00:41:37.740 --> 00:41:39.690
are more metabolically
active, more blood flow,

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:40.530
more neural activity?

00:41:40.530 --> 00:41:44.850
Well, it's this particular
cortico-striatal-thalamic loop.

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:46.260
In addition to that,

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:47.760
some of the drug treatments

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:49.260
that are effective in some,

00:41:49.260 --> 00:41:51.480
and I want to emphasize some individuals,

00:41:51.480 --> 00:41:54.360
at suppressing obsessions
and or compulsions

00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:57.350
such as the selective
serotonin reuptake inhibitors

00:41:57.350 --> 00:41:59.800
or SSRIs, which we'll talk
about in a little bit,

00:42:01.230 --> 00:42:02.970
when people take those drugs,

00:42:02.970 --> 00:42:05.880
they see not just a suppression of

00:42:05.880 --> 00:42:08.460
the obsession and compulsion,

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:12.330
but also a suppression of these
particular neural circuits.

00:42:12.330 --> 00:42:13.620
They become less active.

00:42:13.620 --> 00:42:16.200
Now I want to emphasize
and telegraph a little bit

00:42:16.200 --> 00:42:17.350
of what's coming later,

00:42:18.390 --> 00:42:22.020
these drugs like SSRIs do not
work for everybody with OCD.

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:23.070
And as many of you know,

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:25.530
they carry other certain
problems and side effects

00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:28.110
for many but not all individuals.

00:42:28.110 --> 00:42:29.970
But nonetheless, what we have now

00:42:29.970 --> 00:42:31.920
is an observation that this circuit,

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:33.720
the cortico-striatal-thalamic loop,

00:42:33.720 --> 00:42:35.070
is active in OCD.

00:42:35.070 --> 00:42:38.130
We have a manipulation that
when people take a drug

00:42:38.130 --> 00:42:39.540
that at least in those individuals

00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:41.640
is effective in suppressing or eliminating

00:42:41.640 --> 00:42:42.840
the obsessions and compulsions,

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:44.880
there's less activity in this loop.

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:47.610
And thanks to some very
good animal model studies,

00:42:47.610 --> 00:42:49.200
that at least at this point in time,

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:51.090
you really couldn't do in humans,

00:42:51.090 --> 00:42:53.100
although soon that may change,

00:42:53.100 --> 00:42:55.140
we now know in a causal way

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:59.850
that the equivalent circuitry
exists in other animals,

00:42:59.850 --> 00:43:02.190
such as mice, such as
cats, such as monkeys,

00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:05.880
and that activation of those particular

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:08.130
cortico-striatal-thalamic circuits

00:43:08.130 --> 00:43:11.820
in animal models can indeed evoke OCD

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:14.640
in an individual that prior to that

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:16.290
did not have OCD.

00:43:16.290 --> 00:43:18.270
So I'm just going to
briefly describe one study.

00:43:18.270 --> 00:43:20.640
This is a now classic study published

00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:21.690
in the journal Science,

00:43:21.690 --> 00:43:24.810
one of the three apex journals in 2013.

00:43:24.810 --> 00:43:26.670
The first author on this paper

00:43:26.670 --> 00:43:30.180
is Susanne Ahmari, A-H-M-A-R-I.

00:43:30.180 --> 00:43:32.250
I will provide a link to
this in the show notes.

00:43:32.250 --> 00:43:34.350
It's a truly landmark paper done

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:37.440
in Rene Hen's lab at Columbia University.

00:43:37.440 --> 00:43:38.460
And the title of the paper

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:41.430
is repeated cortico-striatal
stimulation generates,

00:43:41.430 --> 00:43:42.270
that's the key word here,

00:43:42.270 --> 00:43:45.330
generates persistent OCD-like behavior.

00:43:45.330 --> 00:43:47.070
What they did is they took mice,

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:49.200
mice do mouse things.

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:50.670
They move around, they play with toys,

00:43:50.670 --> 00:43:52.590
they eat, they pee, they mate,

00:43:52.590 --> 00:43:54.360
they do various things in their cage,

00:43:54.360 --> 00:43:55.863
but they also groom.

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:59.460
Humans groom, animals with fur groom,

00:43:59.460 --> 00:44:00.870
Well, you hope most people groom,

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:03.330
some people over-groom,
some people under-groom,

00:44:03.330 --> 00:44:04.163
but most people groom.

00:44:04.163 --> 00:44:05.910
They'll comb their hair,
they'll clean, et cetera.

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:08.820
Those are normal behaviors
that humans engage in.

00:44:08.820 --> 00:44:09.900
I'm not aware that mice comb their hair,

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:11.130
but mice adjust their hair.

00:44:11.130 --> 00:44:12.117
So they'll kind of pet their hair

00:44:12.117 --> 00:44:12.950
and they'll do this.

00:44:12.950 --> 00:44:14.670
They'll sometimes even
do it to each other.

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:15.720
We used to have mice in the lab,

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:16.980
now we only do human studies,

00:44:16.980 --> 00:44:19.860
but the mice will groom themselves,

00:44:19.860 --> 00:44:22.380
and typical, what we call wild type mice,

00:44:22.380 --> 00:44:23.250
not because they're wild,

00:44:23.250 --> 00:44:24.630
but because they're typical,

00:44:24.630 --> 00:44:27.690
will groom themselves at
a particular frequency,

00:44:27.690 --> 00:44:30.060
but not to the point where
their hair is falling out.

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:33.150
Not constantly, they are
grooming some of the time

00:44:33.150 --> 00:44:36.720
and they're doing other mouse
things other mouse times.

00:44:36.720 --> 00:44:38.100
So in this particular study,

00:44:38.100 --> 00:44:41.040
what they did is they
used some technology,

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:42.660
which it actually was discussed

00:44:42.660 --> 00:44:45.000
on a previous episode of
The Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:47.400
this is technology that was
developed by a psychiatrist

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:49.320
and bioengineer by the
name of Karl Deisseroth,

00:44:49.320 --> 00:44:52.320
one of my colleagues at
Stanford School of Medicine.

00:44:52.320 --> 00:44:54.270
This is technology that allows researchers

00:44:54.270 --> 00:44:56.880
to use the presentation of light

00:44:56.880 --> 00:45:00.780
to control neural activity
in particular brain areas

00:45:00.780 --> 00:45:03.210
in a very high fidelity way.

00:45:03.210 --> 00:45:05.480
You control the activity in
the cortex of the striatum

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:07.830
or the thalamus when you
want and how you want.

00:45:07.830 --> 00:45:09.660
It's really a beautiful technology.

00:45:09.660 --> 00:45:11.900
In any event, what they
did in this study is,

00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:13.680
or I should say what Susan Ahmari

00:45:13.680 --> 00:45:15.240
and colleagues did in this study

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:20.240
was to stimulate the
cortico-striatal circuitry

00:45:20.280 --> 00:45:22.950
in animals that did not
have any OCD-like behavior.

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:24.210
And when they did that,

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:26.460
those animals started grooming incessantly

00:45:26.460 --> 00:45:29.430
to the point where their hair
was falling out or they even,

00:45:29.430 --> 00:45:31.350
they didn't take the experiments
this far, fortunately,

00:45:31.350 --> 00:45:32.880
but the animals would have a tendency

00:45:32.880 --> 00:45:34.590
to almost rub themselves raw

00:45:34.590 --> 00:45:37.050
in the same way that
somebody who has a compulsion

00:45:37.050 --> 00:45:40.260
to hand wash would, sadly,
people will hand wash

00:45:40.260 --> 00:45:41.093
to the point where their hands

00:45:41.093 --> 00:45:42.510
are actually bleeding and raw.

00:45:42.510 --> 00:45:43.590
It's really that bad.

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:45.600
I know that's tough imagery to imagine,

00:45:45.600 --> 00:45:47.490
and you can't even imagine
why someone would self harm

00:45:47.490 --> 00:45:51.090
in that way, but again,
that's that incredible anxiety

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:53.070
relationship between the
compulsion, excuse me,

00:45:53.070 --> 00:45:54.930
the obsession and the compulsion,

00:45:54.930 --> 00:45:57.600
and the fact that
engaging in the compulsion

00:45:57.600 --> 00:45:59.190
simply strengthens the obsession

00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:00.600
and therefore the anxiety.

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:04.590
So that collection of studies, of data,

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:06.810
FMRI, PET scanning in humans,

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:11.370
the treatment with SSRIs,
and these experiments where

00:46:11.370 --> 00:46:14.460
researchers have actively triggered

00:46:14.460 --> 00:46:17.250
these particular circuits in animal models

00:46:17.250 --> 00:46:18.780
that previously did not have

00:46:18.780 --> 00:46:20.070
too much activity in these circuits

00:46:20.070 --> 00:46:23.370
and then they observe OCD emerging

00:46:23.370 --> 00:46:25.140
really points squarely to the fact

00:46:25.140 --> 00:46:27.480
that the cortico-striatal-thalamic loop

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:30.360
is likely to be the basis of OCD.

00:46:30.360 --> 00:46:32.940
Now, of course, other circuits
could also be involved,

00:46:32.940 --> 00:46:35.490
but the cortico-striatal-thalamic circuit

00:46:35.490 --> 00:46:39.360
seems to be the main circuit
generating OCD-like behavior.

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:40.740
That's a lot of mechanism.

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:42.600
Hopefully it was described in a way

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:44.460
that you can digest and understand.

00:46:44.460 --> 00:46:46.290
And some of you might be
thinking, well, so what?

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:47.250
Why does that help me?

00:46:47.250 --> 00:46:48.780
I mean, I can't reach into my brain

00:46:48.780 --> 00:46:50.100
and turn off my cortex.

00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:52.650
I can't reach into my brain
and turn off my thalamus.

00:46:52.650 --> 00:46:55.500
And indeed, on the one hand, that's true.

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:57.480
But as you'll next learn

00:46:57.480 --> 00:46:59.790
when thinking about the
various behavioral treatments

00:46:59.790 --> 00:47:01.200
and drug treatments

00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:03.570
and holistic treatments for OCD,

00:47:03.570 --> 00:47:05.490
what you'll notice is that each one taps

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:06.840
into a different component

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:09.780
of this cortico-striatal-thalamic loop.

00:47:09.780 --> 00:47:12.690
And by understanding
that, you can start to see

00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:15.270
why certain treatments
might work at one stage

00:47:15.270 --> 00:47:17.310
of the illness versus others.

00:47:17.310 --> 00:47:19.320
You will also start to understand why

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:22.380
obsessive-compulsive personality disorder

00:47:22.380 --> 00:47:24.750
does not have the same
sorts of engagements

00:47:24.750 --> 00:47:25.830
of these neural loops,

00:47:25.830 --> 00:47:30.300
and yet relies on other
aspects of brain and body

00:47:30.300 --> 00:47:33.750
and therefore responds best
to other sorts of treatments.

00:47:33.750 --> 00:47:36.930
Or in some cases, people
with obsessive-compulsive

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:38.970
personality disorder are
not even seeking treatment

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:40.350
as I alluded to before.

00:47:40.350 --> 00:47:41.790
The point here is that by understanding

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:43.230
the underlying mechanism

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:45.990
why certain drugs and
behavioral treatments work

00:47:45.990 --> 00:47:48.150
and don't work will become
immediately apparent

00:47:48.150 --> 00:47:51.420
and in thinking about
that, in knowing that,

00:47:51.420 --> 00:47:53.460
you'll be able to make excellent choices,

00:47:53.460 --> 00:47:56.220
I believe, in terms of what
sorts of treatments you pursue,

00:47:56.220 --> 00:47:57.900
what sorts of treatments you abandon,

00:47:57.900 --> 00:48:00.750
and most importantly, the order,

00:48:00.750 --> 00:48:04.020
the sequence that you pursue
and apply those treatments.

00:48:04.020 --> 00:48:06.390
Before we go any further,
I'd like to give people

00:48:06.390 --> 00:48:07.890
a little bit of a window into

00:48:07.890 --> 00:48:10.890
what a diagnosis for OCD would look like.

00:48:10.890 --> 00:48:13.110
Give you a sense of the sorts of questions

00:48:13.110 --> 00:48:15.600
that a clinician would ask to determine

00:48:15.600 --> 00:48:18.120
whether or not somebody has OCD or not.

00:48:18.120 --> 00:48:19.500
Now, I want to be clear,
I'm not going to do this

00:48:19.500 --> 00:48:20.670
in an exhaustive way.

00:48:20.670 --> 00:48:22.590
I wouldn't want anyone to self-diagnose.

00:48:22.590 --> 00:48:24.690
Although I'm hoping that
by sharing some of this,

00:48:24.690 --> 00:48:26.040
that some of you might get insight

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:27.960
into whether or not you do have obsessions

00:48:27.960 --> 00:48:30.900
and compulsions that
might qualify for OCD,

00:48:30.900 --> 00:48:33.300
and perhaps even to seek out help.

00:48:33.300 --> 00:48:37.860
The most commonly used test of OCD,

00:48:37.860 --> 00:48:38.910
or for OCD, I should say,

00:48:38.910 --> 00:48:42.000
is called the Yale-Brown
Obsessive Compulsive Scale.

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:44.580
And this is, scientists love acronyms

00:48:44.580 --> 00:48:46.857
as do the military, and it's the Y-BOCS,

00:48:46.857 --> 00:48:51.360
the Y-B-O-C-S, the Y-BOCS.

00:48:51.360 --> 00:48:53.700
So typically someone
will go into the clinic

00:48:53.700 --> 00:48:55.770
either because a family
member encouraged them to

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:57.660
or because they feel
that they're suffering

00:48:57.660 --> 00:48:59.490
from obsessions and compulsions,

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:02.460
and before the clinician would proceed

00:49:02.460 --> 00:49:04.020
with any kind of direct questions,

00:49:04.020 --> 00:49:05.550
they would very clearly define

00:49:05.550 --> 00:49:06.930
what obsessions and compulsions are.

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:09.210
And here I'm actually
reading from the Y-BOCS.

00:49:09.210 --> 00:49:11.700
So quote, "obsessions are
unwelcome and distressing ideas,

00:49:11.700 --> 00:49:13.110
thoughts, images or impulses

00:49:13.110 --> 00:49:14.460
that repeatedly enter your mind.

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:16.590
They may seem to occur against your will.

00:49:16.590 --> 00:49:17.880
They may be repugnant to you,

00:49:17.880 --> 00:49:19.530
you may recognize them as senseless

00:49:19.530 --> 00:49:22.380
and they may not fit your personality."

00:49:22.380 --> 00:49:23.790
Then there are compulsions.

00:49:23.790 --> 00:49:25.560
Quote, "Compulsions, on the other hand,

00:49:25.560 --> 00:49:27.720
are behaviors or acts that
you feel driven to perform

00:49:27.720 --> 00:49:31.020
although you may recognize
them as senseless or excessive.

00:49:31.020 --> 00:49:32.910
At times, you may try to resist doing them

00:49:32.910 --> 00:49:34.260
but this may prove difficult.

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:36.720
You may experience anxiety
that does not diminish

00:49:36.720 --> 00:49:38.430
until the behavior is completed."

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:40.680
And as I mentioned before in many cases,

00:49:40.680 --> 00:49:42.330
immediately after the
behavior has completed,

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:45.630
the anxiety doesn't just return,
it indeed can strengthen.

00:49:45.630 --> 00:49:46.890
Now, there are a tremendous
number of questions

00:49:46.890 --> 00:49:47.723
on the Y-BOCS.

00:49:47.723 --> 00:49:49.680
So I'm just going to highlight

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:51.280
a few of the general categories.

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:54.780
Typically, the person
will fill out a checklist,

00:49:54.780 --> 00:49:57.600
so they will designate whether or not

00:49:57.600 --> 00:49:59.790
currently or in the past they have,

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:02.190
for instance, aggressive obsessions,

00:50:02.190 --> 00:50:04.200
fear that one might harm themselves,

00:50:04.200 --> 00:50:05.910
fear that one might harm others,

00:50:05.910 --> 00:50:07.080
fear that they'll steal things,

00:50:07.080 --> 00:50:09.930
fear that they will act
on unwanted impulses,

00:50:09.930 --> 00:50:12.060
currently or in the past or both,

00:50:12.060 --> 00:50:13.110
that's one category.

00:50:13.110 --> 00:50:14.880
The other one are
contamination obsessions.

00:50:14.880 --> 00:50:16.530
So concern with dirt or germs,

00:50:16.530 --> 00:50:18.540
bothered by sticky substances or residues,

00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:19.590
et cetera, et cetera.

00:50:19.590 --> 00:50:21.510
So there are a bunch
of different categories

00:50:21.510 --> 00:50:24.330
that include, for instance,
sexual obsessions,

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:26.490
what are called saving obsessions,

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:28.470
even moral obsessions,

00:50:28.470 --> 00:50:30.870
excess concern with right
or wrong or morality,

00:50:30.870 --> 00:50:33.930
concerned with sacrilege and blasphemy,

00:50:33.930 --> 00:50:35.820
obsession with need for
symmetry and exactness.

00:50:35.820 --> 00:50:37.740
Again, all of these
questions being answered

00:50:37.740 --> 00:50:39.450
as either present in the past

00:50:39.450 --> 00:50:41.010
or not present in the past,

00:50:41.010 --> 00:50:43.170
present currently or
not present currently.

00:50:43.170 --> 00:50:46.290
And then the test generally

00:50:46.290 --> 00:50:49.860
transitions over to questions
about target symptoms.

00:50:49.860 --> 00:50:52.050
They really try and get people to identify

00:50:52.050 --> 00:50:54.630
if they have obsessions, what
are their exact obsessions?

00:50:54.630 --> 00:50:56.190
Now, this turns out to be really important

00:50:56.190 --> 00:50:57.320
because as we talk about some

00:50:57.320 --> 00:50:59.880
of the therapies that really work,

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:01.260
I'll just give away a little bit

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:04.080
of why they work best in certain cases

00:51:04.080 --> 00:51:06.810
and why they don't work
as well in other cases,

00:51:06.810 --> 00:51:09.390
it turns out that it
becomes very important

00:51:09.390 --> 00:51:11.340
for the clinician and the patient

00:51:11.340 --> 00:51:13.647
to not just identify the obsessions

00:51:13.647 --> 00:51:15.243
and the compulsions generally

00:51:15.243 --> 00:51:18.030
in a kind of a generic or top contour way,

00:51:18.030 --> 00:51:21.240
but to really encourage
or even force the patient

00:51:21.240 --> 00:51:25.110
to define very precisely what the biggest,

00:51:25.110 --> 00:51:27.510
most catastrophic fear is,

00:51:27.510 --> 00:51:29.760
what the obsession really relates to.

00:51:29.760 --> 00:51:31.410
That turns out to be very important

00:51:31.410 --> 00:51:34.470
in disrupting this
cortico-striatal-thalamic loop

00:51:34.470 --> 00:51:36.360
and getting relief from symptoms

00:51:36.360 --> 00:51:37.650
one way or the other.

00:51:37.650 --> 00:51:41.130
So the Yale-Brown Obsessive
Compulsive Scale, this Y-BOCS,

00:51:41.130 --> 00:51:42.420
again, is very extensive,

00:51:42.420 --> 00:51:45.690
it goes on for dozens of pages actually,

00:51:45.690 --> 00:51:48.000
and has all these different categories,

00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:50.790
not so much designed to just pinpoint

00:51:50.790 --> 00:51:52.290
what people obsess about

00:51:52.290 --> 00:51:54.810
or what they feel compelled to do,

00:51:54.810 --> 00:51:56.910
but to also try and identify

00:51:56.910 --> 00:51:59.970
what is the fear that's driving all this.

00:51:59.970 --> 00:52:02.160
In the way that we've
set this up thus far,

00:52:02.160 --> 00:52:04.020
we've been talking about
obsessions and compulsions

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:05.910
is kind of existing in a vacuum.

00:52:05.910 --> 00:52:07.260
You're obsessed about germs

00:52:07.260 --> 00:52:09.180
and you're compelled to wash your hands,

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:10.830
obsessed about germs,
compelled to wash your hands.

00:52:10.830 --> 00:52:12.240
Or obsessed about symmetry,

00:52:12.240 --> 00:52:14.220
compelled to put right
angles on everything.

00:52:14.220 --> 00:52:15.510
Or obsessed about counting

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:17.370
and therefore counting, et cetera.

00:52:17.370 --> 00:52:19.650
But beneath that

00:52:19.650 --> 00:52:23.280
is a cognitive component
that is not at all apparent

00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:26.310
from someone describing their obsession

00:52:26.310 --> 00:52:29.550
and from someone describing or
displaying their compulsion.

00:52:29.550 --> 00:52:33.240
The deeper layer to all
that is what is the fear,

00:52:33.240 --> 00:52:37.800
exactly, if one were to
not perform the compulsion,

00:52:37.800 --> 00:52:41.100
meaning what is the fear
that's driving the obsession?

00:52:41.100 --> 00:52:44.340
So that brings us to a
very powerful category

00:52:44.340 --> 00:52:47.730
of treatments that I
should say does not work

00:52:47.730 --> 00:52:49.440
in everybody with OCD

00:52:49.440 --> 00:52:52.020
but works in many people with OCD

00:52:52.020 --> 00:52:56.310
and really speaks to the
underlying neural circuitry

00:52:56.310 --> 00:52:58.710
that generates OCD and
how to interrupt it.

00:52:58.710 --> 00:52:59.880
And that is the treatment

00:52:59.880 --> 00:53:02.220
of cognitive behavioral therapy

00:53:02.220 --> 00:53:03.060
and in particular,

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:06.630
exposure-based cognitive
behavioral therapy.

00:53:06.630 --> 00:53:08.460
So we're going to talk about
cognitive behavioral therapy

00:53:08.460 --> 00:53:10.050
and exposure therapy now,

00:53:10.050 --> 00:53:11.850
but right at the outset,

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:13.230
I want to distinguish the kinds

00:53:13.230 --> 00:53:14.400
of cognitive behavioral therapy

00:53:14.400 --> 00:53:15.810
and exposure therapies that are done

00:53:15.810 --> 00:53:17.187
for obsessive-compulsive disorder,

00:53:17.187 --> 00:53:19.050
for the sorts of cognitive
behavioral therapies

00:53:19.050 --> 00:53:20.910
that are done for other types

00:53:20.910 --> 00:53:22.990
of mental challenges and disorders

00:53:24.300 --> 00:53:27.510
because cognitive
behavioral therapy for OCD

00:53:27.510 --> 00:53:28.920
really has everything to do

00:53:28.920 --> 00:53:31.983
with identifying the utmost fear.

00:53:32.910 --> 00:53:34.110
In some sense, we can think of fears

00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:35.880
as kind of along a hierarchy.

00:53:35.880 --> 00:53:37.950
An the example earlier of somebody

00:53:37.950 --> 00:53:39.840
being afraid to turn left and therefore

00:53:39.840 --> 00:53:41.850
feeling compelled to turn right,

00:53:41.850 --> 00:53:43.170
you would want to take that person

00:53:43.170 --> 00:53:46.500
and really understand
what do they fear most

00:53:46.500 --> 00:53:47.460
about turning left?

00:53:47.460 --> 00:53:49.020
Now they might not be aware of it.

00:53:49.020 --> 00:53:51.390
They might not be conscious
to what that really is,

00:53:51.390 --> 00:53:53.700
but if you were to probe
them in a clinical setting,

00:53:53.700 --> 00:53:55.110
you would eventually get to an answer.

00:53:55.110 --> 00:53:57.660
That answer could be at first,

00:53:57.660 --> 00:53:59.310
I don't know, just, it's just bad.

00:53:59.310 --> 00:54:01.260
I don't know why it's
bad, it makes no sense,

00:54:01.260 --> 00:54:02.280
but it's just bad.

00:54:02.280 --> 00:54:03.420
I do not want to go left.

00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:04.830
I don't know why, I don't know why.

00:54:04.830 --> 00:54:07.080
But if you were to push
that person a little bit

00:54:07.080 --> 00:54:10.320
in a respectful and kind and caring way

00:54:10.320 --> 00:54:11.160
aimed at their treatment,

00:54:11.160 --> 00:54:11.993
if you were to push 'em and say,

00:54:11.993 --> 00:54:13.650
well, what do you mean by bad?

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:16.200
If you turn left, you
think the world would end?

00:54:16.200 --> 00:54:17.850
They might say, no, the
world's not going to end,

00:54:17.850 --> 00:54:20.970
but you know, someone is
going to die suddenly.

00:54:20.970 --> 00:54:24.120
I know that sounds crazy, but
somebody's going to die suddenly.

00:54:24.120 --> 00:54:25.740
This almost sounds like superstition,

00:54:25.740 --> 00:54:27.030
we'll talk about superstitions later,

00:54:27.030 --> 00:54:29.370
but indeed it is somewhat superstitious.

00:54:29.370 --> 00:54:32.070
So for instance, you would
say, who's going to die?

00:54:32.070 --> 00:54:33.660
And they'd say, I don't know.

00:54:33.660 --> 00:54:35.850
And you'd say, no, really
who's going to die?

00:54:35.850 --> 00:54:37.440
If you think about this,
are you going to die?

00:54:37.440 --> 00:54:38.460
Is so and so going to die?

00:54:38.460 --> 00:54:40.560
And very often,

00:54:40.560 --> 00:54:43.050
very often what you find is that

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:44.440
people will start to

00:54:45.450 --> 00:54:48.630
reveal the underlying obsession

00:54:48.630 --> 00:54:51.630
at a level of detail that
both to the clinician

00:54:51.630 --> 00:54:53.370
and to them can be somewhat astonishing

00:54:53.370 --> 00:54:55.170
even though they've been
living with that detail

00:54:55.170 --> 00:54:57.240
in their mind for a very long time.

00:54:57.240 --> 00:54:59.190
Now, how could somebody
start to reveal detail

00:54:59.190 --> 00:55:00.810
about something that's
existed in their mind

00:55:00.810 --> 00:55:04.230
for a very long time, but
not known about it, right?

00:55:04.230 --> 00:55:05.460
Not been aware of it.

00:55:05.460 --> 00:55:06.360
Now, some of you might think,

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:08.010
oh, it's repressed or something.

00:55:08.010 --> 00:55:10.350
That's not at all what's happening.

00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:12.630
If you think about the
architecture of OCD,

00:55:12.630 --> 00:55:14.460
typically, people will have an obsession

00:55:14.460 --> 00:55:16.140
and then they'll engage in the compulsion

00:55:16.140 --> 00:55:18.660
as quickly as they can to
relieve that obsession.

00:55:18.660 --> 00:55:20.400
So in many ways, the disease itself

00:55:20.400 --> 00:55:23.430
prevents people from ever
getting to the bottom

00:55:23.430 --> 00:55:25.500
of that trough, ever getting to the point

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:27.990
where they really clearly
articulate to themselves

00:55:27.990 --> 00:55:30.570
exactly what it is that they fear.

00:55:30.570 --> 00:55:33.450
But it becomes so essential to articulate

00:55:33.450 --> 00:55:35.910
exactly what it is that they fear

00:55:35.910 --> 00:55:37.830
for a somewhat counterintuitive reason.

00:55:37.830 --> 00:55:40.080
You might think, oh,
the moment they realize

00:55:40.080 --> 00:55:42.510
exactly what they fear, everything lifts,

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:44.400
the circuit turns off
and they just feel better

00:55:44.400 --> 00:55:45.870
because they realized it.

00:55:45.870 --> 00:55:47.100
I wish I could tell you that's the case,

00:55:47.100 --> 00:55:48.840
but it turns out it's the opposite.

00:55:48.840 --> 00:55:51.180
What the clinician is
actually trying to do

00:55:51.180 --> 00:55:55.200
is get people to feel
more anxiety, not less.

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:56.340
What they're trying to get them to do

00:55:56.340 --> 00:55:59.400
is to short circuit, no pun intended,

00:55:59.400 --> 00:56:01.650
to intervene in their own neural circuit,

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:06.000
I should say, with that relief of anxiety,

00:56:06.000 --> 00:56:08.670
however brief, brought on by engaging

00:56:08.670 --> 00:56:10.740
in the compulsion
related to the obsession.

00:56:10.740 --> 00:56:13.800
So, whereas typically someone would feel

00:56:13.800 --> 00:56:15.797
the obsession with, ugh,
I don't want to turn left

00:56:15.797 --> 00:56:17.340
'cause something bad's going to happen,

00:56:17.340 --> 00:56:19.740
someone's going to die,
and then they turn right,

00:56:19.740 --> 00:56:22.680
they never get the
option or the opportunity

00:56:22.680 --> 00:56:25.560
to really explore what would happen

00:56:25.560 --> 00:56:28.560
were they to turn left or to
not be able to turn right.

00:56:28.560 --> 00:56:31.020
By forcing them down the path of inquiry,

00:56:31.020 --> 00:56:33.990
that leads them to the place
where they very clearly

00:56:33.990 --> 00:56:37.200
identify the fear, the anxiety,

00:56:37.200 --> 00:56:39.540
it raises the anxiety in them,

00:56:39.540 --> 00:56:43.710
and that's actually what
the clinician is after.

00:56:43.710 --> 00:56:47.100
Cognitive behavioral
therapy and exposure therapy

00:56:47.100 --> 00:56:50.100
in the context of OCD, most often involves

00:56:50.100 --> 00:56:52.710
trying to get people to tolerate,

00:56:52.710 --> 00:56:54.930
not relieve their anxiety.

00:56:54.930 --> 00:56:56.340
This is extremely important.

00:56:56.340 --> 00:56:58.620
And I realize there's variation to this

00:56:58.620 --> 00:57:01.620
depending on the style of
cognitive behavioral therapy,

00:57:01.620 --> 00:57:05.520
the style of exposure therapy,
but almost across the board,

00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:09.540
The goal, again, is to get
people to feel the anxiety

00:57:09.540 --> 00:57:12.870
that normally they are able
to at least partially relieve,

00:57:12.870 --> 00:57:15.870
however briefly, by
engaging in the compulsion.

00:57:15.870 --> 00:57:18.180
So if we think back to that circuit

00:57:18.180 --> 00:57:21.150
of cortico-striatal-thalamic,
what's going on here?

00:57:21.150 --> 00:57:23.460
Where is CBT intervening?

00:57:23.460 --> 00:57:26.370
What part of the circuit
is getting interrupted?

00:57:26.370 --> 00:57:27.203
Well, as you recall,

00:57:27.203 --> 00:57:29.400
the cortex is involved
in conscious perception.

00:57:29.400 --> 00:57:31.620
The thalamus and that
thalamic reticular nucleus

00:57:31.620 --> 00:57:34.860
are involved in the
passage of certain types

00:57:34.860 --> 00:57:38.100
of experience up to our
conscious perception, not others.

00:57:38.100 --> 00:57:39.210
And the striatum is involved

00:57:39.210 --> 00:57:42.450
in this go, no-go type behavior.

00:57:42.450 --> 00:57:47.250
When OCD is really expressing
itself in its fullness,

00:57:47.250 --> 00:57:50.160
people feel an anxiety
around a particular thought

00:57:50.160 --> 00:57:53.220
and they either have a go,

00:57:53.220 --> 00:57:54.660
for instance, wash hands,

00:57:54.660 --> 00:57:58.533
or a no-go, do not turn
left type reaction.

00:57:59.790 --> 00:58:01.650
By having people progressively,

00:58:01.650 --> 00:58:03.600
in a kind of hierarchical way,

00:58:03.600 --> 00:58:06.600
reveal their precise source of anxiety,

00:58:06.600 --> 00:58:08.793
their utmost fear in this context,

00:58:09.630 --> 00:58:12.090
what happens is they feel enormous amounts

00:58:12.090 --> 00:58:13.830
of autonomic arousal.

00:58:13.830 --> 00:58:16.410
Now in the context of anxiety treatment

00:58:16.410 --> 00:58:17.700
or other types of treatments,

00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:19.770
the goal would be to
teach people to dampen,

00:58:19.770 --> 00:58:22.380
to lessen their anxiety
through breathing techniques

00:58:22.380 --> 00:58:24.780
or through visualization techniques

00:58:24.780 --> 00:58:27.240
or through self-talk or
through social support,

00:58:27.240 --> 00:58:28.950
any of the number of
things that are well-known

00:58:28.950 --> 00:58:31.440
to help people self
regulate their own anxiety.

00:58:31.440 --> 00:58:32.880
Here, it's the opposite.

00:58:32.880 --> 00:58:35.100
What they're trying to
get the patient to do

00:58:35.100 --> 00:58:38.310
is to really feel the
anxiety at its maximum,

00:58:38.310 --> 00:58:40.140
but then do the exact opposite

00:58:40.140 --> 00:58:41.820
of whatever the normal compulsion is.

00:58:41.820 --> 00:58:44.310
So if normally the compulsion
is to wash one's hands,

00:58:44.310 --> 00:58:46.680
then the idea is to suppress hand washing

00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:49.830
while being in the experience
of the utmost anxiety.

00:58:49.830 --> 00:58:52.740
Or in the case of not turning left,

00:58:52.740 --> 00:58:55.290
the person is expected to

00:58:55.290 --> 00:58:59.253
or would hopefully be able
to actually turn left,

00:59:01.407 --> 00:59:04.050
and as you can imagine, that
would evoke tremendous anxiety

00:59:04.050 --> 00:59:06.120
and yet to tolerate that anxiety.

00:59:06.120 --> 00:59:06.990
Now I want to be very clear,

00:59:06.990 --> 00:59:08.970
this is not the sort of thing
you want to do on your own.

00:59:08.970 --> 00:59:10.860
This is not the sort of thing
you want to do for a friend.

00:59:10.860 --> 00:59:14.460
This is done by trained
licensed psychologists

00:59:14.460 --> 00:59:15.960
and psychiatrists.

00:59:15.960 --> 00:59:19.230
But nonetheless, it
really points to the fact

00:59:19.230 --> 00:59:22.530
that as a anxiety-related disorder,

00:59:22.530 --> 00:59:25.650
OCD is distinct from
other types of anxiety

00:59:25.650 --> 00:59:27.630
and anxiety-related disorders,

00:59:27.630 --> 00:59:30.420
things like PTSD and
panic disorder, et cetera,

00:59:30.420 --> 00:59:32.970
because the goal again
is to bring the person

00:59:32.970 --> 00:59:36.420
right up close to the thing
that they fear the most

00:59:36.420 --> 00:59:38.640
and then to interrupt the circuit.

00:59:38.640 --> 00:59:41.460
And now you should be able to know,

00:59:41.460 --> 00:59:44.580
just intuitively, 'cause you
understand the mechanisms,

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:46.260
that the circuit you're trying to disrupt

00:59:46.260 --> 00:59:49.350
is the pattern of information flow

00:59:49.350 --> 00:59:51.000
from the thinking part of the brain,

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:52.020
the perception part of the brain,

00:59:52.020 --> 00:59:55.230
which is the cortex, to the striatum.

00:59:55.230 --> 00:59:58.560
The striatum has these
neurons which are active

00:59:58.560 --> 01:00:00.690
that essentially are, I
know it sounds a little bit

01:00:00.690 --> 01:00:02.070
like a discussion about free will,

01:00:02.070 --> 01:00:03.120
but they're trying to get some,

01:00:03.120 --> 01:00:04.950
the person to generate a certain behavior,

01:00:04.950 --> 01:00:06.810
suppress a certain behavior.

01:00:06.810 --> 01:00:08.310
And as anxiety ramps up,

01:00:08.310 --> 01:00:11.730
it's sort of a hydraulic
pressure to do that very thing

01:00:11.730 --> 01:00:12.810
that they've done for so long

01:00:12.810 --> 01:00:14.640
and they suffer from so much.

01:00:14.640 --> 01:00:16.650
We talked about hydraulic
pressure in the context

01:00:16.650 --> 01:00:18.780
of aggression in the aggression episode,

01:00:18.780 --> 01:00:19.953
this is very similar.

01:00:20.790 --> 01:00:22.290
There's a kind of a,

01:00:22.290 --> 01:00:23.490
now when I say hydraulic pressure,

01:00:23.490 --> 01:00:24.900
it's not actual hydraulic pressure,

01:00:24.900 --> 01:00:26.940
it's the confluence of a
lot of different systems.

01:00:26.940 --> 01:00:28.620
It's neurochemicals, we'll soon learn,

01:00:28.620 --> 01:00:30.510
it's hormonal, it's electrical,

01:00:30.510 --> 01:00:32.880
it's a lot of different
things operating in parallel

01:00:32.880 --> 01:00:35.460
so we can't point to one
chemical or transmitter.

01:00:35.460 --> 01:00:37.440
What's happening is the
person is feeling compelled

01:00:37.440 --> 01:00:39.660
to act, act, act to relieve the anxiety

01:00:39.660 --> 01:00:43.440
and through a progressive
type of exposure,

01:00:43.440 --> 01:00:45.030
you don't throw people in the deep end

01:00:45.030 --> 01:00:47.520
in this kind of therapy right off the bat,

01:00:47.520 --> 01:00:49.230
you gradually ratchet them toward

01:00:49.230 --> 01:00:50.910
or move them toward the discussion

01:00:50.910 --> 01:00:52.440
of exactly what they fear the most

01:00:52.440 --> 01:00:54.090
and then eventually move them toward

01:00:54.090 --> 01:00:56.250
the interruption of the compulsion

01:00:56.250 --> 01:00:58.770
as they're feeling this
extremely elevated anxiety,

01:00:58.770 --> 01:00:59.940
of course, within the context

01:00:59.940 --> 01:01:02.610
of a supportive clinical setting.

01:01:02.610 --> 01:01:05.340
But in doing that, what
you are teaching people

01:01:05.340 --> 01:01:08.550
is that the anxiety can
exist without the need

01:01:08.550 --> 01:01:10.140
to engage in the compulsion.

01:01:10.140 --> 01:01:12.990
Now some of this might
sound to people like, oh,

01:01:12.990 --> 01:01:14.280
this is a lot of kind of fancy

01:01:14.280 --> 01:01:15.870
psychological neuroscience speak

01:01:15.870 --> 01:01:17.400
around something that's kind of intuitive.

01:01:17.400 --> 01:01:20.850
But I think for most people,
this is not intuitive.

01:01:20.850 --> 01:01:22.413
And for people with OCD,

01:01:24.270 --> 01:01:25.710
there's no really other way to put it,

01:01:25.710 --> 01:01:29.490
the impulse, the
compulsion to avoid anxiety

01:01:29.490 --> 01:01:31.770
is such a powerful driving force

01:01:31.770 --> 01:01:33.060
that it should now make sense to you

01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:35.760
as to why being able to tolerate anxiety

01:01:35.760 --> 01:01:38.160
and really sit with it
and do the exact opposite

01:01:38.160 --> 01:01:39.720
of what you're normally compelled to do

01:01:39.720 --> 01:01:41.490
is going to be the path to treatment.

01:01:41.490 --> 01:01:45.030
And indeed CBT has been shown
to be enormously effective,

01:01:45.030 --> 01:01:47.880
again for a large number
of people with OCD,

01:01:47.880 --> 01:01:48.990
but not all of them.

01:01:48.990 --> 01:01:51.990
And oftentimes it requires that it also be

01:01:51.990 --> 01:01:54.240
used in concert with
certain drug treatments,

01:01:54.240 --> 01:01:56.250
which we're going to
talk about in a moment.

01:01:56.250 --> 01:01:58.620
Next, let's talk about some
of the really unique features

01:01:58.620 --> 01:02:00.810
of cognitive behavioral
therapy and exposure therapy

01:02:00.810 --> 01:02:04.350
in the context of OCD
that you often don't see

01:02:04.350 --> 01:02:08.280
in the use of CBT, that is
cognitive behavioral therapy,

01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:12.030
for other types of psychiatric
challenges and disorders.

01:02:12.030 --> 01:02:15.390
The first element is one of stair casing.

01:02:15.390 --> 01:02:17.070
And I already mentioned this before,

01:02:17.070 --> 01:02:21.000
but this gradual and progressive increase

01:02:21.000 --> 01:02:24.540
in the anxiety that you're
trying to evoke from the patient,

01:02:24.540 --> 01:02:27.000
from the person suffering from OCD.

01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:31.080
That's done in the context of
the office or the laboratory,

01:02:31.080 --> 01:02:33.873
again, by a trained
and licensed clinician.

01:02:34.740 --> 01:02:36.780
But then the person leaves, right?

01:02:36.780 --> 01:02:40.200
They leave the office,
they leave the laboratory.

01:02:40.200 --> 01:02:42.120
And a very vital component

01:02:42.120 --> 01:02:44.940
of CBT and exposure
therapy for people with OCD

01:02:44.940 --> 01:02:48.420
is that they have and perform
what's called homework,

01:02:48.420 --> 01:02:50.160
is literally what they call.

01:02:50.160 --> 01:02:51.990
This might be seen in
other sorts of treatments

01:02:51.990 --> 01:02:55.320
but for OCD, homework
is extremely important,

01:02:55.320 --> 01:02:56.850
because within the context

01:02:56.850 --> 01:02:58.740
of a laboratory experiment or the clinic,

01:02:58.740 --> 01:03:00.810
patients often feel so much support

01:03:00.810 --> 01:03:02.400
that they can tolerate
those heightened levels

01:03:02.400 --> 01:03:04.770
of anxiety and interrupt
their compulsions.

01:03:04.770 --> 01:03:05.910
Whereas when they get home,

01:03:05.910 --> 01:03:08.340
oftentimes the familiarity
of the environment

01:03:08.340 --> 01:03:09.930
brings 'em to a place
where all of a sudden

01:03:09.930 --> 01:03:12.150
those obsessions and compulsions start

01:03:12.150 --> 01:03:13.200
interacting the same way

01:03:13.200 --> 01:03:16.650
and they have a very hard time
suppressing the behaviors.

01:03:16.650 --> 01:03:17.730
Why would that be?

01:03:17.730 --> 01:03:20.310
Well in neuroscience, we have a phrase,

01:03:20.310 --> 01:03:22.020
it's called conditioned place preference

01:03:22.020 --> 01:03:23.700
and conditioned place avoidance.

01:03:23.700 --> 01:03:24.720
There's some other phrases too

01:03:24.720 --> 01:03:26.310
but basically it all has to do

01:03:26.310 --> 01:03:29.130
with a simple thing which is,

01:03:29.130 --> 01:03:31.740
when you feel something
repeatedly in a given environment,

01:03:31.740 --> 01:03:33.960
or sometimes even once
within a given environment,

01:03:33.960 --> 01:03:36.390
you tend to feel that same thing again

01:03:36.390 --> 01:03:38.760
when you return to that
or similar environments.

01:03:38.760 --> 01:03:40.140
Okay, So conditioned place blank,

01:03:40.140 --> 01:03:42.990
or conditioned place that
is simply fancy nerd speak

01:03:42.990 --> 01:03:44.970
for the fact that when you're in a place

01:03:44.970 --> 01:03:46.380
and something good happens,

01:03:46.380 --> 01:03:48.090
you tend to feel good if
you return to that place

01:03:48.090 --> 01:03:50.670
or a place like it, or
if something bad happens

01:03:50.670 --> 01:03:52.110
in a given place, you tend to feel bad

01:03:52.110 --> 01:03:54.900
when you return to that
place or a place like it.

01:03:54.900 --> 01:03:57.200
I think that most salient
example that leaps to mind

01:03:57.200 --> 01:03:58.980
is in, unfortunately, the category of bad,

01:03:58.980 --> 01:04:02.460
but I had some friends years
ago visit San Francisco.

01:04:02.460 --> 01:04:03.720
There's been a ongoing,

01:04:03.720 --> 01:04:05.490
it seems like it's been happening forever,

01:04:05.490 --> 01:04:06.750
but this is really in the last decade

01:04:06.750 --> 01:04:10.740
of daytime break-ins and
nighttime break-ins into cars

01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:12.960
to steal anything from computers

01:04:12.960 --> 01:04:14.640
to what seems to be like a box of tissues.

01:04:14.640 --> 01:04:16.470
And there are numerous reasons for this,

01:04:16.470 --> 01:04:17.303
I don't want to get into,

01:04:17.303 --> 01:04:18.780
it's not the topic of today's podcast,

01:04:18.780 --> 01:04:20.160
but I will use this as
an opportunity to say

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:21.990
if you're visiting
anywhere in the Bay Area,

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:23.130
do not leave anything in your car

01:04:23.130 --> 01:04:24.960
because the window will get broken into,

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:26.400
sometimes in broad daylight.

01:04:26.400 --> 01:04:28.500
Some good friends of mine
were visiting the Bay Area

01:04:28.500 --> 01:04:30.960
and I texted them and said,

01:04:30.960 --> 01:04:32.940
hey, by the way, when you're
headed to dinner, guys,

01:04:32.940 --> 01:04:36.180
make sure you bring in all
your luggage and computers

01:04:36.180 --> 01:04:38.370
however inconvenient that might be.

01:04:38.370 --> 01:04:41.310
They wrote back, too late,
everything got stolen.

01:04:41.310 --> 01:04:43.290
So some years ago now, I
think five, six years ago

01:04:43.290 --> 01:04:46.620
this happened, sadly,
everything got stolen.

01:04:46.620 --> 01:04:47.580
Most of it could be replaced,

01:04:47.580 --> 01:04:49.680
but some of it was very
sentimental to them.

01:04:49.680 --> 01:04:52.710
Every time we talk, every time we consider

01:04:52.710 --> 01:04:54.450
having a meeting in a particular city,

01:04:54.450 --> 01:04:56.670
this comes up as I don't want to be there,

01:04:56.670 --> 01:04:58.290
I don't like that city anymore, et cetera.

01:04:58.290 --> 01:04:59.820
And of course, San
Francisco has some wonderful

01:04:59.820 --> 01:05:02.610
redeeming features, but it
only takes one bad incident

01:05:02.610 --> 01:05:04.380
in one location to kind of color

01:05:04.380 --> 01:05:07.860
the whole picture dark, so to speak.

01:05:07.860 --> 01:05:09.120
The brain works that way.

01:05:09.120 --> 01:05:12.360
The brain generalizes, it's
not a very specific organ,

01:05:12.360 --> 01:05:13.650
again, it's a prediction machine

01:05:13.650 --> 01:05:15.030
in addition to other things.

01:05:15.030 --> 01:05:17.820
So in the case of CBT therapy,

01:05:17.820 --> 01:05:20.850
the reason there's homework
is that when people go home,

01:05:20.850 --> 01:05:23.460
oftentimes that's when they relapse,

01:05:23.460 --> 01:05:24.480
if you want to call it that,

01:05:24.480 --> 01:05:26.280
back into their obsessions
and compulsions.

01:05:26.280 --> 01:05:30.420
And that location, that conditioned place

01:05:30.420 --> 01:05:33.030
is where it becomes most
important to challenge

01:05:33.030 --> 01:05:34.890
the anxiety and to deal with the anxiety,

01:05:34.890 --> 01:05:36.420
to not try and suppress the anxiety

01:05:36.420 --> 01:05:38.880
through compulsions or other means.

01:05:38.880 --> 01:05:41.730
And when I say other means, I
want to highlight something,

01:05:41.730 --> 01:05:43.830
it will come up again a little
bit later in the podcast,

01:05:43.830 --> 01:05:47.310
that substance abuse is very
common in people with OCD

01:05:47.310 --> 01:05:48.960
because of the anxiety component

01:05:48.960 --> 01:05:50.790
and also because of people's feelings

01:05:50.790 --> 01:05:52.710
that they just can't
escape from the thoughts

01:05:52.710 --> 01:05:57.030
or behavioral patterns that
are so characteristic of OCD.

01:05:57.030 --> 01:05:59.270
So alcohol abuse or cannabis abuse,

01:05:59.270 --> 01:06:02.130
or other forms of narcotics abuse

01:06:02.130 --> 01:06:05.400
are very common in OCD.

01:06:05.400 --> 01:06:06.960
Later, we'll talk about
whether or not cannabis

01:06:06.960 --> 01:06:09.960
can or cannot help with OCD.

01:06:09.960 --> 01:06:11.880
But needless to say,

01:06:11.880 --> 01:06:15.360
suppressing anxiety is
exactly the wrong direction

01:06:15.360 --> 01:06:18.870
that one should take if
the goal is to ultimately

01:06:18.870 --> 01:06:21.510
relieve or eliminate the OCD.

01:06:21.510 --> 01:06:25.260
So we now have two characteristics
of CBT exposure therapy

01:06:25.260 --> 01:06:27.060
that are extremely important for OCD

01:06:27.060 --> 01:06:29.070
and somewhat unique to
the treatment of OCD

01:06:29.070 --> 01:06:31.680
and that's the staircasing up
towards the really bad fear,

01:06:31.680 --> 01:06:34.980
the really severe and
specific articulation

01:06:34.980 --> 01:06:37.380
and understanding and
feeling of how bad things

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:39.690
really would be if someone engaged

01:06:39.690 --> 01:06:42.030
in a particular behavior or
avoided a particular behavior.

01:06:42.030 --> 01:06:43.950
Then there's the component of homework

01:06:43.950 --> 01:06:47.430
given by the clinician for
the person to be able to

01:06:47.430 --> 01:06:49.560
create a broader set of context

01:06:49.560 --> 01:06:52.710
in which they can deal with the anxiety,

01:06:52.710 --> 01:06:54.450
not engage in the compulsions.

01:06:54.450 --> 01:06:57.720
And then a very unique
feature of treatment of OCD

01:06:57.720 --> 01:07:00.810
that you don't see in many
other psychiatric disorders

01:07:00.810 --> 01:07:02.190
are home visits.

01:07:02.190 --> 01:07:03.660
And I find this fascinating.

01:07:03.660 --> 01:07:06.510
I think that the field of
psychiatry and psychology

01:07:06.510 --> 01:07:10.740
traditionally doesn't allow
for or invite home visits,

01:07:10.740 --> 01:07:15.060
but this component of
context, location and context

01:07:15.060 --> 01:07:19.890
being so vital to the
treatment and relief of OCD

01:07:19.890 --> 01:07:23.640
has inspired many
psychiatrists and psychologists

01:07:23.640 --> 01:07:26.130
to get permission to do home visits

01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:27.720
where they actually go
visit their patients

01:07:27.720 --> 01:07:30.240
in their native setting,
in their home cages, right?

01:07:30.240 --> 01:07:32.820
They're not mice, but in
their home-home cages,

01:07:32.820 --> 01:07:34.380
I'm being facetious here, but people,

01:07:34.380 --> 01:07:36.150
mice live in cages, at
least in the laboratory,

01:07:36.150 --> 01:07:39.540
and humans generally live
in houses or elsewhere,

01:07:39.540 --> 01:07:42.450
so they visit them in their home

01:07:42.450 --> 01:07:44.610
in order to see how they're interacting

01:07:44.610 --> 01:07:46.890
and the particular locations that evoke

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:48.660
the most anxiety and the least anxiety.

01:07:48.660 --> 01:07:50.580
Some of the, I don't want
to call them crutches,

01:07:50.580 --> 01:07:53.160
but some of the tools
that people are using

01:07:53.160 --> 01:07:56.940
to confront and deal with the
obsessions and compulsions

01:07:56.940 --> 01:07:59.160
and in particular to try and identify

01:07:59.160 --> 01:08:01.980
some of the tools and tricks

01:08:01.980 --> 01:08:04.320
that people are using to try and avoid

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:06.690
that heightened anxiety,
because, once again,

01:08:06.690 --> 01:08:07.800
and I know I'm repeating myself,

01:08:07.800 --> 01:08:10.170
but I think this is just
so vital and so unique

01:08:10.170 --> 01:08:12.510
about OCD and the treatment of OCD,

01:08:12.510 --> 01:08:15.420
the critical need for the
patient to be able to tolerate

01:08:15.420 --> 01:08:20.190
extremely elevated levels
of anxiety is so crucial.

01:08:20.190 --> 01:08:23.190
So if people are avoiding
certain rooms in the house,

01:08:23.190 --> 01:08:25.980
or if people are avoiding certain foods

01:08:25.980 --> 01:08:27.660
or certain locations in the kitchen,

01:08:27.660 --> 01:08:29.520
the clinician can start to identify that

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:31.230
by mere observation.

01:08:31.230 --> 01:08:33.480
And I should mention here that patients

01:08:33.480 --> 01:08:36.720
are not always aware of
how they're interacting

01:08:36.720 --> 01:08:37.830
with their home environment.

01:08:37.830 --> 01:08:40.683
Some of these patterns are
so deeply ingrained in people

01:08:40.683 --> 01:08:42.810
that they don't even realize that they're

01:08:42.810 --> 01:08:44.130
constantly turning to the left,

01:08:44.130 --> 01:08:45.600
or they don't even realize that

01:08:45.600 --> 01:08:47.790
they're only washing their
hands on one side of the sink.

01:08:47.790 --> 01:08:50.010
And so the clinician,
by visiting the home,

01:08:50.010 --> 01:08:52.580
can start to interrogate
a bit in a polite way,

01:08:52.580 --> 01:08:54.330
in a friendly, in a supportive way

01:08:54.330 --> 01:08:57.600
as to, do you ever think
about why you always

01:08:57.600 --> 01:08:59.160
flip the faucet to the left

01:08:59.160 --> 01:09:01.530
or flip the faucet to
the right, et cetera.

01:09:01.530 --> 01:09:05.760
Now, we all do a lot of
things that are habitual.

01:09:05.760 --> 01:09:08.310
We all do things that are

01:09:08.310 --> 01:09:10.110
somewhat regular from day to day.

01:09:10.110 --> 01:09:13.110
In fact, I would invite
you to ask yourself,

01:09:13.110 --> 01:09:16.530
do you always put your
toothbrush in the same location?

01:09:16.530 --> 01:09:18.060
Do you always cap the toothbrush

01:09:18.060 --> 01:09:19.410
before or after you use it?

01:09:19.410 --> 01:09:20.243
What sorts of things do you-

01:09:20.243 --> 01:09:23.580
You wipe the little threading
on the toothpaste or not?

01:09:23.580 --> 01:09:26.430
I'm somebody, I confess that I have,

01:09:26.430 --> 01:09:28.770
well, I have about 3,500 pet peeves,

01:09:28.770 --> 01:09:29.670
but one of my pet peeves

01:09:29.670 --> 01:09:32.550
is toothpaste kind of on the
thread of the toothpaste.

01:09:32.550 --> 01:09:34.260
It really bothers me, I don't know why,

01:09:34.260 --> 01:09:36.870
almost as much as trying
to wipe it off bothers me,

01:09:36.870 --> 01:09:38.190
which creates a certain challenge.

01:09:38.190 --> 01:09:39.750
And if I talk about this any further,

01:09:39.750 --> 01:09:40.770
then I think I would qualify

01:09:40.770 --> 01:09:43.470
for obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder.

01:09:43.470 --> 01:09:44.640
But I have to say, I don't experience

01:09:44.640 --> 01:09:45.840
a ton of anxiety about it.

01:09:45.840 --> 01:09:47.040
It doesn't govern my life.

01:09:47.040 --> 01:09:48.720
In fact, I realize that right now

01:09:48.720 --> 01:09:50.670
there are tubes of toothpaste

01:09:50.670 --> 01:09:52.200
that have toothpaste along the thread

01:09:52.200 --> 01:09:53.635
everywhere in the world

01:09:53.635 --> 01:09:54.468
and it doesn't really bother me.

01:09:54.468 --> 01:09:57.780
I can still sit here and
provide some information

01:09:57.780 --> 01:09:58.620
about OCD to you.

01:09:58.620 --> 01:10:02.250
It's not intrusive, at
least not to my awareness.

01:10:02.250 --> 01:10:03.960
So by the home visit,

01:10:03.960 --> 01:10:06.120
the therapist can really start to explore

01:10:06.120 --> 01:10:09.210
through direct questioning
and can allow the patient

01:10:09.210 --> 01:10:11.460
to explore through direct
questioning of themselves

01:10:11.460 --> 01:10:12.810
the things that they might be conscious of

01:10:12.810 --> 01:10:15.270
and the things that they
might not be conscious of

01:10:15.270 --> 01:10:17.610
that would qualify for OCD.

01:10:17.610 --> 01:10:20.250
So I'd like to just briefly
summarize the key elements

01:10:20.250 --> 01:10:23.370
of cognitive behavioral
therapy and exposure therapy

01:10:23.370 --> 01:10:26.190
and how they can be combined
with drug treatments

01:10:26.190 --> 01:10:27.393
that are very effective.

01:10:28.290 --> 01:10:29.910
Much of what I'm going to talk about next

01:10:29.910 --> 01:10:33.540
relates to the data
and indeed the practice

01:10:33.540 --> 01:10:38.250
of an incredible research
scientist and clinician.

01:10:38.250 --> 01:10:40.140
So this is Helen Blair Simpson,

01:10:40.140 --> 01:10:42.120
or I should say Dr. Helen Blair Simpson,

01:10:42.120 --> 01:10:44.430
because she is indeed an MD medical doctor

01:10:44.430 --> 01:10:46.050
and a PhD research scientist

01:10:46.050 --> 01:10:49.230
at Columbia University School of Medicine.

01:10:49.230 --> 01:10:52.290
And one of the world's foremost experts,

01:10:52.290 --> 01:10:54.780
if not the expert, I would
put her in a category

01:10:54.780 --> 01:10:57.960
of maybe just one to three people who

01:10:57.960 --> 01:11:01.470
is most knowledgeable about
the mechanisms of OCD,

01:11:01.470 --> 01:11:04.740
is actively researching OCD in humans,

01:11:04.740 --> 01:11:06.150
trying to find new treatments,

01:11:06.150 --> 01:11:08.760
trying to unveil new mechanisms

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:12.240
and expand on our current
understanding and who also treats

01:11:12.240 --> 01:11:14.643
OCD quite actively in her own clinic.

01:11:16.200 --> 01:11:19.320
Dr. Simpson gave a beautiful presentation

01:11:19.320 --> 01:11:21.960
which she summarized
some of the core elements

01:11:21.960 --> 01:11:23.730
of CBT and exposure therapy

01:11:23.730 --> 01:11:26.190
for the treatment of
obsessive-compulsive disorders.

01:11:26.190 --> 01:11:29.340
She describes that the key
procedures are exposures,

01:11:29.340 --> 01:11:32.070
of course, done in person

01:11:32.070 --> 01:11:34.200
and with the actual thing that evokes

01:11:34.200 --> 01:11:36.450
the obsessions and compulsions.

01:11:36.450 --> 01:11:39.630
So this could be the sweaty
towel as described earlier,

01:11:39.630 --> 01:11:43.380
or could be any number of
different triggers done

01:11:43.380 --> 01:11:45.090
with the patient in real time,

01:11:45.090 --> 01:11:47.100
so in vivo, as we say.

01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:50.250
And it could also be
things that are imaginal,

01:11:50.250 --> 01:11:52.050
sitting somebody down in a chair,

01:11:52.050 --> 01:11:53.340
in an office and saying,

01:11:53.340 --> 01:11:55.950
okay, I want you to imagine the thing

01:11:55.950 --> 01:11:57.360
that triggers the intrusive thought,

01:11:57.360 --> 01:12:01.050
or let's just focus on the
intrusive thought as it arises,

01:12:01.050 --> 01:12:03.180
and then to explore and expose the patient

01:12:03.180 --> 01:12:04.770
to their obsessions and
compulsions that way.

01:12:04.770 --> 01:12:06.900
So it can be real, or it can be imaginal.

01:12:06.900 --> 01:12:08.250
And the goal, of course, then

01:12:08.250 --> 01:12:10.920
is to gradually and progressively

01:12:10.920 --> 01:12:12.450
increase the level of anxiety,

01:12:12.450 --> 01:12:15.390
but then to intervene in
so-called ritual prevention

01:12:15.390 --> 01:12:18.300
to prevent the person from
engaging in the compulsion.

01:12:18.300 --> 01:12:20.910
The goals, again I'm paraphrasing here,

01:12:20.910 --> 01:12:24.330
are to, as she states, disconfirm fears

01:12:24.330 --> 01:12:28.770
and challenge the beliefs
about the obsessions

01:12:28.770 --> 01:12:30.960
and compulsions, to
intervene in the thoughts

01:12:30.960 --> 01:12:32.430
and the behaviors, and to break the habit

01:12:32.430 --> 01:12:34.110
of ritualizing and avoiding.

01:12:34.110 --> 01:12:35.460
Now, how is this typically done?

01:12:35.460 --> 01:12:38.490
What are the nuts and
bolts of this procedure?

01:12:38.490 --> 01:12:42.330
Typically, this is done
through two planning sessions

01:12:42.330 --> 01:12:43.163
with the patient.

01:12:43.163 --> 01:12:45.450
So describing to the
patient what will happen

01:12:45.450 --> 01:12:47.790
and when it will happen
and how long it will happen

01:12:47.790 --> 01:12:50.010
so that they're not just thrown into this

01:12:50.010 --> 01:12:51.090
out of the blue.

01:12:51.090 --> 01:12:55.710
And then 15 exposure sessions
done twice a week or more.

01:12:55.710 --> 01:12:57.120
So the one thing to really understand

01:12:57.120 --> 01:12:58.500
about cognitive behavioral therapy

01:12:58.500 --> 01:13:00.240
is that it can take some period of time,

01:13:00.240 --> 01:13:03.480
several or more weeks, as
many as 10 or 12 weeks.

01:13:03.480 --> 01:13:05.160
However, as you'll soon learn,

01:13:05.160 --> 01:13:06.900
many of the drug treatments
that are effective

01:13:06.900 --> 01:13:09.750
in treating OCD either
alone or in combination

01:13:09.750 --> 01:13:13.440
with behavioral therapies
also can take 8, 10, 12 weeks

01:13:13.440 --> 01:13:16.080
or longer, and many of
those never work at all.

01:13:16.080 --> 01:13:17.760
So even though 10 to 12 weeks

01:13:17.760 --> 01:13:19.140
seems like a long period of time,

01:13:19.140 --> 01:13:20.400
it's actually a pretty standard.

01:13:20.400 --> 01:13:22.590
If you'd like to see
more complete description

01:13:22.590 --> 01:13:24.630
of the protocols for
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:13:24.630 --> 01:13:26.730
and exposure therapy for OCD,

01:13:26.730 --> 01:13:28.290
I'll provide links to two papers,

01:13:28.290 --> 01:13:30.630
Kozak and Foa, F-O-A,

01:13:30.630 --> 01:13:32.310
which is published in 1997,

01:13:32.310 --> 01:13:33.600
which might seem like a long time ago,

01:13:33.600 --> 01:13:36.510
but nonetheless, that the
protocols are still very useful.

01:13:36.510 --> 01:13:39.000
And then the second paper
is by that last author,

01:13:39.000 --> 01:13:40.800
FOA et al in 2012

01:13:40.800 --> 01:13:42.960
and we'll provide links to both of those.

01:13:42.960 --> 01:13:45.930
In addition, Dr. Blair Simpson and others

01:13:45.930 --> 01:13:48.660
have explored what are the best treatments

01:13:48.660 --> 01:13:51.360
for patients with OCD by comparing

01:13:51.360 --> 01:13:54.270
cognitive behavioral
therapy alone, placebo,

01:13:54.270 --> 01:13:57.060
so essentially no intervention

01:13:57.060 --> 01:13:59.130
or something that takes an
equivalent amount of time

01:13:59.130 --> 01:14:03.090
but is not thought to be
effective in treatment.

01:14:03.090 --> 01:14:07.380
As well as selective
serotonin reuptake inhibitors.

01:14:07.380 --> 01:14:09.721
So what is an SSRI?

01:14:09.721 --> 01:14:13.050
An SSRI is a drug that
prevents the re-uptake

01:14:13.050 --> 01:14:14.820
of serotonin at the synapse.

01:14:14.820 --> 01:14:15.653
What are synapses?

01:14:15.653 --> 01:14:17.580
They're the little spaces between neurons

01:14:17.580 --> 01:14:19.530
where neurons communicate with one another

01:14:19.530 --> 01:14:22.680
by vomiting little bits of
chemical into the space,

01:14:22.680 --> 01:14:26.010
the synapse, and then those
chemicals either evoke

01:14:26.010 --> 01:14:27.900
or suppress the electrical activity

01:14:27.900 --> 01:14:30.720
of the next neuron across the synapse.

01:14:30.720 --> 01:14:32.580
And in this case, the neurotransmitter,

01:14:32.580 --> 01:14:35.010
the chemical that we're
referring to is serotonin.

01:14:35.010 --> 01:14:38.670
SSRI, selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitors

01:14:38.670 --> 01:14:43.020
prevent the reuptake of
the chemical that's left,

01:14:43.020 --> 01:14:45.390
in this case, the serotonin
that's left in the synapse.

01:14:45.390 --> 01:14:48.150
After that, I call it
vomiting to be dramatic,

01:14:48.150 --> 01:14:49.170
but it's not actually vomiting,

01:14:49.170 --> 01:14:52.710
the extrusion of the
chemical into the synapse.

01:14:52.710 --> 01:14:55.800
And as a consequence,
there's more serotonin around

01:14:55.800 --> 01:14:57.720
to have more of an effect over time,

01:14:57.720 --> 01:15:00.690
the net effect being more
serotonergic transmission,

01:15:00.690 --> 01:15:02.670
more serotonin overall.

01:15:02.670 --> 01:15:04.470
So not more serotonin being made,

01:15:04.470 --> 01:15:06.870
more serotonin being available for use,

01:15:06.870 --> 01:15:08.670
that's what an SSRI does.

01:15:08.670 --> 01:15:13.470
So they compared cognitive
behavioral therapy, SSRIs,

01:15:13.470 --> 01:15:14.700
they also had the placebo group

01:15:14.700 --> 01:15:16.200
and they had cognitive behavioral therapy

01:15:16.200 --> 01:15:19.950
plus the selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitor.

01:15:19.950 --> 01:15:22.710
This was a 12-week study
done as described before,

01:15:22.710 --> 01:15:25.380
two times a week over
the course of 12 weeks.

01:15:25.380 --> 01:15:28.380
First of all, the most
important thing, of course,

01:15:28.380 --> 01:15:29.520
placebo did nothing.

01:15:29.520 --> 01:15:34.520
It did not relieve the OCD
to any significant degree.

01:15:34.860 --> 01:15:35.693
How did they know that?

01:15:35.693 --> 01:15:36.960
They gave them the Y-BOCS test

01:15:36.960 --> 01:15:40.590
that we talked about
before, the Yale-Brown test

01:15:40.590 --> 01:15:42.960
with all those questions
of which I read a few.

01:15:42.960 --> 01:15:46.560
So the OCD severity that one has to have

01:15:46.560 --> 01:15:49.230
on the Y-BOCS is measured
in terms of an index

01:15:49.230 --> 01:15:52.860
that goes from here from
8 all the way up to 28,

01:15:52.860 --> 01:15:53.990
that shouldn't mean anything.

01:15:53.990 --> 01:15:55.890
So that number eight is
kind of meaningless here.

01:15:55.890 --> 01:15:58.290
It's in terms of an index
that's only meaningful

01:15:58.290 --> 01:16:01.350
for the Y-BOCS, but if
somebody has a threshold

01:16:01.350 --> 01:16:04.110
of 16 or higher, it means
that they're still having

01:16:04.110 --> 01:16:05.430
somewhat debilitating symptoms

01:16:05.430 --> 01:16:07.170
or very debilitating symptoms.

01:16:07.170 --> 01:16:10.410
Placebo did not reduce the
obsessions or compulsions

01:16:10.410 --> 01:16:12.270
to any significant degree.

01:16:12.270 --> 01:16:14.490
However, and I think quite excitingly,

01:16:14.490 --> 01:16:17.680
cognitive behavioral
therapy had a dramatic

01:16:18.570 --> 01:16:22.320
effect in reducing the
obsessions and compulsions

01:16:22.320 --> 01:16:26.010
such that by four weeks, that
score that, in this case,

01:16:26.010 --> 01:16:27.270
ranged from 8 to 28,

01:16:27.270 --> 01:16:31.380
dropped all the way from
25 down to about 11.

01:16:31.380 --> 01:16:35.190
So it's a huge drop in the
severity of the symptoms.

01:16:35.190 --> 01:16:36.600
Now, what's really interesting

01:16:36.600 --> 01:16:39.480
is that when you look
at the effects of SSRIs

01:16:39.480 --> 01:16:42.900
in the treatment of OCD symptoms,

01:16:42.900 --> 01:16:45.960
they had a significant effect
in reducing the symptoms

01:16:45.960 --> 01:16:48.780
of OCD that showed up first at four weeks,

01:16:48.780 --> 01:16:51.300
and then continued to eight weeks.

01:16:51.300 --> 01:16:54.450
In fact, there was a progressive
and further reduction

01:16:54.450 --> 01:16:57.120
in OCD symptoms from the
four to eight week period.

01:16:57.120 --> 01:16:59.970
Again, these are the people
just taking the SSRI,

01:16:59.970 --> 01:17:01.650
and then it sort of
flattened out a little bit,

01:17:01.650 --> 01:17:03.090
such that by 12 weeks,

01:17:03.090 --> 01:17:05.760
there was still a significant
reduction in OCD symptoms

01:17:05.760 --> 01:17:09.000
for people taking SSRIs
as compared to placebo.

01:17:09.000 --> 01:17:11.400
But the severity of their symptoms

01:17:11.400 --> 01:17:14.010
was still much greater than those

01:17:14.010 --> 01:17:16.590
receiving cognitive
behavioral therapy alone.

01:17:16.590 --> 01:17:18.150
So at least in this study,

01:17:18.150 --> 01:17:19.680
and I should tell you which study it is,

01:17:19.680 --> 01:17:21.990
this is Foa, Liebowitz et al 2005

01:17:21.990 --> 01:17:23.400
in the American Journal of Psychiatry,

01:17:23.400 --> 01:17:24.810
we'll also provide a link to this

01:17:24.810 --> 01:17:27.060
so you can peruse the data if you like.

01:17:27.060 --> 01:17:28.440
But at least in this study,

01:17:28.440 --> 01:17:31.350
cognitive behavioral therapy
was the most effective,

01:17:31.350 --> 01:17:35.130
selective serotonin reuptake
inhibitors, less effective.

01:17:35.130 --> 01:17:36.720
So what happens when you combine them?

01:17:36.720 --> 01:17:38.130
Well, they explored that as well,

01:17:38.130 --> 01:17:40.253
and the combination of
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:17:40.253 --> 01:17:42.810
and the SSRIs together

01:17:42.810 --> 01:17:47.463
did not lead to any further
decrease in OCD symptoms.

01:17:48.330 --> 01:17:51.480
This points to the idea that
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:17:51.480 --> 01:17:52.830
is the most effective treatment.

01:17:52.830 --> 01:17:54.540
And again, when I say
cognitive behavioral therapy,

01:17:54.540 --> 01:17:56.520
now I'm still referring
to cognitive behavioral,

01:17:56.520 --> 01:17:58.770
slash, exposure therapy done in the way

01:17:58.770 --> 01:17:59.610
that I detailed before,

01:17:59.610 --> 01:18:01.950
twice a week for 12 weeks or more.

01:18:01.950 --> 01:18:03.690
So all of the data, at
least in this study,

01:18:03.690 --> 01:18:05.880
point to the fact that
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:18:05.880 --> 01:18:08.430
is really effective
and the most effective.

01:18:08.430 --> 01:18:11.910
Does it alleviate OCD
symptoms for everybody?

01:18:11.910 --> 01:18:12.743
No.

01:18:12.743 --> 01:18:13.920
Is it very time consuming? Yes.

01:18:13.920 --> 01:18:17.730
Twice a week for two sessions
or more of 15 minutes,

01:18:17.730 --> 01:18:19.800
sometimes in the office,
plus there's homework,

01:18:19.800 --> 01:18:22.290
plus, in an ideal case,
there's also home visits

01:18:22.290 --> 01:18:24.240
from the psychiatrist or psychologist,

01:18:24.240 --> 01:18:28.530
that's a lot of investment,
a lot of time investment,

01:18:28.530 --> 01:18:31.590
to say nothing of the
potential financial investment.

01:18:31.590 --> 01:18:34.350
Now, Dr. Blair Simpson has
given some beautiful talks

01:18:34.350 --> 01:18:37.290
where she describes these data

01:18:37.290 --> 01:18:39.750
and also emphasizes the fact that

01:18:39.750 --> 01:18:42.300
despite the demonstrated power

01:18:42.300 --> 01:18:45.150
of cognitive behavioral therapy
for the treatment of OCD,

01:18:45.150 --> 01:18:46.620
most people are given drug treatments

01:18:46.620 --> 01:18:49.050
simply because of the availability
of those drug treatments.

01:18:49.050 --> 01:18:50.083
Now, when I say most people,

01:18:50.083 --> 01:18:51.720
I want to emphasize that I'm referring

01:18:51.720 --> 01:18:54.750
to most people who
actually go seek treatment

01:18:54.750 --> 01:18:56.820
because a really
important thing to realize

01:18:56.820 --> 01:18:58.710
is that most people with OCD

01:18:58.710 --> 01:19:01.920
do not actually go seek
evidence-based treatment.

01:19:01.920 --> 01:19:03.720
I want to repeat that,
most people with OCD

01:19:03.720 --> 01:19:05.580
do not seek evidence-based treatment,

01:19:05.580 --> 01:19:06.870
which is a tragic thing.

01:19:06.870 --> 01:19:09.570
One of the motivations for
doing this podcast episode

01:19:09.570 --> 01:19:11.160
is to try and encourage people

01:19:11.160 --> 01:19:13.200
who think they may have
persistent obsessions

01:19:13.200 --> 01:19:16.110
and compulsions to seek treatment,

01:19:16.110 --> 01:19:19.770
but most people don't
for a variety of reasons

01:19:19.770 --> 01:19:22.680
we spelled out earlier, shame, et cetera.

01:19:22.680 --> 01:19:25.350
Of those that do, the first line of attack

01:19:25.350 --> 01:19:29.370
is typically a prescription,
most often an SSRI,

01:19:29.370 --> 01:19:31.350
although not always just SSRIs

01:19:31.350 --> 01:19:35.460
because soon we'll talk
about the somewhat common use

01:19:35.460 --> 01:19:39.110
of also prescribing a
low dose of a neuroleptic

01:19:39.110 --> 01:19:42.570
or an antipsychotic, not always but often.

01:19:42.570 --> 01:19:44.790
So the important thing to understand here

01:19:44.790 --> 01:19:47.580
is that excellent
researchers like Dr. Simpson

01:19:47.580 --> 01:19:49.920
understand that while there are treatments

01:19:49.920 --> 01:19:52.800
that we could say are best or are ideal

01:19:52.800 --> 01:19:54.840
based on the data, that
doesn't necessarily mean

01:19:54.840 --> 01:19:56.730
that's what's being deployed

01:19:56.730 --> 01:19:58.650
most often in the general public.

01:19:58.650 --> 01:20:01.470
As a consequence, Dr. Simpson and others

01:20:01.470 --> 01:20:04.530
have explored in a very practical way

01:20:04.530 --> 01:20:07.140
whether or not it matters if somebody

01:20:07.140 --> 01:20:09.870
is getting SSRI treatment

01:20:09.870 --> 01:20:14.280
and is experiencing that
reduction in OCD symptoms

01:20:14.280 --> 01:20:17.160
that as you may recall, is more than

01:20:17.160 --> 01:20:19.950
what they would experience
with placebo alone,

01:20:19.950 --> 01:20:22.400
but not as dramatic a
reduction in OCD symptoms

01:20:22.400 --> 01:20:25.050
as they would get with
cognitive behavioral therapy.

01:20:25.050 --> 01:20:27.540
And as I mentioned before,
there was this exploration

01:20:27.540 --> 01:20:29.160
of combining drug treatment

01:20:29.160 --> 01:20:31.470
with cognitive behavioral
therapy from the outset,

01:20:31.470 --> 01:20:34.170
but they also quite impressively explored

01:20:34.170 --> 01:20:37.290
what happens when people
who are already taking SSRIs

01:20:37.290 --> 01:20:39.510
initiate cognitive behavioral therapy.

01:20:39.510 --> 01:20:41.040
This is a really wonderful thing

01:20:41.040 --> 01:20:43.740
that they've done this
because in doing that,

01:20:43.740 --> 01:20:45.420
first of all, they're acknowledging that

01:20:45.420 --> 01:20:47.250
there are many people out
there who have sought treatment

01:20:47.250 --> 01:20:49.800
and are getting some
relief from those SSRIs,

01:20:49.800 --> 01:20:52.590
but it perhaps is not as much
relief as they could get.

01:20:52.590 --> 01:20:56.340
And they are actively
acknowledging that many people

01:20:56.340 --> 01:20:58.290
are getting these drug treatments first.

01:20:58.290 --> 01:20:59.610
In fact, most often people are getting

01:20:59.610 --> 01:21:01.260
these drug treatments first.

01:21:01.260 --> 01:21:03.330
So what happens when you add in

01:21:03.330 --> 01:21:04.620
cognitive behavioral therapy?

01:21:04.620 --> 01:21:05.910
Well, the good news is when you add

01:21:05.910 --> 01:21:07.980
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:21:07.980 --> 01:21:10.830
to someone who's already taking SSRIs,

01:21:10.830 --> 01:21:13.200
that further improves their symptoms.

01:21:13.200 --> 01:21:16.320
Now that's different than the results

01:21:16.320 --> 01:21:18.150
that I described before

01:21:18.150 --> 01:21:20.490
from the same laboratory in fact,

01:21:20.490 --> 01:21:23.520
that if you combine
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:21:23.520 --> 01:21:25.620
with SSRIs from the outset,

01:21:25.620 --> 01:21:28.293
there's no additional benefit of SSRI.

01:21:29.370 --> 01:21:30.840
However, as I just described,

01:21:30.840 --> 01:21:33.060
if someone is already taking an SSRI

01:21:33.060 --> 01:21:37.380
and they're experiencing a
reduction in their OCD symptoms,

01:21:37.380 --> 01:21:40.110
by adding in cognitive behavioral therapy,

01:21:40.110 --> 01:21:44.624
there is a further reduction
in the symptoms of OCD.

01:21:44.624 --> 01:21:45.457
This is very important.

01:21:45.457 --> 01:21:46.800
So for those of you that
have sought treatment

01:21:46.800 --> 01:21:49.010
and you're taking a SSRI,

01:21:49.010 --> 01:21:50.460
or if you're thinking about treatment

01:21:50.460 --> 01:21:52.080
and you're prescribed an SSRI,

01:21:52.080 --> 01:21:53.520
the ideal scenario really would be

01:21:53.520 --> 01:21:55.020
to combine the drug treatment

01:21:55.020 --> 01:21:56.460
with cognitive behavioral therapy,

01:21:56.460 --> 01:21:58.830
or in some cases, maybe cognitive
behavioral therapy alone,

01:21:58.830 --> 01:22:00.960
although that's a decision
that you really have to make

01:22:00.960 --> 01:22:02.170
with the close

01:22:03.240 --> 01:22:06.690
advice and oversight of
a licensed physician,

01:22:06.690 --> 01:22:08.700
because, of course, these
are prescription drugs.

01:22:08.700 --> 01:22:10.680
And anytime you're going to add or remove

01:22:10.680 --> 01:22:12.210
a prescription drug or change dosage,

01:22:12.210 --> 01:22:14.657
you really want to do that
in close discussion with

01:22:14.657 --> 01:22:16.590
and on the advice of your physician.

01:22:16.590 --> 01:22:17.700
I don't just say that to protect me,

01:22:17.700 --> 01:22:19.067
I say that to protect you

01:22:19.067 --> 01:22:20.640
'cause it's just the right thing to do.

01:22:20.640 --> 01:22:22.650
So again, cognitive behavioral therapy

01:22:22.650 --> 01:22:23.880
is extremely powerful.

01:22:23.880 --> 01:22:26.070
Drug treatments seem less powerful though.

01:22:26.070 --> 01:22:27.360
If you're already on a drug treatment,

01:22:27.360 --> 01:22:29.670
adding cognitive behavioral
therapy can really help.

01:22:29.670 --> 01:22:31.020
So I've been talking about SSRIs

01:22:31.020 --> 01:22:32.550
and described a little
bit about how they work

01:22:32.550 --> 01:22:34.650
at a kind of superficial level of

01:22:34.650 --> 01:22:36.600
keeping more serotonin in the synapse

01:22:36.600 --> 01:22:39.570
so that more serotonin can be in action

01:22:39.570 --> 01:22:42.570
as opposed to gobbled
back up by those neurons.

01:22:42.570 --> 01:22:43.470
I should just mention what some

01:22:43.470 --> 01:22:46.230
of the selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitors are.

01:22:46.230 --> 01:22:48.633
So things like clomipramine,

01:22:49.500 --> 01:22:52.740
which is not entirely selective,

01:22:52.740 --> 01:22:54.870
I should say that that one generally falls

01:22:54.870 --> 01:22:56.610
into a category of less selective.

01:22:56.610 --> 01:22:59.130
So it can impair

01:22:59.130 --> 01:23:02.630
or can enhance some of
the other neurotransmitter

01:23:02.630 --> 01:23:05.850
or neuromodulator systems
like epinephrine, et cetera.

01:23:05.850 --> 01:23:09.420
The selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitors are,

01:23:09.420 --> 01:23:11.910
at least the classic ones
are, fluoxetine, Prozac,

01:23:11.910 --> 01:23:15.600
fluvoxamine, Luvox,
paroxetine, sertraline,

01:23:15.600 --> 01:23:17.643
citalopram, et cetera, et cetera.

01:23:19.080 --> 01:23:21.900
There are about six or classic SSRIs,

01:23:21.900 --> 01:23:23.430
some of them like citalopram

01:23:23.430 --> 01:23:26.370
are used in children and are available

01:23:26.370 --> 01:23:27.870
in pediatric doses.

01:23:27.870 --> 01:23:30.660
Some like Prozac may or may
not be used in children.

01:23:30.660 --> 01:23:33.720
The details of which SSRIs, et cetera,

01:23:33.720 --> 01:23:36.180
is a very extensive
literature and discussion.

01:23:36.180 --> 01:23:39.090
And I think it's safe to say that

01:23:39.090 --> 01:23:41.430
which drugs to use and which dosage

01:23:41.430 --> 01:23:44.100
and whether or not to continue, excuse me,

01:23:44.100 --> 01:23:46.320
the same dosage over time depends a lot

01:23:46.320 --> 01:23:48.930
on the individual variation
that people express

01:23:48.930 --> 01:23:50.490
and the responses that they have.

01:23:50.490 --> 01:23:52.710
All of these drugs, in
fact, I think we can say

01:23:52.710 --> 01:23:54.930
all drugs have side effects.

01:23:54.930 --> 01:23:58.590
The question is how detrimental
those side effects are

01:23:58.590 --> 01:23:59.423
to daily life.

01:23:59.423 --> 01:24:02.400
The SSRIs are well known to
have effects on appetite.

01:24:02.400 --> 01:24:04.920
In some cases, they abolish appetite.

01:24:04.920 --> 01:24:06.960
In some cases, they just
reduce it a little bit.

01:24:06.960 --> 01:24:09.120
In some cases, they increase appetite.

01:24:09.120 --> 01:24:10.770
Really is highly individual.

01:24:10.770 --> 01:24:12.360
They can have effects on libido.

01:24:12.360 --> 01:24:14.703
For instance, they can reduce sex drive,

01:24:15.720 --> 01:24:17.160
sometimes in a dose dependent way,

01:24:17.160 --> 01:24:19.380
sometimes in a way that's
more like a step function

01:24:19.380 --> 01:24:22.110
where people are fine at
say 5 or 10 milligrams,

01:24:22.110 --> 01:24:25.020
but then they get to 15 milligrams

01:24:25.020 --> 01:24:27.933
and there's a cliff for their libido.

01:24:28.860 --> 01:24:30.540
That can happen, it really depends.

01:24:30.540 --> 01:24:32.700
Please don't take those
dosages as exact values

01:24:32.700 --> 01:24:34.800
'cause this is going to depend on

01:24:34.800 --> 01:24:37.620
what they're being used for,
depression or anxiety or OCD,

01:24:37.620 --> 01:24:39.450
and it's also going to depend
on the drug, et cetera.

01:24:39.450 --> 01:24:41.634
I just threw out those numbers as a way

01:24:41.634 --> 01:24:43.470
to illustrate what a
kind of a step function

01:24:43.470 --> 01:24:44.303
would look like.

01:24:44.303 --> 01:24:46.200
It's not gradual, it's
immediate at a given dose

01:24:46.200 --> 01:24:47.700
is what that means.

01:24:47.700 --> 01:24:50.880
The other thing is that
some of these drugs

01:24:50.880 --> 01:24:52.920
will have transient effects.

01:24:52.920 --> 01:24:54.750
So side effects that show
up and then disappear

01:24:54.750 --> 01:24:57.240
or sadly people will
sometimes take these drugs

01:24:57.240 --> 01:24:59.130
for a while and then side
effects will surface later

01:24:59.130 --> 01:25:00.900
that weren't there previously

01:25:00.900 --> 01:25:03.060
depending on life factors,
nutrition factors.

01:25:03.060 --> 01:25:05.850
So it's a very complicated
landscape overall.

01:25:05.850 --> 01:25:07.500
And that's why it's really
important to explore

01:25:07.500 --> 01:25:09.990
any kind of drug treatment,
SSRI or otherwise,

01:25:09.990 --> 01:25:12.330
really in close communication
with a psychiatrist

01:25:12.330 --> 01:25:14.190
who really understands
the pharmacokinetics

01:25:14.190 --> 01:25:15.630
and has a lot of patient history

01:25:15.630 --> 01:25:17.130
and experience with them.

01:25:17.130 --> 01:25:18.390
So what I'm about to tell you next

01:25:18.390 --> 01:25:20.790
is most certainly going
to come as a big surprise,

01:25:20.790 --> 01:25:22.920
which is that despite the fact

01:25:22.920 --> 01:25:24.960
that the selective serotonin
reuptake inhibitors

01:25:24.960 --> 01:25:27.720
can be effective in reducing
the symptoms of OCD,

01:25:27.720 --> 01:25:29.250
at least somewhat,

01:25:29.250 --> 01:25:31.290
and certainly more than placebo,

01:25:31.290 --> 01:25:33.810
there is very little, if any evidence,

01:25:33.810 --> 01:25:36.840
that the serotonin system
is disrupted in OCD.

01:25:36.840 --> 01:25:38.670
And I have to point out that this is

01:25:38.670 --> 01:25:42.300
a somewhat consistent theme
in the field of psychiatry,

01:25:42.300 --> 01:25:45.330
that is a given drug can be very effective

01:25:45.330 --> 01:25:48.540
or even partially effective
in reducing symptoms

01:25:48.540 --> 01:25:50.880
or in changing the overall landscape

01:25:50.880 --> 01:25:53.640
of a psychiatric disorder or illness,

01:25:53.640 --> 01:25:56.100
and yet there is very little,

01:25:56.100 --> 01:25:58.050
if any evidence, that
that particular system

01:25:58.050 --> 01:26:02.550
is what's causal for OCD, or anxiety,

01:26:02.550 --> 01:26:03.810
or depression, et cetera.

01:26:03.810 --> 01:26:05.460
This is just the landscape
that we're living in

01:26:05.460 --> 01:26:06.990
in terms of our understanding of the brain

01:26:06.990 --> 01:26:09.720
and psychiatry and the ways
of treating brain disorders.

01:26:09.720 --> 01:26:12.510
So as a consequence,
there are a huge number

01:26:12.510 --> 01:26:15.720
of academic reviews that
clinicians and research scientists

01:26:15.720 --> 01:26:17.493
have generated and read and share.

01:26:18.330 --> 01:26:22.440
One of the more, I think, thorough ones

01:26:22.440 --> 01:26:24.780
in recent years was published in 2021.

01:26:24.780 --> 01:26:25.710
I'll provide a link to this.

01:26:25.710 --> 01:26:29.130
This is by an excellent,
truly excellent researcher

01:26:29.130 --> 01:26:31.260
from Yale university School of Medicine,

01:26:31.260 --> 01:26:32.490
I should say not just a researcher

01:26:32.490 --> 01:26:35.010
but a clinician scientist,
again, an MD-PhD.

01:26:35.010 --> 01:26:37.170
This is Christopher Pittenger

01:26:37.170 --> 01:26:38.190
And the title of the review

01:26:38.190 --> 01:26:41.970
is Pharmacotherapeutic Strategies

01:26:41.970 --> 01:26:44.430
and New Targets in OCD.

01:26:44.430 --> 01:26:46.740
And again, we'll provide a link to it.

01:26:46.740 --> 01:26:49.680
This is a just gorgeous review describing,

01:26:49.680 --> 01:26:51.480
as I just told you, that
the serotonin system

01:26:51.480 --> 01:26:53.370
isn't really disrupted in OCD

01:26:53.370 --> 01:26:56.040
and yet SSRIs can be very effective.

01:26:56.040 --> 01:27:00.120
The review goes on to explore
even what sorts of receptors

01:27:00.120 --> 01:27:01.830
for serotonin might be involved.

01:27:01.830 --> 01:27:05.070
If it's in fact the case that serotonin

01:27:05.070 --> 01:27:10.070
is a culprit in the
creation of OCD symptoms.

01:27:11.430 --> 01:27:13.830
Talk about the serotonin 2A receptor

01:27:13.830 --> 01:27:15.180
and the serotonin 1A receptor.

01:27:15.180 --> 01:27:16.800
Why am I mentioning all that detail?

01:27:16.800 --> 01:27:18.270
If in fact it's not clear,

01:27:18.270 --> 01:27:20.250
serotonin is involved because

01:27:20.250 --> 01:27:21.840
I'll just tell you right now,

01:27:21.840 --> 01:27:23.190
there is currently a lot of interest

01:27:23.190 --> 01:27:25.320
in whether or not some
of the psychedelics,

01:27:25.320 --> 01:27:27.077
in particular psilocybin, can be effective

01:27:27.077 --> 01:27:29.520
in the treatment of OCD.

01:27:29.520 --> 01:27:32.580
Psilocybin has been shown
in various clinical trials

01:27:32.580 --> 01:27:34.050
in particular the clinical trials done

01:27:34.050 --> 01:27:35.880
at Johns Hopkins School of Medicine

01:27:35.880 --> 01:27:37.650
by Matthew Johnson and others.

01:27:37.650 --> 01:27:39.420
Matthew was on The Huberman Lab Podcast.

01:27:39.420 --> 01:27:41.040
He's been on the Tim Ferris podcast.

01:27:41.040 --> 01:27:42.990
He's been on the Lex Fridman podcast.

01:27:42.990 --> 01:27:44.280
He's a world class researcher

01:27:44.280 --> 01:27:46.560
on the use of psychedelics for depression

01:27:46.560 --> 01:27:49.500
and other psychiatric challenges.

01:27:49.500 --> 01:27:51.720
And their psilocybin treatment

01:27:51.720 --> 01:27:53.610
has been seen, at least in those trials,

01:27:53.610 --> 01:27:55.110
to be very effective in the treatment

01:27:55.110 --> 01:27:56.960
of certain kinds of major depression.

01:27:58.080 --> 01:28:00.210
Currently the exploration of psilocybin

01:28:00.210 --> 01:28:02.100
for the treatment of OCD

01:28:02.100 --> 01:28:03.750
has not yielded similar results,

01:28:03.750 --> 01:28:05.460
although the studies are ongoing.

01:28:05.460 --> 01:28:07.530
Again, has not yielded
similar effectiveness,

01:28:07.530 --> 01:28:09.120
but the studies are ongoing

01:28:09.120 --> 01:28:11.070
And the serotonin 2A receptor

01:28:11.070 --> 01:28:12.750
and the serotonin 1A receptors

01:28:12.750 --> 01:28:16.440
are primary targets for
the drug psilocybin.

01:28:16.440 --> 01:28:17.880
So I figured there were
going to be some questions

01:28:17.880 --> 01:28:20.790
about whether or not
psychedelics help with OCD,

01:28:20.790 --> 01:28:23.040
thus far it's inconclusive.

01:28:23.040 --> 01:28:25.470
If any of you have been
part of clinical trials

01:28:25.470 --> 01:28:28.590
or have knowledge or intuition
about this relationship

01:28:28.590 --> 01:28:30.090
or potential relationship, I should say,

01:28:30.090 --> 01:28:31.920
between psilocybin and other psychedelics

01:28:31.920 --> 01:28:33.930
in OCD, please put them
in the comment section.

01:28:33.930 --> 01:28:35.973
We'd love to love to hear from you.

01:28:37.140 --> 01:28:38.640
One thing I should point out

01:28:38.640 --> 01:28:40.560
is that even though serotonin

01:28:40.560 --> 01:28:43.050
has not been directly implicated in OCD,

01:28:43.050 --> 01:28:45.720
serotonin and the general
systems of serotonin,

01:28:45.720 --> 01:28:47.550
the circuits in the brain
that carry serotonin

01:28:47.550 --> 01:28:49.980
and depend on it have been shown

01:28:49.980 --> 01:28:53.910
to impact cognitive
flexibility and inflexibility,

01:28:53.910 --> 01:28:56.100
which are kind of hallmark themes of OCD.

01:28:56.100 --> 01:28:59.130
So in animals that have
their serotonin depleted

01:28:59.130 --> 01:29:01.950
or in humans that have very
low levels of serotonin,

01:29:01.950 --> 01:29:05.880
you can see evidence of
cognitive inflexibility,

01:29:05.880 --> 01:29:08.040
challenges in tasks, switching challenges

01:29:08.040 --> 01:29:10.830
and switching the rules by
which one performs a game,

01:29:10.830 --> 01:29:14.940
challenges in any kind of
cognitive domain switching.

01:29:14.940 --> 01:29:17.340
And so that does indirectly implicate

01:29:17.340 --> 01:29:20.100
serotonin in some of the aspects of OCD.

01:29:20.100 --> 01:29:21.810
Again, when one starts to explore

01:29:21.810 --> 01:29:23.310
the different transmitter systems

01:29:23.310 --> 01:29:26.070
that have been explored in
animal models and in humans,

01:29:26.070 --> 01:29:27.810
it's a vast, vast landscape,

01:29:27.810 --> 01:29:30.900
but serotonergic drugs do seem to be

01:29:30.900 --> 01:29:32.970
the most effective drugs in treating OCD

01:29:32.970 --> 01:29:35.970
despite the fact, again, despite the fact

01:29:35.970 --> 01:29:37.230
that there's no direct evidence

01:29:37.230 --> 01:29:42.120
that serotonin systems
are the problem in OCD.

01:29:42.120 --> 01:29:45.180
If you recall the
cortico-striatal-thalamic loop

01:29:45.180 --> 01:29:48.930
that is so central to the etiology,

01:29:48.930 --> 01:29:52.500
the presence and the
patterns of symptoms in OCD,

01:29:52.500 --> 01:29:54.660
of course, serotonin is
impacting that system.

01:29:54.660 --> 01:29:57.390
Serotonin is impacting just
about every system in the brain,

01:29:57.390 --> 01:29:59.190
but there's no evidence that

01:29:59.190 --> 01:30:00.630
tinkering with serotonin levels,

01:30:00.630 --> 01:30:02.820
specifically in that network,

01:30:02.820 --> 01:30:06.540
is what's leading to
the improvements in OCD.

01:30:06.540 --> 01:30:10.260
However, if people go into a FMRI scanner

01:30:10.260 --> 01:30:11.580
and those people have OCD

01:30:11.580 --> 01:30:13.410
and they evoke the
obsessions and compulsions,

01:30:13.410 --> 01:30:17.430
you see activity in that
cortico-striatal-thalamic loop.

01:30:17.430 --> 01:30:21.900
Treatments like SSRIs that
reduce the symptoms of OCD

01:30:21.900 --> 01:30:24.600
equate to a situation where
there is less activity

01:30:24.600 --> 01:30:26.130
in that loop.

01:30:26.130 --> 01:30:29.220
And I should point out
cognitive behavioral therapy,

01:30:29.220 --> 01:30:30.900
which we have no reason to believe

01:30:30.900 --> 01:30:32.580
only taps into the serotonin system,

01:30:32.580 --> 01:30:34.800
I think it would be extreme stretch,

01:30:34.800 --> 01:30:36.636
it would be false actually to say

01:30:36.636 --> 01:30:38.070
that that cognitive
behavioral therapy taps

01:30:38.070 --> 01:30:39.630
only into the serotonin system,

01:30:39.630 --> 01:30:41.700
clearly it's going to affect
a huge number of circuits

01:30:41.700 --> 01:30:43.050
in neurochemical systems.

01:30:43.050 --> 01:30:45.660
Well, people who do
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:30:45.660 --> 01:30:47.550
and find some relief for OCD,

01:30:47.550 --> 01:30:49.080
they also show reductions

01:30:49.080 --> 01:30:51.840
in those cortico-striatal-thalamic loops.

01:30:51.840 --> 01:30:53.250
So basically we have a situation

01:30:53.250 --> 01:30:56.040
where we have a behavioral
therapy that works,

01:30:56.040 --> 01:30:57.870
in many people, not all,

01:30:57.870 --> 01:30:59.100
and we have a pretty good understanding

01:30:59.100 --> 01:31:00.120
of about why it works.

01:31:00.120 --> 01:31:01.950
It increases anxiety tolerance,

01:31:01.950 --> 01:31:06.950
and interference with pattern execution,

01:31:07.020 --> 01:31:08.820
getting people to not engage

01:31:08.820 --> 01:31:10.350
in the same sorts of behaviors

01:31:10.350 --> 01:31:12.180
that are detrimental to them.

01:31:12.180 --> 01:31:14.280
And we have drug treatments that work

01:31:14.280 --> 01:31:15.240
at least to some degree,

01:31:15.240 --> 01:31:16.500
but we don't know how they work

01:31:16.500 --> 01:31:17.850
or where they work in the brain.

01:31:17.850 --> 01:31:20.010
One of the things that really unifies

01:31:20.010 --> 01:31:23.400
the behavioral treatments
and the drug treatments

01:31:23.400 --> 01:31:25.440
is that they take some period of time.

01:31:25.440 --> 01:31:27.330
Some relief from symptoms seems to show up

01:31:27.330 --> 01:31:29.880
around four weeks and
certainly by eight weeks

01:31:29.880 --> 01:31:33.210
for both cognitive behavioral
therapy and the SSRIs,

01:31:33.210 --> 01:31:36.390
but it's really at the 10 to 12 week stage

01:31:36.390 --> 01:31:38.040
when someone's been
doing these twice a week,

01:31:38.040 --> 01:31:39.990
cognitive behavioral sessions,

01:31:39.990 --> 01:31:43.650
where they've been taking
a SSRI for 10 to 12 weeks,

01:31:43.650 --> 01:31:45.810
that the really significant reduction

01:31:45.810 --> 01:31:49.170
in OCD symptoms starts to really show up.

01:31:49.170 --> 01:31:51.150
Now, up until now, I've
been talking about the fact

01:31:51.150 --> 01:31:54.000
that people are getting
relief from these treatments,

01:31:54.000 --> 01:31:55.710
but sadly,

01:31:55.710 --> 01:31:57.300
in the case of OCD,

01:31:57.300 --> 01:32:00.540
there is a significant population

01:32:00.540 --> 01:32:03.780
that simply does not respond to CBT

01:32:03.780 --> 01:32:06.600
or to SSRIs, or to their combination,

01:32:06.600 --> 01:32:10.560
which is why psychiatrists
also explore the combination

01:32:10.560 --> 01:32:13.470
of SSRIs and neuroleptics

01:32:13.470 --> 01:32:16.170
or drugs that tap into the
so-called dopamine system

01:32:16.170 --> 01:32:17.430
or the glutamate system.

01:32:17.430 --> 01:32:20.040
These are other neurotransmitters
and neuromodulators

01:32:20.040 --> 01:32:21.810
that impact different
circuits in the brain.

01:32:21.810 --> 01:32:23.070
And just to really remind you

01:32:23.070 --> 01:32:25.170
what neurotransmitters
and neuromodulators do,

01:32:25.170 --> 01:32:28.110
because this is important
to contextualize all this,

01:32:28.110 --> 01:32:30.990
neurotransmitters are
typically involved in the

01:32:30.990 --> 01:32:32.820
rapid communication between neurons.

01:32:32.820 --> 01:32:34.830
And the two most common
neurotransmitters for that

01:32:34.830 --> 01:32:37.200
are the neurotransmitter glutamate,

01:32:37.200 --> 01:32:38.818
which we say is excitatory,

01:32:38.818 --> 01:32:40.311
meaning when it's
released into the synapse,

01:32:40.311 --> 01:32:43.849
it causes the next neuron to
be more active, or active,

01:32:43.849 --> 01:32:46.099
and GABA which is a neurotransmitter

01:32:46.099 --> 01:32:47.791
that is inhibitory,
meaning when it's released

01:32:47.791 --> 01:32:49.590
into the synapse, typically, not always,

01:32:49.590 --> 01:32:52.320
but typically, that GABA
is going to encourage

01:32:52.320 --> 01:32:54.510
the next neuron to be
less electrically active

01:32:54.510 --> 01:32:56.133
or even silence its activity.

01:32:57.330 --> 01:32:59.820
The neuromodulators, by contrast,

01:32:59.820 --> 01:33:01.980
So not neurotransmitters,
but neuromodulators

01:33:01.980 --> 01:33:03.960
like dopamine, serotonin, epinephrine,

01:33:03.960 --> 01:33:05.770
and acetylcholine and others

01:33:06.960 --> 01:33:08.490
operate a little bit differently.

01:33:08.490 --> 01:33:10.620
They tend to act a
little bit more broadly.

01:33:10.620 --> 01:33:12.330
They can act within the synapse,

01:33:12.330 --> 01:33:15.180
but they can also change
the general patterns

01:33:15.180 --> 01:33:16.410
of activity in the brain,

01:33:16.410 --> 01:33:18.270
making certain circuits more likely

01:33:18.270 --> 01:33:21.420
to be active and other circuits
less likely to be active.

01:33:21.420 --> 01:33:23.730
So when we say dopamine does X

01:33:23.730 --> 01:33:25.770
or dopamine does Y, or serotonin does X

01:33:25.770 --> 01:33:26.603
or serotonin does Y,

01:33:26.603 --> 01:33:28.140
they don't really do one thing,

01:33:28.140 --> 01:33:30.570
they change the sort of overall tonality.

01:33:30.570 --> 01:33:32.100
They make it more likely or less likely

01:33:32.100 --> 01:33:33.840
that certain circuits will be active.

01:33:33.840 --> 01:33:36.510
You can think of them as
kind of activating playlists

01:33:36.510 --> 01:33:39.540
or genres of activity in the brain,

01:33:39.540 --> 01:33:42.360
rather than being involved
in the specific communication

01:33:42.360 --> 01:33:44.220
or specific songs, if you will,

01:33:44.220 --> 01:33:47.430
in this analogy, or discussions
between particular neurons.

01:33:47.430 --> 01:33:51.540
So when we hear that
SSRIs increase serotonin

01:33:51.540 --> 01:33:53.430
and reduce the symptoms of OCD,

01:33:53.430 --> 01:33:56.490
or a neuroleptic reduces
the amount of dopamine

01:33:56.490 --> 01:33:59.790
and makes people feel calmer for instance,

01:33:59.790 --> 01:34:04.790
or can remove some stereotype,
repetitive motor behavior,

01:34:05.070 --> 01:34:06.840
which they can either generate

01:34:06.840 --> 01:34:09.450
or reduce motor behavior it turns out.

01:34:09.450 --> 01:34:11.240
So when I say that, what I'm referring to

01:34:11.240 --> 01:34:12.960
is the fact that these neuromodulators

01:34:12.960 --> 01:34:15.810
are kind of turning up the
volume on certain circuits

01:34:15.810 --> 01:34:18.540
and turning down the
volume on other circuits.

01:34:18.540 --> 01:34:22.080
I say that because if
you are going to explore

01:34:22.080 --> 01:34:25.440
drug treatments again
with a licensed physician,

01:34:25.440 --> 01:34:28.380
if you're going to explore
drug treatments for OCD,

01:34:28.380 --> 01:34:31.830
and in particular, if you
are not getting results

01:34:31.830 --> 01:34:34.440
from SSRIs, or you're not getting results

01:34:34.440 --> 01:34:35.670
from cognitive behavioral therapy

01:34:35.670 --> 01:34:37.590
or the side effect profiles of the drugs

01:34:37.590 --> 01:34:39.750
that you're taking for OCD

01:34:39.750 --> 01:34:43.110
are causing problems that
you don't want to take them,

01:34:43.110 --> 01:34:45.000
well, then it's important to understand

01:34:45.000 --> 01:34:47.130
that anytime you take one of these drugs,

01:34:47.130 --> 01:34:48.810
they're not acting specifically

01:34:48.810 --> 01:34:51.510
on the cortico-striatal-thalamic circuit.

01:34:51.510 --> 01:34:52.343
That would be wonderful.

01:34:52.343 --> 01:34:54.300
That's the future of psychiatry,

01:34:54.300 --> 01:34:57.360
but as now, when you take a drug,

01:34:57.360 --> 01:34:58.350
it acts systemically.

01:34:58.350 --> 01:35:00.600
So it's impacting serotonin in your gut.

01:35:00.600 --> 01:35:02.790
It's also impacting serotonin
in other areas of the brain,

01:35:02.790 --> 01:35:07.790
hence the effects on things
like digestion or libido or

01:35:08.100 --> 01:35:09.000
any number of different things

01:35:09.000 --> 01:35:10.890
that serotonin is involved in.

01:35:10.890 --> 01:35:14.070
Likewise, if you take a
neuroleptic like haloperidol

01:35:14.070 --> 01:35:17.580
or something that reduces
dopamine transmission,

01:35:17.580 --> 01:35:19.410
well, then it's going to
have some motor effects

01:35:19.410 --> 01:35:21.420
'cause dopamine is
involved in the generation

01:35:21.420 --> 01:35:23.700
of motor sequences and
smooth limb movement.

01:35:23.700 --> 01:35:24.810
That's why people with Parkinson's

01:35:24.810 --> 01:35:27.060
who don't have much dopamine
will get a resting tremor,

01:35:27.060 --> 01:35:28.620
have a hard time
generating smooth movement.

01:35:28.620 --> 01:35:31.080
And so the side effects
start to make sense,

01:35:31.080 --> 01:35:33.660
given the huge number of
different neural circuits

01:35:33.660 --> 01:35:36.000
that these different
neuromodulators are involved in.

01:35:36.000 --> 01:35:38.010
I don't say that to be discouraging,

01:35:38.010 --> 01:35:40.620
I say that to encourage patients

01:35:40.620 --> 01:35:43.050
and careful systematic exploration

01:35:43.050 --> 01:35:45.570
of different drug treatments for OCD

01:35:45.570 --> 01:35:49.920
always again with the careful and close

01:35:49.920 --> 01:35:52.080
guidance and oversight of a psychiatrist

01:35:52.080 --> 01:35:53.700
because psychiatrists really understand

01:35:53.700 --> 01:35:56.190
which side effect profiles make it likely

01:35:56.190 --> 01:36:00.510
that you can or cannot or will never,

01:36:00.510 --> 01:36:02.610
or maybe someday will be
able to take a given drug

01:36:02.610 --> 01:36:03.630
at a given dose.

01:36:03.630 --> 01:36:05.310
They're the ones that
really have that knowledge.

01:36:05.310 --> 01:36:06.780
This is not the sort
of thing that you want

01:36:06.780 --> 01:36:09.570
to cowboy and go try
and figure out yourself.

01:36:09.570 --> 01:36:11.670
Now, I also want to acknowledge

01:36:11.670 --> 01:36:13.530
that there are other
forms of drug treatments.

01:36:13.530 --> 01:36:15.150
We touched on psilocybin briefly,

01:36:15.150 --> 01:36:16.890
but there are other
forms of drug treatments

01:36:16.890 --> 01:36:19.260
that have been explored for OCD.

01:36:19.260 --> 01:36:21.240
Earlier, we talked a
little bit about cannabis.

01:36:21.240 --> 01:36:25.680
Why would cannabis be a
place of exploration at all?

01:36:25.680 --> 01:36:26.640
Well, first of all,

01:36:26.640 --> 01:36:29.610
a number of people try
and self medicate for OCD.

01:36:29.610 --> 01:36:31.380
There is some clinical evidence,

01:36:31.380 --> 01:36:32.760
I'm not talking about recreational use,

01:36:32.760 --> 01:36:33.780
I'm talking about clinical evidence

01:36:33.780 --> 01:36:35.640
that cannabis can reduce anxiety.

01:36:35.640 --> 01:36:37.590
Now earlier we were talking about

01:36:37.590 --> 01:36:41.070
not reducing anxiety, but
learning anxiety tolerance

01:36:41.070 --> 01:36:44.160
in order to deal with and treat OCD

01:36:44.160 --> 01:36:46.863
in the context of cognitive
behavioral therapies.

01:36:47.850 --> 01:36:49.530
That doesn't necessarily rule out cannabis

01:36:49.530 --> 01:36:52.707
as a candidate for the treatment of OCD.

01:36:52.707 --> 01:36:54.660
And in fact, this has been explored.

01:36:54.660 --> 01:36:59.250
A study from Dr. Blair Simpson
herself looked at this.

01:36:59.250 --> 01:37:00.960
This was a fairly small scale study.

01:37:00.960 --> 01:37:02.280
So first of all, I'll give you the title.

01:37:02.280 --> 01:37:03.390
And again, we'll provide a link.

01:37:03.390 --> 01:37:05.520
This is entitled, Acute
effects of cannabinoids

01:37:05.520 --> 01:37:07.620
on symptoms of
obsessive-compulsive disorder:

01:37:07.620 --> 01:37:09.450
A human laboratory study.

01:37:09.450 --> 01:37:12.120
very briefly, this was 14 adults with OCD.

01:37:12.120 --> 01:37:14.430
They had prior experience with cannabis.

01:37:14.430 --> 01:37:16.590
This was randomized, placebo-controlled.

01:37:16.590 --> 01:37:20.640
The cannabis was smoked,
they had different varietals,

01:37:20.640 --> 01:37:21.540
as they're called.

01:37:21.540 --> 01:37:22.440
They had a placebo.

01:37:22.440 --> 01:37:26.820
So this is basically a condition
in which certain subjects

01:37:26.820 --> 01:37:30.270
consumed a cigarette that had 0% THC,

01:37:30.270 --> 01:37:33.730
others had 7% THC,
other groups that is, or

01:37:35.040 --> 01:37:37.980
some had 0.4% CBD and THC.

01:37:37.980 --> 01:37:39.060
So they looked at CBD.

01:37:39.060 --> 01:37:40.953
I know a lot of people out
there are interested in CBD.

01:37:40.953 --> 01:37:42.690
This is one of the few
studies I could find

01:37:42.690 --> 01:37:45.150
where they explored different percentages

01:37:45.150 --> 01:37:47.170
of THC and CBD in these

01:37:48.150 --> 01:37:51.000
cannabis or marijuana
cigarettes basically.

01:37:51.000 --> 01:37:52.920
The total amount that they consumed,

01:37:52.920 --> 01:37:54.660
I believe, was 800 milligrams.

01:37:54.660 --> 01:37:56.730
These, again, are not suggestions.

01:37:56.730 --> 01:37:58.710
These are just simply
reporting what's in this study.

01:37:58.710 --> 01:38:00.510
You can, again, I'll provide a link.

01:38:01.650 --> 01:38:04.740
They looked at OCD symptoms, ratings.

01:38:04.740 --> 01:38:06.870
They looked at cardiovascular effects.

01:38:06.870 --> 01:38:08.550
They had a large number
of different things

01:38:08.550 --> 01:38:09.510
that they explored.

01:38:09.510 --> 01:38:11.760
And I should say this
study was done in 2020,

01:38:11.760 --> 01:38:15.090
and it was the first
placebo-controlled investigation

01:38:15.090 --> 01:38:18.120
of cannabis in adults with
obsessive-compulsive disorder.

01:38:18.120 --> 01:38:19.200
Pretty interesting.

01:38:19.200 --> 01:38:21.600
And I'm just reading from
their conclusions here.

01:38:21.600 --> 01:38:23.310
The data suggests that smoked cannabis,

01:38:23.310 --> 01:38:26.520
whether containing primarily THC or CBD,

01:38:26.520 --> 01:38:28.020
remember they looked at
different concentrations

01:38:28.020 --> 01:38:30.330
of those, has little acute impact,

01:38:30.330 --> 01:38:32.680
meaning immediate impact on OCD symptoms

01:38:33.870 --> 01:38:35.850
and yield smaller reductions in anxiety

01:38:35.850 --> 01:38:37.170
compared to placebo.

01:38:37.170 --> 01:38:40.440
So they did not see a, when
I say a positive effect,

01:38:40.440 --> 01:38:43.230
I mean a ameliorative effect,

01:38:43.230 --> 01:38:45.690
an effect in reducing symptoms of OCD

01:38:45.690 --> 01:38:47.670
from cannabis or CBD,

01:38:47.670 --> 01:38:49.740
which, it's unfortunate.

01:38:49.740 --> 01:38:51.600
I think it's unfortunate anytime
a treatment doesn't work.

01:38:51.600 --> 01:38:52.890
But nonetheless, those are the data,

01:38:52.890 --> 01:38:54.540
I'm sure there are going
to be other studies.

01:38:54.540 --> 01:38:55.710
I'm sure there are also going to be people

01:38:55.710 --> 01:38:57.570
in the YouTube comments section saying

01:38:57.570 --> 01:39:01.620
that cannabis and CBD
helps their OCD symptoms,

01:39:01.620 --> 01:39:04.050
at least I anticipate there probably will.

01:39:04.050 --> 01:39:05.160
Almost everything I say here,

01:39:05.160 --> 01:39:06.720
somebody will contradict it with something

01:39:06.720 --> 01:39:08.940
from their experience, which
I encourage, by the way.

01:39:08.940 --> 01:39:11.310
I want to hear about your
experience with certain things

01:39:11.310 --> 01:39:14.400
even if it's not from randomized
placebo-controlled studies,

01:39:14.400 --> 01:39:15.870
I still find it very interesting

01:39:15.870 --> 01:39:18.270
to know what people are doing
and what they're experiencing.

01:39:18.270 --> 01:39:20.490
I think that's one of the
better uses of social media

01:39:20.490 --> 01:39:22.650
comment sections, is to be
able to share some of that,

01:39:22.650 --> 01:39:25.260
not in an advice-giving
way or prescriptive way,

01:39:25.260 --> 01:39:27.420
but simply as a way to share and encourage

01:39:27.420 --> 01:39:29.070
different types of exploration.

01:39:29.070 --> 01:39:30.360
There are other sorts of drug treatments

01:39:30.360 --> 01:39:32.970
that are gaining popularity for OCD,

01:39:32.970 --> 01:39:34.740
at least in the research realm.

01:39:34.740 --> 01:39:38.220
One treatment that is a legal, L-E-G-A-L.

01:39:38.220 --> 01:39:39.180
Sometimes when I say legal,

01:39:39.180 --> 01:39:41.610
sometimes people think I say
illegal, but that is legal,

01:39:41.610 --> 01:39:45.870
at least by prescription in
the United States, is ketamine.

01:39:45.870 --> 01:39:49.380
The actions of ketamine
are somewhat complex

01:39:49.380 --> 01:39:50.550
although we know, for instance,

01:39:50.550 --> 01:39:52.710
that ketamine acts on
the glutamate system,

01:39:52.710 --> 01:39:56.430
it tends to disrupt the transmission

01:39:56.430 --> 01:39:58.530
or the relationship, I should say,

01:39:58.530 --> 01:40:01.230
between glutamate, not glutamine,

01:40:01.230 --> 01:40:04.290
not the amino acid, but
glutamate, the neurotransmitter,

01:40:04.290 --> 01:40:07.890
and the so-called NMDA, the
N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor,

01:40:07.890 --> 01:40:10.590
which is a receptor that's very special

01:40:10.590 --> 01:40:12.720
in the nervous system
because when glutamate binds

01:40:12.720 --> 01:40:16.230
to the NMDA receptor, it tends to

01:40:16.230 --> 01:40:18.420
offer the opportunity for
that particular synapse

01:40:18.420 --> 01:40:20.640
to get stronger, so-called neuroplasticity

01:40:20.640 --> 01:40:24.420
and ketamine is a,
essentially, an antagonist,

01:40:24.420 --> 01:40:26.400
although it works through
a complicated mechanism,

01:40:26.400 --> 01:40:28.440
it tends to block that

01:40:28.440 --> 01:40:31.470
binding of glutamate to the NMDA receptor

01:40:31.470 --> 01:40:32.670
or the effectiveness of that.

01:40:32.670 --> 01:40:35.670
Ketamine therapy is now
being used quite extensively

01:40:35.670 --> 01:40:37.530
for the treatment of
trauma and for depression.

01:40:37.530 --> 01:40:39.540
It leads to a dissociative state.

01:40:39.540 --> 01:40:41.800
It's a so-called dissociative analgesic

01:40:42.954 --> 01:40:44.670
in the variety of ways
in which that happens.

01:40:44.670 --> 01:40:45.960
We did an episode on depression.

01:40:45.960 --> 01:40:48.870
We're going to do another entire
episode all about ketamine

01:40:48.870 --> 01:40:51.240
describing the networks that
ketamine impacts, et cetera.

01:40:51.240 --> 01:40:54.060
Ketamine therapies are
being explored for OCD.

01:40:54.060 --> 01:40:57.660
As of now, the data
look somewhat promising,

01:40:57.660 --> 01:41:00.540
but there's still a lot more
work that needs to be done.

01:41:00.540 --> 01:41:02.640
My read of the data are that

01:41:02.640 --> 01:41:05.370
the more extensive clinical trials

01:41:05.370 --> 01:41:06.420
have not happened yet.

01:41:06.420 --> 01:41:09.300
The smaller studies that have happened

01:41:09.300 --> 01:41:11.400
revealed that some
patients do get some relief

01:41:11.400 --> 01:41:13.500
from ketamine therapy for OCD,

01:41:13.500 --> 01:41:17.400
but there was nothing
overwhelmingly pointing to the fact

01:41:17.400 --> 01:41:21.570
that ketamine is a magic
bullet for OCD treatment.

01:41:21.570 --> 01:41:24.420
So cannabis, CBD, at least now,

01:41:24.420 --> 01:41:26.580
even though it's one smaller study,

01:41:26.580 --> 01:41:29.160
there's no real evidence that it can

01:41:29.160 --> 01:41:30.843
alleviate OCD symptoms.

01:41:31.740 --> 01:41:33.270
If there are new studies published soon,

01:41:33.270 --> 01:41:34.620
I'll be sure to update you.

01:41:34.620 --> 01:41:35.730
And if you see those studies,

01:41:35.730 --> 01:41:36.563
please send them to me.

01:41:36.563 --> 01:41:38.910
Ketamine therapy, the jury is still out,

01:41:38.910 --> 01:41:40.860
psilocybin, The jury is still out.

01:41:40.860 --> 01:41:42.450
These are early days.

01:41:42.450 --> 01:41:45.420
Another treatment that's
becoming somewhat common,

01:41:45.420 --> 01:41:47.700
or at least people are
commonly excited about

01:41:47.700 --> 01:41:50.010
is transcranial magnetic stimulation.

01:41:50.010 --> 01:41:52.320
So this is the use of a magnetic coil.

01:41:52.320 --> 01:41:53.910
This is completely noninvasive,

01:41:53.910 --> 01:41:57.030
placed on one portion of the skull,

01:41:57.030 --> 01:41:59.920
and one can direct magnetic

01:42:00.780 --> 01:42:03.150
energy toward particular
areas of the brain

01:42:03.150 --> 01:42:04.950
to either suppress, or nowadays,

01:42:04.950 --> 01:42:07.440
you can also activate
particular brain regions.

01:42:07.440 --> 01:42:08.490
There are some interesting data

01:42:08.490 --> 01:42:10.470
showing that if TMS is applied

01:42:10.470 --> 01:42:11.940
to areas of the brain involved

01:42:11.940 --> 01:42:14.010
in the generation of motor action,

01:42:14.010 --> 01:42:15.750
so the so-called motor areas,

01:42:15.750 --> 01:42:18.600
or supplementary motor
areas as they're called,

01:42:18.600 --> 01:42:23.190
while people think about
or have intrusive thoughts,

01:42:23.190 --> 01:42:25.650
we know that the TMS coil can interrupt

01:42:25.650 --> 01:42:29.130
the motor behaviors, the
compulsive behaviors,

01:42:29.130 --> 01:42:31.600
and at least in a small cohort of studies

01:42:32.536 --> 01:42:35.130
and a small number of
patients within those studies,

01:42:35.130 --> 01:42:37.380
this has been shown to be effective,

01:42:37.380 --> 01:42:39.450
not just while the coil
is on the head, of course,

01:42:39.450 --> 01:42:41.970
but after the study has been performed

01:42:41.970 --> 01:42:43.500
or the treatment's been performed

01:42:43.500 --> 01:42:47.850
in reducing OCD symptoms
by disrupting the tendency

01:42:47.850 --> 01:42:51.570
for the compulsive behavior
to be so automatic.

01:42:51.570 --> 01:42:54.810
One of the key features of
obsessive-compulsive disorder

01:42:54.810 --> 01:42:58.650
is that, especially if it's
been around for a while,

01:42:58.650 --> 01:43:00.360
the person's been dealing
with it for a while,

01:43:00.360 --> 01:43:03.420
there isn't a pattern in
which the person thinks,

01:43:03.420 --> 01:43:05.970
oh, I have this contamination fear,

01:43:05.970 --> 01:43:08.370
or I need symmetry, or
I'm kind of obsessed

01:43:08.370 --> 01:43:09.720
to count to the number seven.

01:43:09.720 --> 01:43:11.433
And then they pause and they go,

01:43:11.433 --> 01:43:12.900
ooh, and then they do it.

01:43:12.900 --> 01:43:15.660
No, typically there's a very close

01:43:15.660 --> 01:43:18.570
pairing of the obsession
and the compulsion in time

01:43:18.570 --> 01:43:19.950
so that somebody's
walking down the street,

01:43:19.950 --> 01:43:21.180
thinking one, two, three,
four, five, six, seven,

01:43:21.180 --> 01:43:23.310
one, two, three, four,
five, six seven, seven...

01:43:23.310 --> 01:43:24.870
and then they're doing this

01:43:24.870 --> 01:43:27.150
in such rapid succession

01:43:27.150 --> 01:43:29.430
because the obsessions
are coming up so quickly.

01:43:29.430 --> 01:43:31.680
Thoughts can be generated very quickly.

01:43:31.680 --> 01:43:32.943
And then they're
generating the compulsions

01:43:32.943 --> 01:43:34.320
as a way to beat down

01:43:34.320 --> 01:43:36.690
or to try and suppress that anxiety

01:43:36.690 --> 01:43:38.100
and then it comes right back up again

01:43:38.100 --> 01:43:40.410
at even stronger as I described earlier.

01:43:40.410 --> 01:43:42.480
So transcranial magnetic stimulation

01:43:42.480 --> 01:43:45.093
seems to intervene in these
various fast processes.

01:43:46.110 --> 01:43:48.300
Right now, I don't think it's fair to say

01:43:48.300 --> 01:43:50.820
that TMS is a magic bullet either.

01:43:50.820 --> 01:43:52.470
I think there's a lot
of excitement about TMS

01:43:52.470 --> 01:43:53.940
and in particular,

01:43:53.940 --> 01:43:55.890
I really want to nail this point home,

01:43:55.890 --> 01:43:58.590
in particular, there's excitement about

01:43:58.590 --> 01:44:01.800
the combination of TMS
with drug treatments,

01:44:01.800 --> 01:44:05.880
or the combination of TMS with
cognitive behavioral therapy.

01:44:05.880 --> 01:44:07.470
And this is a really important point,

01:44:07.470 --> 01:44:08.700
not just for sake of discussion

01:44:08.700 --> 01:44:10.230
about obsessive-compulsive disorder,

01:44:10.230 --> 01:44:14.760
but also depression, ADHD, schizophrenia,

01:44:14.760 --> 01:44:17.760
any number of different
psychiatric challenges

01:44:17.760 --> 01:44:21.480
and disorders in most cases
are going to respond best

01:44:21.480 --> 01:44:23.280
to a combination of behavioral treatment

01:44:23.280 --> 01:44:25.410
that's ongoing that
occurs in the laboratory

01:44:25.410 --> 01:44:26.243
and clinical setting,

01:44:26.243 --> 01:44:28.740
but also in the home setting
where there's homework,

01:44:28.740 --> 01:44:29.943
maybe even home visits.

01:44:31.170 --> 01:44:33.690
Drug treatments, often, not always,

01:44:33.690 --> 01:44:35.220
are a terrific augment to

01:44:35.220 --> 01:44:36.620
those cognitive behavioral therapies

01:44:36.620 --> 01:44:38.040
or other behavioral therapies.

01:44:38.040 --> 01:44:40.050
And then now we are living in the age

01:44:40.050 --> 01:44:41.310
of brain-machine interface.

01:44:41.310 --> 01:44:43.020
You have companies like Neuralink

01:44:43.020 --> 01:44:45.630
that I think it's fair
to say are going to enter

01:44:45.630 --> 01:44:48.210
the brain machine-interface world first

01:44:48.210 --> 01:44:51.960
through the treatment
of certain syndromes,

01:44:51.960 --> 01:44:53.940
movement syndromes or
psychiatric syndromes

01:44:53.940 --> 01:44:56.160
probably before they
start putting electrodes

01:44:56.160 --> 01:44:58.650
into the brain to
stimulate enhanced memory

01:44:58.650 --> 01:45:00.060
or enhanced cognition, who knows,

01:45:00.060 --> 01:45:01.830
I don't know exactly what they're doing

01:45:01.830 --> 01:45:02.937
behind the walls of Neuralink.

01:45:02.937 --> 01:45:04.560
But I have to imagine, in fact,

01:45:04.560 --> 01:45:06.810
I would wager maybe not both arms,

01:45:06.810 --> 01:45:08.340
but I'll wager my left arm

01:45:08.340 --> 01:45:13.340
that the first set of
FDA approved technologies

01:45:13.380 --> 01:45:15.960
to come out of companies like Neuralink

01:45:15.960 --> 01:45:17.400
are going to be those for
the treatment of things

01:45:17.400 --> 01:45:19.200
like Parkinson's and movement disorders

01:45:19.200 --> 01:45:21.630
and cognitive disorders, rather than,

01:45:21.630 --> 01:45:24.030
shall we say, kind of recreational

01:45:24.030 --> 01:45:26.100
cognitive enhancement
or things of that sort.

01:45:26.100 --> 01:45:28.530
So transcranial magnetic
stimulation is noninvasive.

01:45:28.530 --> 01:45:30.900
It doesn't involve going
down below the skull,

01:45:30.900 --> 01:45:33.690
can have some effect,
but most laboratories

01:45:33.690 --> 01:45:36.210
that I'm aware of at
Stanford and elsewhere

01:45:36.210 --> 01:45:38.400
that are exploring TMS for things like OCD

01:45:38.400 --> 01:45:40.530
and other types of psychiatric

01:45:40.530 --> 01:45:43.830
challenges are using TMS in combination

01:45:43.830 --> 01:45:45.660
with drug therapies, are using,

01:45:45.660 --> 01:45:46.980
in some cases, for instance,

01:45:46.980 --> 01:45:49.710
a laboratory at Stanford, hope
to get 'em on the podcast,

01:45:49.710 --> 01:45:51.780
a psychiatrist, Nolan Williams,

01:45:51.780 --> 01:45:54.180
is exploring TMS in combination with

01:45:54.180 --> 01:45:56.370
psychedelic therapies, not
necessarily at the same time,

01:45:56.370 --> 01:45:57.890
but nonetheless combining them

01:45:57.890 --> 01:46:00.510
or exploring how they
impact brain circuitry.

01:46:00.510 --> 01:46:03.240
So if you have OCD, should
you run out and get TMS,

01:46:03.240 --> 01:46:04.920
or should you try ketamine therapy,

01:46:04.920 --> 01:46:06.420
of course, with a licensed physician?

01:46:06.420 --> 01:46:08.490
I think it's too early to say yes.

01:46:08.490 --> 01:46:11.400
I think the answer is
we need to wait and see.

01:46:11.400 --> 01:46:13.527
I think cognitive behavioral
therapy, the SSRIs,

01:46:13.527 --> 01:46:16.470
and some other drug
treatments like neuroleptics

01:46:16.470 --> 01:46:19.410
combined with SSRIs and
cognitive behavioral therapy

01:46:19.410 --> 01:46:22.530
are where the real bulk of the data are.

01:46:22.530 --> 01:46:25.170
I want to make one additional
point about cannabis CBD

01:46:25.170 --> 01:46:27.840
as it relates to
obsessive-compulsive disorder.

01:46:27.840 --> 01:46:30.210
To me, it's not at all surprising

01:46:30.210 --> 01:46:34.350
that cannabis CBD did not
improve symptoms of OCD.

01:46:34.350 --> 01:46:36.690
Because in my discussion
with Dr. Paul Conti

01:46:36.690 --> 01:46:38.310
a few weeks ago,

01:46:38.310 --> 01:46:39.480
and I should mention, Dr. Conti

01:46:39.480 --> 01:46:41.673
is indeed a medical
doctor, a psychiatrist,

01:46:43.080 --> 01:46:45.270
we were talking about
cannabis and its various uses,

01:46:45.270 --> 01:46:48.270
because it does have some
clinical applications.

01:46:48.270 --> 01:46:50.820
And he mentioned that
one of the main effects

01:46:50.820 --> 01:46:54.630
of cannabis is to tighten focus

01:46:54.630 --> 01:46:57.660
and to enhance concentration on

01:46:57.660 --> 01:47:00.450
and thoughts about one particular thing.

01:47:00.450 --> 01:47:03.120
And in some cases that can
be clinically beneficial,

01:47:03.120 --> 01:47:06.120
and in other cases that can
be clinically detrimental.

01:47:06.120 --> 01:47:11.120
If you accept the idea that
cannabis increases focus,

01:47:11.880 --> 01:47:15.870
and you think about OCD
and the networks involved,

01:47:15.870 --> 01:47:18.180
and you think about the
anxiety and the relationship

01:47:18.180 --> 01:47:20.880
between the obsession and compulsion,

01:47:20.880 --> 01:47:22.680
well, then it shouldn't
come as any surprise

01:47:22.680 --> 01:47:26.130
that cannabis did not
improve the symptoms of OCD

01:47:26.130 --> 01:47:29.040
because if anything,
it would increase focus

01:47:29.040 --> 01:47:30.870
on the obsessions and the compulsions.

01:47:30.870 --> 01:47:31.950
Now that's not what they observed.

01:47:31.950 --> 01:47:33.960
They did not see an exacerbation

01:47:33.960 --> 01:47:37.380
or a worsening of the
symptoms of OCD with cannabis,

01:47:37.380 --> 01:47:39.120
at least that's not my read of the data,

01:47:39.120 --> 01:47:41.100
but they did not see an improvement

01:47:41.100 --> 01:47:44.130
in OCD symptoms with cannabis or CBD.

01:47:44.130 --> 01:47:46.110
And to me, that's not surprising

01:47:46.110 --> 01:47:49.560
given that cannabis CBD
seems to increase focus.

01:47:49.560 --> 01:47:51.630
Next, I'd like to talk
about some of the research

01:47:51.630 --> 01:47:55.110
on and the roles of hormones in OCD,

01:47:55.110 --> 01:47:55.943
because it turns out to be

01:47:55.943 --> 01:47:57.870
a very interesting relationship there.

01:47:57.870 --> 01:47:59.580
But before I do, I want
to point out something

01:47:59.580 --> 01:48:02.490
that I realize I probably
should have said earlier,

01:48:02.490 --> 01:48:05.610
which is one of the key
things for someone with OCD

01:48:05.610 --> 01:48:08.190
to come to understand if
they're going to experience

01:48:08.190 --> 01:48:09.810
any relief of their symptoms,

01:48:09.810 --> 01:48:10.980
whether or not they're
doing drug treatments

01:48:10.980 --> 01:48:12.990
or behavioral treatments or otherwise,

01:48:12.990 --> 01:48:17.613
is that thoughts are
not as bad as actions.

01:48:18.720 --> 01:48:20.760
Thoughts are not as bad as actions.

01:48:20.760 --> 01:48:24.090
One of the kind of rules
that people with OCD

01:48:24.090 --> 01:48:26.160
seem to adopt for themselves

01:48:26.160 --> 01:48:28.530
is that thoughts are really,

01:48:28.530 --> 01:48:30.330
truly the equivalent of actions.

01:48:30.330 --> 01:48:32.580
So they'll have an intrusive thought and,

01:48:32.580 --> 01:48:33.990
we haven't spent too
much time on this today,

01:48:33.990 --> 01:48:35.970
but earlier I touched on the fact

01:48:35.970 --> 01:48:38.280
that some of the intrusive
thoughts that people have

01:48:38.280 --> 01:48:40.470
in OCD are really disturbing.

01:48:40.470 --> 01:48:42.180
They can be really gross,

01:48:42.180 --> 01:48:44.100
or at least gross to that person.

01:48:44.100 --> 01:48:49.050
They can evoke imagery that
is toxic or infectious,

01:48:49.050 --> 01:48:51.630
or is highly sexualized in a way

01:48:51.630 --> 01:48:54.480
that is disturbing to
them, it can be very taboo.

01:48:54.480 --> 01:48:56.130
This is not uncommon

01:48:56.130 --> 01:48:57.780
when you start talking to people with OCD

01:48:57.780 --> 01:48:59.490
and you start pulling on the thread.

01:48:59.490 --> 01:49:01.470
Again, this would be a psychiatrist

01:49:01.470 --> 01:49:03.630
who was trained to ask the right questions

01:49:03.630 --> 01:49:05.910
and gain the comfort
and trust of a patient.

01:49:05.910 --> 01:49:06.743
And they start to reveal that

01:49:06.743 --> 01:49:09.630
these thoughts are really
intrusive and kind of disturbing,

01:49:09.630 --> 01:49:11.220
which is why they feel so compelled

01:49:11.220 --> 01:49:13.380
to try and suppress them with behaviors.

01:49:13.380 --> 01:49:17.280
One of the powerful elements
of treatment for OCD

01:49:17.280 --> 01:49:20.490
is to really support the patient

01:49:20.490 --> 01:49:24.210
and make them realize that
thoughts are just thoughts

01:49:24.210 --> 01:49:26.550
and that everyone has disturbing thoughts.

01:49:26.550 --> 01:49:28.380
And that oftentimes
those disturbing thoughts

01:49:28.380 --> 01:49:31.440
arise at the most
inconvenient, and sometimes,

01:49:31.440 --> 01:49:35.010
what seems like the most
inappropriate circumstances.

01:49:35.010 --> 01:49:37.350
And this relates to a
whole larger discussion

01:49:37.350 --> 01:49:39.780
that we could have about what are thoughts

01:49:39.780 --> 01:49:40.620
and why do they surface,

01:49:40.620 --> 01:49:43.530
and how come when you stand
at the edge of a bridge,

01:49:43.530 --> 01:49:45.210
even if you do not want to jump off,

01:49:45.210 --> 01:49:46.680
you think about jumping off.

01:49:46.680 --> 01:49:48.450
And this has to do with the fact

01:49:48.450 --> 01:49:50.850
that your nervous system,
as a prediction machine,

01:49:50.850 --> 01:49:52.710
is oftentimes testing possibilities.

01:49:52.710 --> 01:49:56.050
And sometimes that testing
goes way off into the

01:49:56.910 --> 01:50:00.540
Netherlands of the thought patterns

01:50:00.540 --> 01:50:03.360
and emotional patterns that
we all have inside of us.

01:50:03.360 --> 01:50:05.850
The big difference between
a thought and an action

01:50:05.850 --> 01:50:08.870
is that, of course, the nervous system is,

01:50:08.870 --> 01:50:11.100
in one case, not translating

01:50:11.100 --> 01:50:14.043
those patterns of thinking
into motor sequences.

01:50:14.970 --> 01:50:18.660
That nerdy way of saying
thoughts aren't actions,

01:50:18.660 --> 01:50:19.493
believe it or not,

01:50:19.493 --> 01:50:21.480
can be helpful for people if they really

01:50:21.480 --> 01:50:23.730
think about that and
use it as an opportunity

01:50:23.730 --> 01:50:27.360
to realize that, first of
all, they're not crazy.

01:50:27.360 --> 01:50:30.300
They're not thinking
and feeling this stuff

01:50:30.300 --> 01:50:32.190
because they're bad or evil.

01:50:32.190 --> 01:50:34.200
And of course, sometimes
this can cross over

01:50:34.200 --> 01:50:35.820
with other elements of life

01:50:35.820 --> 01:50:37.800
where we place moral judgment on people

01:50:37.800 --> 01:50:38.633
for certain behaviors.

01:50:38.633 --> 01:50:40.680
I think that's part of a
healthy society, of course,

01:50:40.680 --> 01:50:42.630
that's why we have laws and punishments

01:50:42.630 --> 01:50:45.690
and rewards for that matter
for certain types of behaviors.

01:50:45.690 --> 01:50:47.730
But this idea that thoughts
are not as bad as actions

01:50:47.730 --> 01:50:49.770
and that thoughts can be tolerated

01:50:49.770 --> 01:50:51.660
and the anxiety around thoughts

01:50:51.660 --> 01:50:54.060
can be tolerated and
over time can diminish,

01:50:54.060 --> 01:50:56.310
that's a very powerful hallmark theme

01:50:56.310 --> 01:50:57.450
of the treatment of OCD

01:50:57.450 --> 01:50:59.940
so I'd be remiss if I didn't mention it.

01:50:59.940 --> 01:51:01.920
Thoughts are not actions.

01:51:01.920 --> 01:51:04.530
Actions can harm us, they
can harm other people,

01:51:04.530 --> 01:51:08.520
they can soak up enormous amounts of time.

01:51:08.520 --> 01:51:10.920
Thoughts can soak up
enormous amounts of time.

01:51:10.920 --> 01:51:11.940
They can be very troubling.

01:51:11.940 --> 01:51:13.200
They can be very detrimental.

01:51:13.200 --> 01:51:15.810
We of course want to be sensitive to that,

01:51:15.810 --> 01:51:18.810
but when it really comes down to it,

01:51:18.810 --> 01:51:22.830
the first step in treatment
for OCD is this realization

01:51:22.830 --> 01:51:24.510
where the approach to the realization

01:51:24.510 --> 01:51:27.300
that thoughts are not as bad as actions.

01:51:27.300 --> 01:51:29.550
So what about hormones in OCD?

01:51:29.550 --> 01:51:30.750
Well, this has been explored,

01:51:30.750 --> 01:51:33.660
albeit not as extensively
as I would've liked to find,

01:51:33.660 --> 01:51:35.340
but when I went into the literature,

01:51:35.340 --> 01:51:38.280
I found one particularly
interesting study,

01:51:38.280 --> 01:51:40.230
entitled, Neurosteroid Levels in Patients

01:51:40.230 --> 01:51:42.180
with Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder.

01:51:42.180 --> 01:51:43.013
First author, Erbay

01:51:43.013 --> 01:51:45.377
And as always, we'll
provide a link to the study.

01:51:47.040 --> 01:51:49.560
The objective of this
study was to explore serum

01:51:49.560 --> 01:51:53.730
within blood, neurosteroid
levels in people with OCD.

01:51:53.730 --> 01:51:54.563
Why?

01:51:54.563 --> 01:51:55.860
Well, because of the relationship

01:51:55.860 --> 01:51:57.360
between OCD and anxiety

01:51:57.360 --> 01:51:59.670
and the fact that in
stress-related disorders

01:51:59.670 --> 01:52:01.263
such as anxiety and depression,

01:52:02.280 --> 01:52:04.140
the hormones have been
extensively explored,

01:52:04.140 --> 01:52:06.780
but not so much in OCD,
at least until this study.

01:52:06.780 --> 01:52:09.090
So they compared serum levels of a number

01:52:09.090 --> 01:52:11.580
of different hormones,
progesterone, pregnenolone,

01:52:11.580 --> 01:52:15.360
DHEA, cortisol, and testosterone.

01:52:15.360 --> 01:52:17.297
This was done in 30 patients with OCD

01:52:17.297 --> 01:52:19.620
and 30 healthy controls.

01:52:19.620 --> 01:52:22.620
So it's not a huge study,
but it's enough to draw

01:52:22.620 --> 01:52:24.000
some pretty nice conclusions.

01:52:24.000 --> 01:52:26.910
These subjects were 18 to 49 years old,

01:52:26.910 --> 01:52:30.330
and the controls were age and sex matched

01:52:30.330 --> 01:52:31.200
healthy volunteers.

01:52:31.200 --> 01:52:32.850
Again, no OCD.

01:52:32.850 --> 01:52:34.260
What was the basic
takeaway from the study?

01:52:34.260 --> 01:52:36.900
The basic takeaway from the
study was that in females

01:52:36.900 --> 01:52:39.630
with OCD, there was evidence

01:52:39.630 --> 01:52:43.650
for significantly elevated
cortisol and DHEA.

01:52:43.650 --> 01:52:45.330
Now that's interesting because cortisol

01:52:45.330 --> 01:52:48.570
is well known to be associated
with the stress system.

01:52:48.570 --> 01:52:50.670
Although every day, should mention,

01:52:50.670 --> 01:52:54.300
we all, male or female,
everybody experiences

01:52:54.300 --> 01:52:56.790
an increase in cortisol
shortly after awakening.

01:52:56.790 --> 01:52:59.040
That's a healthy increase in cortisol.

01:52:59.040 --> 01:53:03.150
Late shift, I mean, late in
the day peaks in cortisol

01:53:03.150 --> 01:53:04.950
where a shift in that cortisol peak

01:53:04.950 --> 01:53:09.330
to later in the day is a known correlate

01:53:09.330 --> 01:53:11.583
of depression and anxiety disorders.

01:53:12.660 --> 01:53:14.157
So the fact that cortisol is elevated

01:53:14.157 --> 01:53:18.420
and DHEA are elevated in female patients

01:53:18.420 --> 01:53:20.200
with OCD suggests that

01:53:21.750 --> 01:53:24.480
cortisol is either reflective of or causal

01:53:24.480 --> 01:53:26.820
for the increase in anxiety.

01:53:26.820 --> 01:53:28.920
We don't know the
direction of that effect.

01:53:28.920 --> 01:53:31.680
Now in male patients with OCD,

01:53:31.680 --> 01:53:33.810
there was evidence for increased cortisol.

01:53:33.810 --> 01:53:36.860
Again, not surprising given the
role of anxiety in cortisol,

01:53:36.860 --> 01:53:39.930
or I should say, given the
role of cortisol in anxiety

01:53:39.930 --> 01:53:42.870
and the increasing anxiety seen in OCD,

01:53:42.870 --> 01:53:45.990
but there are also significant
reductions in testosterone,

01:53:45.990 --> 01:53:48.180
which should also not surprise us

01:53:48.180 --> 01:53:52.020
because cortisol and
testosterone more or less compete

01:53:52.020 --> 01:53:54.990
in some fashion for their own production,

01:53:54.990 --> 01:53:57.900
both are derived from
the molecule cholesterol.

01:53:57.900 --> 01:53:59.670
And there are certain biochemical pathways

01:53:59.670 --> 01:54:01.860
that can either direct
that cholesterol molecule

01:54:01.860 --> 01:54:05.220
toward cortisol synthesis
or testosterone synthesis,

01:54:05.220 --> 01:54:06.540
but not both.

01:54:06.540 --> 01:54:07.373
So they compete.

01:54:07.373 --> 01:54:09.060
So when cortisol goes up in general,

01:54:09.060 --> 01:54:10.200
not always, but in general,

01:54:10.200 --> 01:54:12.570
testosterone goes down and vice versa.

01:54:12.570 --> 01:54:14.310
If you want to learn more
about the relationship

01:54:14.310 --> 01:54:15.810
between cortisol and testosterone,

01:54:15.810 --> 01:54:17.850
and there are even some tools
to try and optimize those

01:54:17.850 --> 01:54:20.400
ratios in both males and females,

01:54:20.400 --> 01:54:21.690
you can find that in our episode

01:54:21.690 --> 01:54:23.730
on optimizing testosterone and estrogen,

01:54:23.730 --> 01:54:25.800
that's at hubermanlab.com.

01:54:25.800 --> 01:54:28.440
Now, I would say the
most interesting aspect

01:54:28.440 --> 01:54:31.800
of this study is not
that DHEA and cortisol

01:54:31.800 --> 01:54:33.200
are elevated in females with OCD

01:54:33.200 --> 01:54:35.670
or that cortisol and testosterone

01:54:35.670 --> 01:54:36.630
have this opposite effect,

01:54:36.630 --> 01:54:40.110
cortisol up and testosterone
down in males with OCD,

01:54:40.110 --> 01:54:43.080
but rather the relationship
between all of those,

01:54:43.080 --> 01:54:45.573
DHEA, cortisol, and testosterone.

01:54:46.770 --> 01:54:49.350
In terms of GABA, GABA again

01:54:49.350 --> 01:54:51.570
being this inhibitory neurotransmitter

01:54:51.570 --> 01:54:56.040
that tends to quiet
certain neuronal pathways,

01:54:56.040 --> 01:54:58.830
it does different things
at different synapses,

01:54:58.830 --> 01:55:01.170
but in general, the more
GABA that's present,

01:55:01.170 --> 01:55:02.790
the more inhibition that's present,

01:55:02.790 --> 01:55:05.520
and therefore the more
suppression of neural activity.

01:55:05.520 --> 01:55:10.520
And DHEA is known to
be a potent antagonist

01:55:11.430 --> 01:55:13.470
of the GABA system.

01:55:13.470 --> 01:55:18.470
So here we have elevated DHEA in females.

01:55:18.540 --> 01:55:21.090
And I should also
mention that testosterone

01:55:21.090 --> 01:55:23.820
is also known to tap into the GABA system.

01:55:23.820 --> 01:55:26.130
Typically, when testosterone is elevated,

01:55:26.130 --> 01:55:29.760
GABA transmission, at
least is slightly elevated.

01:55:29.760 --> 01:55:32.280
So here we have a situation
in which the pattern

01:55:32.280 --> 01:55:36.510
of hormones in females and males with OCD

01:55:36.510 --> 01:55:40.770
are different from those
in people without OCD

01:55:40.770 --> 01:55:43.860
such that GABA transmission is altered

01:55:43.860 --> 01:55:47.640
and the net effect would be
an overall reduction in GABA.

01:55:47.640 --> 01:55:51.390
Now GABA, as an inhibitory
neurotransmitter,

01:55:51.390 --> 01:55:53.670
and broadly speaking is associated

01:55:53.670 --> 01:55:55.770
with lower levels of anxiety,

01:55:55.770 --> 01:55:57.750
and it tends to create balance

01:55:57.750 --> 01:55:59.430
within various neural circuits.

01:55:59.430 --> 01:56:00.630
Now, that's a very broad statement,

01:56:00.630 --> 01:56:03.540
but we know for instance, in epilepsy,

01:56:03.540 --> 01:56:05.010
that GABA levels are reduced

01:56:05.010 --> 01:56:06.990
and therefore you get runaway excitation

01:56:06.990 --> 01:56:08.340
of certain circuits in the brain,

01:56:08.340 --> 01:56:10.740
and therefore seizures, either petite mal,

01:56:10.740 --> 01:56:13.350
mini seizures, or grand
mal, massive seizures,

01:56:13.350 --> 01:56:15.690
or even drop seizures where
people completely collapse

01:56:15.690 --> 01:56:16.830
to the floor in seizure.

01:56:16.830 --> 01:56:18.180
You may have seen this before.

01:56:18.180 --> 01:56:19.950
I certainly have, it's very dramatic

01:56:19.950 --> 01:56:23.010
and it actually is quite
debilitating for people

01:56:23.010 --> 01:56:24.660
because obviously they don't
know when these seizures

01:56:24.660 --> 01:56:27.030
are coming on most often, and then

01:56:27.030 --> 01:56:29.910
they can fall into a stove
or while driving, et cetera.

01:56:29.910 --> 01:56:33.180
So the situation with OCD is one in which,

01:56:33.180 --> 01:56:36.393
for whatever reason, we don't
know the direction of effect.

01:56:37.530 --> 01:56:39.510
Certain hormones are elevated in females

01:56:39.510 --> 01:56:41.280
and certain hormones are elevated in males

01:56:41.280 --> 01:56:45.570
and those hormones differ
between males and females,

01:56:45.570 --> 01:56:48.090
and yet they both funnel into a system

01:56:48.090 --> 01:56:51.210
where GABAergic or GABA
transmission in the brain

01:56:51.210 --> 01:56:53.460
is reduced because of this ability

01:56:53.460 --> 01:56:54.900
for those particular hormones

01:56:54.900 --> 01:56:57.210
to be antagonists to GABA,

01:56:57.210 --> 01:57:01.770
and as a consequence, there's
likely to be overall levels

01:57:01.770 --> 01:57:05.400
of increased excitation in
certain networks in the brain

01:57:05.400 --> 01:57:07.972
and that brings us back to this

01:57:07.972 --> 01:57:09.840
cortico-striatal-thalamic loop,

01:57:09.840 --> 01:57:12.390
this repetitive loop
that seems to reinforce,

01:57:12.390 --> 01:57:16.560
we can say reinforces
obsession, leads to anxiety,

01:57:16.560 --> 01:57:20.400
leads to compulsion, leads to
transient relief of anxiety,

01:57:20.400 --> 01:57:24.090
but then increase in anxiety,
increased obsession, anxiety,

01:57:24.090 --> 01:57:26.280
compulsion, anxiety,
compulsion, anxiety, compulsion,

01:57:26.280 --> 01:57:27.780
and so on and so forth.

01:57:27.780 --> 01:57:32.070
So I have not found
studies that have explored

01:57:32.070 --> 01:57:34.380
adjusting testosterone levels

01:57:34.380 --> 01:57:36.090
through exogenous administration,

01:57:36.090 --> 01:57:37.650
cream or injection or otherwise,

01:57:37.650 --> 01:57:42.240
or that have focused on
reducing DHEA in females.

01:57:42.240 --> 01:57:43.830
If anyone is aware of such studies,

01:57:43.830 --> 01:57:46.860
please put them in the
comment section on YouTube

01:57:46.860 --> 01:57:47.850
or send them to us.

01:57:47.850 --> 01:57:50.640
We have a contact site on the
website at hubermanlab.com,

01:57:50.640 --> 01:57:53.280
but the comment section
on YouTube would be best.

01:57:53.280 --> 01:57:56.220
But because we know that
hormones impact neuromodulators

01:57:56.220 --> 01:57:58.050
and neurotransmitters,
as I just described,

01:57:58.050 --> 01:58:00.120
and that those neuromodulators
and neurotransmitters

01:58:00.120 --> 01:58:02.220
play an intimate role in the generation

01:58:02.220 --> 01:58:05.340
and the treatment of things like OCD,

01:58:05.340 --> 01:58:08.340
it stands to reason that manipulations

01:58:08.340 --> 01:58:10.560
of those hormone systems, however subtle

01:58:10.560 --> 01:58:12.900
or dramatic might, I want to highlight,

01:58:12.900 --> 01:58:16.230
might prove useful in
adjusting the symptoms of OCD

01:58:16.230 --> 01:58:19.230
and I hope that this is
an area that researchers

01:58:19.230 --> 01:58:20.940
are going to pursue in
the very near future

01:58:20.940 --> 01:58:24.570
because many of the
treatments for reducing DHEA

01:58:24.570 --> 01:58:27.090
or increasing testosterone
or reducing cortisol

01:58:27.090 --> 01:58:29.910
have already made it through FDA approval.

01:58:29.910 --> 01:58:32.400
They're out there, they're
readily prescribed.

01:58:32.400 --> 01:58:34.020
Many of them are already in generic form

01:58:34.020 --> 01:58:36.420
which means that the
patents have already lapsed

01:58:36.420 --> 01:58:39.210
on the first versions of those drugs.

01:58:39.210 --> 01:58:42.030
So when they're available as
generic drugs, very often,

01:58:42.030 --> 01:58:44.700
they're available at
significantly lower cost.

01:58:44.700 --> 01:58:46.050
There's a whole discussion we had there

01:58:46.050 --> 01:58:49.470
about patent laws and prescription drugs.

01:58:49.470 --> 01:58:52.140
But because these drugs
are largely available

01:58:52.140 --> 01:58:55.740
in prescription yet generic form,

01:58:55.740 --> 01:58:57.060
I think there's a great opportunity

01:58:57.060 --> 01:59:00.600
to explore how hormones,
not just cortisol,

01:59:00.600 --> 01:59:01.770
testosterone, and DHA,

01:59:01.770 --> 01:59:04.140
but the huge category of hormones

01:59:04.140 --> 01:59:06.510
might impact the symptoms of OCD,

01:59:06.510 --> 01:59:09.210
especially since many
of the symptoms of OCD

01:59:09.210 --> 01:59:11.250
show up right around the time of puberty.

01:59:11.250 --> 01:59:12.690
We haven't talked a lot
about childhood OCD,

01:59:12.690 --> 01:59:14.700
'cause we're going to do an entire series

01:59:14.700 --> 01:59:18.160
on childhood psychiatric
disorders and challenges but

01:59:19.260 --> 01:59:22.380
many children develop OCD early as young

01:59:22.380 --> 01:59:24.270
as three or four, believe it or not,

01:59:24.270 --> 01:59:26.040
or even 6 or 7 and 10

01:59:26.040 --> 01:59:28.860
and in adolescence, and
certainly around puberty.

01:59:28.860 --> 01:59:30.540
and in young adulthood.

01:59:30.540 --> 01:59:32.580
It is rare, although it does happen,

01:59:32.580 --> 01:59:34.980
that people will develop
OCD very late in life

01:59:34.980 --> 01:59:37.740
around 40 or older, just
kind of spontaneously.

01:59:37.740 --> 01:59:39.600
Most often when you look
at their clinical history,

01:59:39.600 --> 01:59:41.580
you find that either they were hiding it

01:59:41.580 --> 01:59:43.320
or is being suppressed in some way,

01:59:43.320 --> 01:59:46.140
or if it does spontaneously
show up late in life

01:59:46.140 --> 01:59:49.083
like mid-thirties or in one's forties,

01:59:50.070 --> 01:59:52.530
typically there's a
traumatic brain injury,

01:59:52.530 --> 01:59:55.260
could be due to stroke or
physical injury to the head

01:59:55.260 --> 01:59:57.150
or something of that sort.

01:59:57.150 --> 01:59:58.740
Nonetheless,

01:59:58.740 --> 02:00:00.630
there is a interesting correlation

02:00:00.630 --> 02:00:03.330
between the onset of puberty
in certain forms of OCD.

02:00:03.330 --> 02:00:05.400
There's certain forms of, or I should say,

02:00:05.400 --> 02:00:06.840
there's certain aspects of menopause

02:00:06.840 --> 02:00:08.160
that can relate to OCD.

02:00:08.160 --> 02:00:10.260
You can find all these
things in the literature.

02:00:10.260 --> 02:00:13.170
All this to say that hormones
impact neurotransmitters

02:00:13.170 --> 02:00:15.060
and neuromodulators, which clearly impact

02:00:15.060 --> 02:00:17.220
the kinds of circuits
that are involved in OCD

02:00:17.220 --> 02:00:19.140
and it makes sense that,

02:00:19.140 --> 02:00:21.630
and I would hope that there
would be an exploration

02:00:21.630 --> 02:00:23.400
of how these hormones impact OCD

02:00:23.400 --> 02:00:24.750
in the not too distant future.

02:00:24.750 --> 02:00:26.580
Now there is an extensive
literature exploring

02:00:26.580 --> 02:00:27.960
how testosterone therapy,

02:00:27.960 --> 02:00:32.430
both in males and
females, can be effective

02:00:32.430 --> 02:00:34.440
in some cases in the treatment

02:00:34.440 --> 02:00:36.570
of anxiety-related disorders,

02:00:36.570 --> 02:00:40.140
but not, at least to my
knowledge, in OCD in particular.

02:00:40.140 --> 02:00:42.330
So this whole area of the use

02:00:42.330 --> 02:00:44.280
of testosterone and estrogen therapies,

02:00:44.280 --> 02:00:46.290
DHEA, cortisol suppression,

02:00:46.290 --> 02:00:49.890
or maybe even enhancement
for the treatment of OCD

02:00:49.890 --> 02:00:52.350
is essentially a big black box

02:00:52.350 --> 02:00:56.550
that very soon, I believe, will be lit.

02:00:56.550 --> 02:00:58.920
I realize that a number of
listeners of this podcast

02:00:58.920 --> 02:01:02.580
are probably interested in the non-typical

02:01:02.580 --> 02:01:04.713
or holistic treatments for OCD.

02:01:05.550 --> 02:01:07.920
Dr. Blair Simpson's lab
has at least one study

02:01:07.920 --> 02:01:11.400
exploring the role of
mindfulness meditation

02:01:11.400 --> 02:01:14.310
for the treatment of OCD.

02:01:14.310 --> 02:01:17.340
There, the data are a
little bit complicated

02:01:17.340 --> 02:01:19.060
and I should mention that

02:01:19.980 --> 02:01:22.680
good things are happening, at
least in the United States,

02:01:22.680 --> 02:01:24.060
probably elsewhere as well,

02:01:24.060 --> 02:01:26.430
but good things are happening in terms of

02:01:26.430 --> 02:01:29.100
the exploration of things like meditation

02:01:29.100 --> 02:01:31.140
and other, let's call them non-traditional

02:01:31.140 --> 02:01:34.680
or holistic forms of treatment
for psychiatric disorders

02:01:34.680 --> 02:01:37.230
because of the division
of complimentary health

02:01:37.230 --> 02:01:38.370
that's now been launched

02:01:38.370 --> 02:01:39.630
by the National Institutes of Health.

02:01:39.630 --> 02:01:42.690
So, whereas before
people would think about

02:01:42.690 --> 02:01:44.970
meditation or yoga nidra,

02:01:44.970 --> 02:01:48.690
or even CBD supplementation
for that matter,

02:01:48.690 --> 02:01:50.910
as kind of fringe maybe, or kind of woo

02:01:50.910 --> 02:01:53.670
or non-traditional at the very least,

02:01:53.670 --> 02:01:55.680
the National Institutes of
Health in the United States

02:01:55.680 --> 02:01:59.250
has now devoted an entire division,

02:01:59.250 --> 02:02:02.400
an entire Institute,
purely for the exploration

02:02:02.400 --> 02:02:04.740
of things like breathing
practices, meditation, et cetera.

02:02:04.740 --> 02:02:06.150
So there's a cancer institute,

02:02:06.150 --> 02:02:07.860
there's a hearing and deafness institute,

02:02:07.860 --> 02:02:09.570
there's a vision institute,

02:02:09.570 --> 02:02:11.580
and now there's this
complimentary health Institute,

02:02:11.580 --> 02:02:14.610
which I think is a wonderful addition

02:02:14.610 --> 02:02:18.000
to the more traditional
aspects of medicine.

02:02:18.000 --> 02:02:21.090
I think no possible useful treatment

02:02:21.090 --> 02:02:23.700
should be overlooked or
unresearched in my opinion,

02:02:23.700 --> 02:02:25.830
provided that can be done safely.

02:02:25.830 --> 02:02:28.650
And as I mentioned,
Dr. Blair Simpson's lab

02:02:28.650 --> 02:02:31.347
has looked at the role
of mindfulness meditation

02:02:31.347 --> 02:02:33.330
and the treatment of OCD.

02:02:33.330 --> 02:02:36.120
Now we should all keep in mind,

02:02:36.120 --> 02:02:38.400
no pun intended, that most of the data

02:02:38.400 --> 02:02:41.970
on mindfulness meditation
shows that it increases

02:02:41.970 --> 02:02:43.860
the ability to focus.

02:02:43.860 --> 02:02:46.440
Now, this brings us back to a
kind of repeating theme today,

02:02:46.440 --> 02:02:48.060
which is that increased focus

02:02:48.060 --> 02:02:50.770
may not be the best thing
for somebody with OCD

02:02:51.870 --> 02:02:55.200
because it might increase
focus on the obsession

02:02:55.200 --> 02:02:57.030
and/or compulsion.

02:02:57.030 --> 02:02:59.700
Turns out that mindfulness
meditation can be useful

02:02:59.700 --> 02:03:00.990
in the treatment of OCD,

02:03:00.990 --> 02:03:04.050
but mainly by way of how it impacts

02:03:04.050 --> 02:03:07.110
the focus on and the ability

02:03:07.110 --> 02:03:10.050
to engage in cognitive
behavioral therapies.

02:03:10.050 --> 02:03:13.290
So it's very unlikely, at
least by my read of the data,

02:03:13.290 --> 02:03:15.540
to be a direct effect of meditation

02:03:15.540 --> 02:03:16.680
on relieving the symptoms,

02:03:16.680 --> 02:03:19.530
rather it seems that
meditation is increasing focus

02:03:19.530 --> 02:03:23.040
on things like cognitive
behavioral therapy homework

02:03:23.040 --> 02:03:24.720
and to not focus on other things

02:03:24.720 --> 02:03:28.110
and therefore indirectly
improving the symptoms of OCD.

02:03:28.110 --> 02:03:29.940
Now somewhat surprisingly, at least to me,

02:03:29.940 --> 02:03:32.640
there have also been a fairly
large number of studies

02:03:32.640 --> 02:03:36.480
exploring how nutraceuticals,
as they're sometimes called,

02:03:36.480 --> 02:03:38.640
supplements that are
available over the counter

02:03:38.640 --> 02:03:41.730
can impact the treatment of
obsessive-compulsive disorder.

02:03:41.730 --> 02:03:45.000
Now there's such an extensive
number of different compounds

02:03:45.000 --> 02:03:47.310
and supplements that
fall under the category

02:03:47.310 --> 02:03:49.290
of nutraceuticals and
that have been explored

02:03:49.290 --> 02:03:51.120
in the treatment of OCD
that I'd like to point you

02:03:51.120 --> 02:03:53.670
to a review that is entitled,

02:03:53.670 --> 02:03:54.960
Nutraceuticals in the treatment

02:03:54.960 --> 02:03:57.690
of obsessive-compulsive
disorder: a review,

02:03:57.690 --> 02:04:00.510
excuse me, of mechanistic
and clinical evidence.

02:04:00.510 --> 02:04:04.500
So it's published in 2011,
so it's over 10-years-old.

02:04:04.500 --> 02:04:05.953
And so by now,

02:04:05.953 --> 02:04:08.460
I have to imagine that
there are an enormous number

02:04:08.460 --> 02:04:11.430
of additional substances
that could be explored,

02:04:11.430 --> 02:04:14.400
but there are just one or two
here that I want to focus on.

02:04:14.400 --> 02:04:15.233
Here in this review,

02:04:15.233 --> 02:04:18.990
they describe effects
of 5-HTP and tryptophan,

02:04:18.990 --> 02:04:21.060
so things that are in
the serotonin pathway,

02:04:21.060 --> 02:04:21.893
which would make sense

02:04:21.893 --> 02:04:23.910
given what we know about the SSRIs

02:04:23.910 --> 02:04:26.040
that people would explore
how different supplements

02:04:26.040 --> 02:04:28.290
that increase serotonergic transmission

02:04:28.290 --> 02:04:30.360
might impact OCD.

02:04:30.360 --> 02:04:33.000
What you find is that they
do have significant effects

02:04:33.000 --> 02:04:36.720
in improving or reducing
the symptoms of OCD

02:04:36.720 --> 02:04:39.450
in somewhat similar way
to some of the SSRIs.

02:04:39.450 --> 02:04:41.313
But you of course have to be careful.

02:04:42.540 --> 02:04:44.550
Anything that's going to tap into a given

02:04:44.550 --> 02:04:46.860
neurochemical system to the same degree

02:04:46.860 --> 02:04:50.160
may very likely have the
same sorts of side effects

02:04:50.160 --> 02:04:51.660
that a prescription drug would.

02:04:51.660 --> 02:04:53.240
One compound that I like to focus on

02:04:53.240 --> 02:04:54.750
in a little more depth, however,

02:04:54.750 --> 02:04:57.450
because it's exciting
and interesting to me

02:04:57.450 --> 02:04:59.280
is inositol.

02:04:59.280 --> 02:05:01.410
Inositol is a compound that
we are going to talk about

02:05:01.410 --> 02:05:03.750
in several future podcasts, because,

02:05:03.750 --> 02:05:05.070
well, first of all,

02:05:05.070 --> 02:05:07.440
it seems that it can
have impressive effects

02:05:07.440 --> 02:05:09.030
on reducing anxiety.

02:05:09.030 --> 02:05:10.590
It also can have pretty impressive effects

02:05:10.590 --> 02:05:13.380
in improving fertility
and particular in women

02:05:13.380 --> 02:05:15.870
with polycystic ovarian syndrome.

02:05:15.870 --> 02:05:18.570
And here I'm referring
specifically to myo-inositol

02:05:18.570 --> 02:05:20.160
because it comes in several forms.

02:05:20.160 --> 02:05:23.790
And it does appear that
900 milligrams of inositol

02:05:23.790 --> 02:05:26.970
can improve sleep and can reduce anxiety

02:05:26.970 --> 02:05:30.240
perhaps when taken at that
dosage or higher dosages.

02:05:30.240 --> 02:05:33.270
I will just confess, first
of all, I don't have OCD,

02:05:33.270 --> 02:05:35.520
although I will also confess
that when I was a child,

02:05:35.520 --> 02:05:37.200
I had a transient tick.

02:05:37.200 --> 02:05:39.120
I've talked about this on podcast before.

02:05:39.120 --> 02:05:40.140
It was a grunting tick.

02:05:40.140 --> 02:05:41.490
So when I was about six or seven,

02:05:41.490 --> 02:05:44.760
I recall a trip to
Washington DC with my family,

02:05:44.760 --> 02:05:48.690
where I was feeling a strong

02:05:48.690 --> 02:05:51.510
desire or need even as I recall,

02:05:51.510 --> 02:05:53.790
to grunt in order to clear something

02:05:53.790 --> 02:05:56.190
in my throat, but I didn't
have anything in my throat.

02:05:56.190 --> 02:05:58.110
I didn't have a cold
or any postnasal drip,

02:05:58.110 --> 02:06:00.120
it was really just the feeling
that I needed to do that,

02:06:00.120 --> 02:06:02.370
to release some sort of tension.

02:06:02.370 --> 02:06:05.100
And I remember my dad at the time

02:06:05.100 --> 02:06:06.240
telling me don't do that.

02:06:06.240 --> 02:06:07.920
Don't do that, it's not good to grunt

02:06:07.920 --> 02:06:08.753
or something like that.

02:06:08.753 --> 02:06:11.160
I think he saw that it was a
kind of compulsive behavior.

02:06:11.160 --> 02:06:12.900
And so I would actually
hide in the back seat

02:06:12.900 --> 02:06:15.330
of the rental car and do
it, or I'd hide in my room.

02:06:15.330 --> 02:06:17.040
Fortunately for me, it was transient,

02:06:17.040 --> 02:06:19.710
I think about six months or
a year later, it disappeared.

02:06:19.710 --> 02:06:22.800
Although I did notice, actually
an ex-girlfriend of mine

02:06:22.800 --> 02:06:24.540
point out that when I get very tired

02:06:24.540 --> 02:06:26.070
and I've been working very long hours,

02:06:26.070 --> 02:06:28.620
sometimes that grunting
tick will reappear.

02:06:28.620 --> 02:06:29.460
What does that mean?

02:06:29.460 --> 02:06:30.360
Do I have Tourette's?

02:06:30.360 --> 02:06:31.560
I don't know, maybe.

02:06:31.560 --> 02:06:32.790
I was never diagnosed with Tourette's.

02:06:32.790 --> 02:06:35.460
Do I have OCD? Maybe.

02:06:35.460 --> 02:06:36.780
I certainly could be accused

02:06:36.780 --> 02:06:39.360
of having obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder,

02:06:39.360 --> 02:06:42.420
which we'll talk about
still in a few minutes.

02:06:42.420 --> 02:06:45.240
But the point here is that many children

02:06:45.240 --> 02:06:48.690
transiently express ticks
or low level Tourette's

02:06:48.690 --> 02:06:50.700
or OCD, and again, transiently

02:06:50.700 --> 02:06:52.680
and it disappears over time.

02:06:52.680 --> 02:06:54.840
So inositol has been explored

02:06:54.840 --> 02:06:56.280
in a bunch of different contexts,

02:06:56.280 --> 02:06:58.923
including for ticks in OCD, et cetera.

02:06:59.820 --> 02:07:03.330
Going back to inositol
and its current use,

02:07:03.330 --> 02:07:04.380
or I should say my current use,

02:07:04.380 --> 02:07:07.260
I've been taking 900
milligrams of inositol

02:07:07.260 --> 02:07:10.710
as in addition to my
existing toolkit for sleep,

02:07:10.710 --> 02:07:13.290
which I've talked about
many times on this podcast

02:07:13.290 --> 02:07:15.030
and other podcasts, consists

02:07:15.030 --> 02:07:18.300
of magnesium threonate,
apigenin, and theanine.

02:07:18.300 --> 02:07:19.710
If you want to know more about that kit,

02:07:19.710 --> 02:07:21.880
you can go to our newsletter,
Neural Network Newsletter

02:07:21.880 --> 02:07:23.610
at hubermanlab.com.

02:07:23.610 --> 02:07:24.840
The toolkit for sleep is there.

02:07:24.840 --> 02:07:26.520
You don't even have to
sign up for the newsletter

02:07:26.520 --> 02:07:27.930
but it'll give you a flavor
of the sorts of things

02:07:27.930 --> 02:07:29.070
that are in the newsletter.

02:07:29.070 --> 02:07:33.000
In any case, I've been experimenting a bit

02:07:33.000 --> 02:07:35.550
with taking 900 milligrams of myo-inositol

02:07:35.550 --> 02:07:37.650
either alone or combination
with that sleep kit.

02:07:37.650 --> 02:07:39.090
And I must say the sleep I've been getting

02:07:39.090 --> 02:07:42.420
on inositol is extremely deep

02:07:42.420 --> 02:07:44.490
and does seem to lead to enhanced levels

02:07:44.490 --> 02:07:46.050
of focus and alertness during the day.

02:07:46.050 --> 02:07:46.950
And perhaps you're noticing that

02:07:46.950 --> 02:07:47.850
'cause I'm talking more quickly

02:07:47.850 --> 02:07:49.470
on this podcast than in previous podcast.

02:07:49.470 --> 02:07:50.303
No, I'm just kidding.

02:07:50.303 --> 02:07:51.420
I don't think the two things relate

02:07:51.420 --> 02:07:53.400
in any kind of causal way.

02:07:53.400 --> 02:07:56.430
The point here is that inositol is known

02:07:56.430 --> 02:07:59.400
to be pretty effective
in reducing anxiety,

02:07:59.400 --> 02:08:01.380
but when taken at very high dosages.

02:08:01.380 --> 02:08:03.150
Can it do the same at low dosages?

02:08:03.150 --> 02:08:03.983
We don't know.

02:08:03.983 --> 02:08:06.630
I would consider 900
milligrams a low dose.

02:08:06.630 --> 02:08:08.790
Most of this, given the fact

02:08:08.790 --> 02:08:11.820
that most of the studies of inositol

02:08:11.820 --> 02:08:13.440
have explored very high dosages,

02:08:13.440 --> 02:08:16.380
like even 10 or 12 grams per day,

02:08:16.380 --> 02:08:18.780
which I must say seems exceedingly high

02:08:18.780 --> 02:08:20.520
and they do report that
some of the subjects

02:08:20.520 --> 02:08:22.770
in those experiments actually stop taking

02:08:22.770 --> 02:08:25.020
the inositol because of gastric discomfort

02:08:25.020 --> 02:08:27.570
or gastric distress as it's called.

02:08:27.570 --> 02:08:30.123
So I've reported my results with sleep

02:08:30.123 --> 02:08:32.220
in a kind of anecdotal way.

02:08:32.220 --> 02:08:33.570
They certainly aren't
peer-reviewed studies

02:08:33.570 --> 02:08:35.670
that I described about my own experience

02:08:35.670 --> 02:08:36.900
in an anecdotal way.

02:08:36.900 --> 02:08:39.310
But nonetheless, it's been explored that

02:08:40.320 --> 02:08:42.750
things like glycine, which is another,

02:08:42.750 --> 02:08:43.830
which is an amino acid,

02:08:43.830 --> 02:08:45.810
which also acts as an
inhibitory neurotransmitter

02:08:45.810 --> 02:08:47.640
in the brain, taken at very high dosages,

02:08:47.640 --> 02:08:50.820
60 grams per day, that is a absolutely

02:08:50.820 --> 02:08:52.860
astonishingly high amount of glycine.

02:08:52.860 --> 02:08:55.110
I would not recommend
taking that much glycine

02:08:55.110 --> 02:08:56.400
unless you're part of a study

02:08:56.400 --> 02:08:58.980
where they tell you to
and you know it's safe.

02:08:58.980 --> 02:09:01.980
18 grams, excuse me, of an inositol,

02:09:01.980 --> 02:09:04.740
these are very, very high
dosages used in these studies.

02:09:04.740 --> 02:09:06.960
Nonetheless, there's some interesting data

02:09:06.960 --> 02:09:09.520
about inositol leading to

02:09:10.410 --> 02:09:13.620
some alleviation of OCD symptoms

02:09:13.620 --> 02:09:16.560
or partial alleviation of OCD symptoms

02:09:16.560 --> 02:09:20.400
in as little as two weeks after initiating

02:09:20.400 --> 02:09:21.780
the supplement protocol.

02:09:21.780 --> 02:09:24.030
So I think there's a great
future for these nutraceuticals,

02:09:24.030 --> 02:09:26.070
meaning I think more
systematic exploration

02:09:26.070 --> 02:09:27.390
in particular of lower dosages

02:09:27.390 --> 02:09:29.850
in the context of OCD treatment.

02:09:29.850 --> 02:09:33.780
And as we saw before for the SSRIs

02:09:33.780 --> 02:09:35.610
and other prescription drug treatments,

02:09:35.610 --> 02:09:37.710
I think there really
needs to be an exploration

02:09:37.710 --> 02:09:39.660
of these nutraceuticals in combination

02:09:39.660 --> 02:09:40.740
with behavioral therapies.

02:09:40.740 --> 02:09:42.510
And who knows, maybe with
brain machine interface

02:09:42.510 --> 02:09:45.240
like cranial magnetic stimulation as well.

02:09:45.240 --> 02:09:47.160
Now way back at the
beginning of the episode,

02:09:47.160 --> 02:09:50.430
I alluded to the fact
that OCD is one thing,

02:09:50.430 --> 02:09:51.720
obsessive-compulsive disorder,

02:09:51.720 --> 02:09:54.090
and it's truly a disorder
and it's truly debilitating

02:09:54.090 --> 02:09:55.653
and it's extremely common,

02:09:56.580 --> 02:09:58.020
and then there's this other thing

02:09:58.020 --> 02:10:00.420
called obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder,

02:10:00.420 --> 02:10:01.950
which is distinct from that

02:10:01.950 --> 02:10:05.040
does not have the intrusive component

02:10:05.040 --> 02:10:06.630
so people don't feel overwhelmed

02:10:06.630 --> 02:10:09.660
or overtaken by these thoughts,

02:10:09.660 --> 02:10:12.510
rather, they find that the obsessions

02:10:12.510 --> 02:10:15.848
can sometimes serve them
or they even welcome them.

02:10:15.848 --> 02:10:17.700
And I think many of us
know people like this,

02:10:17.700 --> 02:10:20.160
I perhaps even could be
accused or who knows,

02:10:20.160 --> 02:10:21.570
maybe have been accused of having

02:10:21.570 --> 02:10:25.050
an obsessive-compulsive
personality at times.

02:10:25.050 --> 02:10:26.250
Why do I draw this distinction?

02:10:26.250 --> 02:10:30.030
Well, first of all, we've come
to a point in human history,

02:10:30.030 --> 02:10:32.610
I think in large part
because of social media

02:10:32.610 --> 02:10:33.450
but also in large part

02:10:33.450 --> 02:10:36.300
because there are a number
of discussions being held

02:10:36.300 --> 02:10:40.500
about mental health that have
brought terms like trauma,

02:10:40.500 --> 02:10:43.440
depression, OCD, et cetera,

02:10:43.440 --> 02:10:45.930
into the common vernacular

02:10:45.930 --> 02:10:48.840
so that people will
say, ah, you're so OCD,

02:10:48.840 --> 02:10:50.960
or someone will say I
was traumatized by that,

02:10:50.960 --> 02:10:53.070
or I was traumatized by this.

02:10:53.070 --> 02:10:54.480
We should be very careful, right?

02:10:54.480 --> 02:10:56.790
I'm certainly not the word police,

02:10:56.790 --> 02:10:59.670
but we should be very careful in the use

02:10:59.670 --> 02:11:01.590
of certain types of language,

02:11:01.590 --> 02:11:05.190
especially language that
has real psychiatric

02:11:05.190 --> 02:11:06.820
and psychological definitions

02:11:07.800 --> 02:11:09.780
because it can really draw us off course

02:11:09.780 --> 02:11:11.940
in providing relief for
some of these syndromes.

02:11:11.940 --> 02:11:13.053
For instance,

02:11:14.040 --> 02:11:16.530
the word trauma is thrown
around left and right nowadays.

02:11:16.530 --> 02:11:18.690
I was traumatized by this,
or that caused trauma,

02:11:18.690 --> 02:11:20.550
you're giving me trauma.

02:11:20.550 --> 02:11:23.160
Listen, I realize that many people

02:11:23.160 --> 02:11:24.900
are traumatized by certain events

02:11:24.900 --> 02:11:26.640
including things that are said to them,

02:11:26.640 --> 02:11:27.990
I absolutely acknowledge that,

02:11:27.990 --> 02:11:31.080
hence our episodes on
trauma and trauma treatment,

02:11:31.080 --> 02:11:32.970
several of them, in fact.

02:11:32.970 --> 02:11:35.940
Dr. Conti, Dr. David Spiegel,

02:11:35.940 --> 02:11:38.190
and then dedicated solo episodes

02:11:38.190 --> 02:11:41.670
with just me blabbing about
trauma and trauma treatment.

02:11:41.670 --> 02:11:44.943
But as Dr. Conti so
appropriately pointed out,

02:11:45.930 --> 02:11:48.780
trauma is really something that changes

02:11:48.780 --> 02:11:51.900
our neural circuitry and
therefore our thoughts

02:11:51.900 --> 02:11:54.180
and our behaviors in a very persistent way

02:11:54.180 --> 02:11:55.530
that is detrimental to us.

02:11:55.530 --> 02:11:57.630
Not every bad event is traumatizing,

02:11:57.630 --> 02:11:59.880
not everything that we
dislike or even that we hate

02:11:59.880 --> 02:12:02.070
or that feels terrible
to us is traumatizing.

02:12:02.070 --> 02:12:04.890
For something to reach
the level of trauma,

02:12:04.890 --> 02:12:07.710
it really needs to change
our neural circuitry

02:12:07.710 --> 02:12:09.660
and therefore our
thoughts and our behaviors

02:12:09.660 --> 02:12:12.600
in a persistent way that
is maladaptive for us.

02:12:12.600 --> 02:12:17.190
Similarly, just calling
someone obsessive is one thing,

02:12:17.190 --> 02:12:20.100
saying that someone has
OCD or assuming one has OCD

02:12:20.100 --> 02:12:23.010
simply because they have a
personality or a phenotype,

02:12:23.010 --> 02:12:25.290
as we say, where they need
things in perfect order,

02:12:25.290 --> 02:12:27.090
like I find myself correcting these pens

02:12:27.090 --> 02:12:29.490
making sure that the caps are facing

02:12:29.490 --> 02:12:32.640
in the same direction
for instance right now,

02:12:32.640 --> 02:12:35.280
that is not the same as OCD.

02:12:35.280 --> 02:12:37.200
If, for instance, I
can tolerate these pens

02:12:37.200 --> 02:12:38.460
being at different orientation

02:12:38.460 --> 02:12:40.230
or even throw the cap on
the floor or something,

02:12:40.230 --> 02:12:42.060
it doesn't create a lot of anxiety for me.

02:12:42.060 --> 02:12:44.190
I confess, I agree it's a
little bit in the moment,

02:12:44.190 --> 02:12:45.990
but then I can forget
about it and move on.

02:12:45.990 --> 02:12:47.730
That's one of the key distinctions

02:12:47.730 --> 02:12:50.640
between obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder

02:12:50.640 --> 02:12:53.070
and obsessive-compulsive disorder

02:12:53.070 --> 02:12:54.870
in its strictest form.

02:12:54.870 --> 02:12:56.940
Now, once one hears that OCD

02:12:56.940 --> 02:12:58.290
is different than obsessive-compulsive

02:12:58.290 --> 02:13:01.080
personality disorder
because of this difference

02:13:01.080 --> 02:13:03.720
in how intrusive the thoughts are or not,

02:13:03.720 --> 02:13:06.780
then that's useful, but
it really doesn't tell us

02:13:06.780 --> 02:13:09.420
anything about what is
happening mechanistically

02:13:09.420 --> 02:13:11.550
in one situation or another.

02:13:11.550 --> 02:13:14.070
Fortunately, there are
beautiful data again

02:13:14.070 --> 02:13:15.750
from Dr. Blair Simpson's lab.

02:13:15.750 --> 02:13:17.970
And you can tell based
on the number of studies

02:13:17.970 --> 02:13:19.770
that I've referred to from her laboratory,

02:13:19.770 --> 02:13:22.920
she's truly one of the
luminaries in this field,

02:13:22.920 --> 02:13:26.790
that there really are some
fundamental wiring differences

02:13:26.790 --> 02:13:29.580
and behavioral differences
and psychological differences

02:13:29.580 --> 02:13:32.430
between people who have
obsessive-compulsive disorder

02:13:32.430 --> 02:13:34.230
and those who have obsessive-compulsive

02:13:34.230 --> 02:13:36.450
personality disorder.

02:13:36.450 --> 02:13:39.990
So this is a study, first
author, Pinto, P-I-N-T-O

02:13:39.990 --> 02:13:42.930
entitled, Capacity to
delay reward differentiates

02:13:42.930 --> 02:13:44.280
obsessive-compulsive disorder

02:13:44.280 --> 02:13:47.280
and obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder.

02:13:47.280 --> 02:13:50.070
And the methods in this
study were to take 25 people

02:13:50.070 --> 02:13:53.790
with OCD and 25 people
with obsessive-compulsive

02:13:53.790 --> 02:13:56.340
personality disorder and 25 people

02:13:56.340 --> 02:13:58.890
who have both, because it
is possible to have both

02:13:58.890 --> 02:13:59.940
and that's important to point out,

02:13:59.940 --> 02:14:02.040
and 25 so-called healthy controls,

02:14:02.040 --> 02:14:03.720
people that don't have
obsessive-compulsive

02:14:03.720 --> 02:14:07.200
personality disorder or
obsessive-compulsive disorder.

02:14:07.200 --> 02:14:08.760
They take clinical assessments

02:14:08.760 --> 02:14:11.220
and then they took a number of tests

02:14:11.220 --> 02:14:15.720
that probed their ability
to defer gratification,

02:14:15.720 --> 02:14:17.070
something called, in the laboratory,

02:14:17.070 --> 02:14:18.600
we call it delayed discounting.

02:14:18.600 --> 02:14:22.230
So their ability to defer gratification

02:14:22.230 --> 02:14:24.780
through a task where they
can either accept reward

02:14:24.780 --> 02:14:26.430
right away or accept reward later.

02:14:26.430 --> 02:14:28.890
Some of you may have heard
of the two marshmallow task.

02:14:28.890 --> 02:14:31.620
This is based on a study
that was performed years ago

02:14:31.620 --> 02:14:33.750
on young children at
Stanford and elsewhere

02:14:33.750 --> 02:14:36.180
where they take young
children into a room,

02:14:36.180 --> 02:14:39.180
they offer them a marshmallow,

02:14:39.180 --> 02:14:41.280
kids like marshmallows generally,

02:14:41.280 --> 02:14:44.280
and you say, you can eat
the marshmallow right now

02:14:44.280 --> 02:14:46.440
or you can wait some period of time,

02:14:46.440 --> 02:14:49.380
and if you are able to wait
and not eat the marshmallow,

02:14:49.380 --> 02:14:50.970
you can have two marshmallows.

02:14:50.970 --> 02:14:53.190
And in general, children
want two marshmallows

02:14:53.190 --> 02:14:54.690
more than they want one marshmallow.

02:14:54.690 --> 02:14:56.370
So really what you're probing is

02:14:56.370 --> 02:14:59.040
their ability to access
delayed gratification.

02:14:59.040 --> 02:15:01.560
And they're very entertaining,

02:15:01.560 --> 02:15:06.560
even truly amusing videos
of this on the internet.

02:15:06.810 --> 02:15:09.480
So if you just do two
marshmallow task video

02:15:09.480 --> 02:15:10.313
and you go into YouTube,

02:15:10.313 --> 02:15:12.270
what you'll find is that
the children will use

02:15:12.270 --> 02:15:16.140
all sorts of strategies
to delay gratification.

02:15:16.140 --> 02:15:18.750
Some of the kids will
cover the marshmallow.

02:15:18.750 --> 02:15:20.400
Others will talk to the
marshmallow and say,

02:15:20.400 --> 02:15:21.690
I know you're not that delicious.

02:15:21.690 --> 02:15:23.430
You look delicious, but
no, you're not delicious.

02:15:23.430 --> 02:15:24.530
They'll engage with the marshmallow

02:15:24.530 --> 02:15:25.770
in all sorts of cute ways.

02:15:25.770 --> 02:15:27.960
They'll turn around and try
to, you know, avoidance,

02:15:27.960 --> 02:15:30.990
which actually speaks to a
whole category of behaviors

02:15:30.990 --> 02:15:32.610
that people with OCD also use.

02:15:32.610 --> 02:15:34.230
I'm not saying these kids had OCD,

02:15:34.230 --> 02:15:38.670
but avoidance behaviors are
very much a component of OCD.

02:15:38.670 --> 02:15:40.440
People really trying to avoid the thing

02:15:40.440 --> 02:15:43.440
that evokes the obsession.

02:15:43.440 --> 02:15:46.440
Well some kids are able
to delay gratification,

02:15:46.440 --> 02:15:48.690
some aren't and it's
debatable as to whether or not

02:15:48.690 --> 02:15:50.520
the kids that are able
to delay gratification

02:15:50.520 --> 02:15:52.380
go on to have more
successful lives or not.

02:15:52.380 --> 02:15:54.180
Initially, that was the
conclusion of those studies.

02:15:54.180 --> 02:15:55.410
There's still a lot of debate about it,

02:15:55.410 --> 02:15:57.870
we'll bring an expert on to
give us the final conclusion

02:15:57.870 --> 02:15:59.700
on this 'cause there is one
and it's very interesting

02:15:59.700 --> 02:16:01.050
and not intuitive.

02:16:01.050 --> 02:16:03.600
Nonetheless, adults are also faced

02:16:03.600 --> 02:16:05.880
with decisions every day, all day

02:16:05.880 --> 02:16:08.880
as to whether or not they
can delay gratification.

02:16:08.880 --> 02:16:13.410
And this study used a, not
a two marshmallow task,

02:16:13.410 --> 02:16:16.230
but a game that involved rewards

02:16:16.230 --> 02:16:17.670
where people could delay in order

02:16:17.670 --> 02:16:19.320
to get greater rewards later.

02:16:19.320 --> 02:16:20.670
What is the conclusion?

02:16:20.670 --> 02:16:23.040
Well, first of all, obsessive-compulsive

02:16:23.040 --> 02:16:26.040
and obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder subjects

02:16:26.040 --> 02:16:30.720
both showed impairments in
their psychosocial functioning

02:16:30.720 --> 02:16:31.860
and quality of life.

02:16:31.860 --> 02:16:34.260
They had compulsive behavior.

02:16:34.260 --> 02:16:36.330
So these are people that
are suffering in their life

02:16:36.330 --> 02:16:39.090
because their compulsions
are really strong.

02:16:39.090 --> 02:16:41.310
So it's not just being really nit-picky

02:16:41.310 --> 02:16:42.630
or really orderly in one case

02:16:42.630 --> 02:16:44.160
and having full blown OCD in the other,

02:16:44.160 --> 02:16:47.340
both sets of subjects
are challenged in life

02:16:47.340 --> 02:16:49.140
because they're having relationship issues

02:16:49.140 --> 02:16:50.700
or job-related issues, et cetera,

02:16:50.700 --> 02:16:52.383
because they are that compulsive.

02:16:53.370 --> 02:16:55.530
However, the individuals with

02:16:55.530 --> 02:16:58.560
obsessive-compulsive personality disorder,

02:16:58.560 --> 02:17:01.950
they discounted the value
of delayed gratification

02:17:01.950 --> 02:17:03.300
significantly less than those

02:17:03.300 --> 02:17:05.403
with obsessive-compulsive disorder.

02:17:06.270 --> 02:17:07.440
What do I mean?

02:17:07.440 --> 02:17:09.060
They are both impairing disorders

02:17:09.060 --> 02:17:10.590
that are marked by compulsive behaviors,

02:17:10.590 --> 02:17:12.210
here I'm paraphrasing,

02:17:12.210 --> 02:17:14.160
but they can be
differentiated by the presence

02:17:14.160 --> 02:17:15.510
of obsessions in OCD.

02:17:15.510 --> 02:17:17.430
So obsessions in OCD.

02:17:17.430 --> 02:17:21.630
People with OCD are absolutely fixated

02:17:21.630 --> 02:17:24.300
on certain ideas and
those ideas are intrusive.

02:17:24.300 --> 02:17:26.190
Again, that's the hallmark theme.

02:17:26.190 --> 02:17:29.220
And by an excessive
capacity to delay reward

02:17:29.220 --> 02:17:31.470
in obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder.

02:17:31.470 --> 02:17:33.780
That is people who have
obsessive-compulsive

02:17:33.780 --> 02:17:36.450
personality disorder are really good

02:17:36.450 --> 02:17:38.220
at delaying gratification.

02:17:38.220 --> 02:17:41.670
So they are able to
concentrate very intensely

02:17:41.670 --> 02:17:44.310
and perform very intensely in ways

02:17:44.310 --> 02:17:46.300
that allow them to instill order

02:17:47.220 --> 02:17:49.320
such that they can delay reward.

02:17:49.320 --> 02:17:53.673
Now you can see why this
contour of symptoms,

02:17:54.540 --> 02:17:57.340
meaning that the people with OCD

02:17:58.200 --> 02:18:00.930
are experiencing intrusive thoughts,

02:18:00.930 --> 02:18:03.060
whereas the people with
obsessive-compulsive

02:18:03.060 --> 02:18:06.180
personality disorder
show an enhanced ability

02:18:06.180 --> 02:18:07.833
to defer gratification.

02:18:10.110 --> 02:18:11.370
You could see how that would lead

02:18:11.370 --> 02:18:12.810
to very different outcomes.

02:18:12.810 --> 02:18:15.570
People with obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder

02:18:15.570 --> 02:18:18.630
can actually leverage
that personality disorder

02:18:18.630 --> 02:18:21.090
to perform better in
certain domains of life,

02:18:21.090 --> 02:18:22.230
not all domains of life,

02:18:22.230 --> 02:18:23.070
because remember, again,

02:18:23.070 --> 02:18:25.380
these people are in this study

02:18:25.380 --> 02:18:28.260
and they're showing up as
experiencing challenges in life

02:18:28.260 --> 02:18:31.410
because of their obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder.

02:18:31.410 --> 02:18:33.840
Nonetheless, people with
obsessive-compulsive

02:18:33.840 --> 02:18:35.310
personality disorder, you could imagine,

02:18:35.310 --> 02:18:39.090
would be very good at say architecture

02:18:39.090 --> 02:18:41.670
or anything that involves
instilling a ton of order.

02:18:41.670 --> 02:18:44.820
Maybe sushi chef, for instance,
maybe a chef in general.

02:18:44.820 --> 02:18:46.800
I know chefs that just
kind of throw things around

02:18:46.800 --> 02:18:50.100
like the chef on the Muppets
and just like throw things

02:18:50.100 --> 02:18:51.540
everywhere and still produce amazing food.

02:18:51.540 --> 02:18:54.270
And then there's some people
there incredibly exacting,

02:18:54.270 --> 02:18:55.830
they're just incredibly precise.

02:18:55.830 --> 02:18:56.910
I think that movie, what is it?

02:18:56.910 --> 02:18:58.440
Jiro Dreams of Sushi?

02:18:58.440 --> 02:19:00.390
That movie is incredible.

02:19:00.390 --> 02:19:02.190
Certainly not saying he
has obsessive-compulsive

02:19:02.190 --> 02:19:04.350
personality disorder, but
I think it's fair to say

02:19:04.350 --> 02:19:09.180
that he is obsessive or
extremely meticulous and orderly

02:19:09.180 --> 02:19:10.950
about everything from start to finish.

02:19:10.950 --> 02:19:13.770
You can imagine a huge array
of different occupations

02:19:13.770 --> 02:19:16.380
and life endeavors where
this would be beneficial,

02:19:16.380 --> 02:19:18.510
science being one of them
where data collection

02:19:18.510 --> 02:19:22.050
and analysis is exceedingly
important that one be precise,

02:19:22.050 --> 02:19:25.710
or mathematics or physics or engineering,

02:19:25.710 --> 02:19:27.990
anything where precision has a payoff

02:19:27.990 --> 02:19:32.100
and gaining precision takes time and delay

02:19:32.100 --> 02:19:34.200
of immediate gratification,

02:19:34.200 --> 02:19:35.790
you can imagine that obsessive-compulsive

02:19:35.790 --> 02:19:38.070
personality disorder would synergize well

02:19:38.070 --> 02:19:40.230
with those sorts of
activities and professions.

02:19:40.230 --> 02:19:43.140
Whereas obsessive-compulsive
disorder is really intrusive.

02:19:43.140 --> 02:19:46.140
It's preventing functionality in many

02:19:46.140 --> 02:19:47.730
different domains of life.

02:19:47.730 --> 02:19:51.330
So the key takeaway here is that

02:19:51.330 --> 02:19:53.730
when we use the words
obsessive-compulsive,

02:19:53.730 --> 02:19:55.530
or we call someone obsessive-compulsive,

02:19:55.530 --> 02:19:57.240
or we are trying to
evaluate whether or not

02:19:57.240 --> 02:19:58.500
we are obsessive compulsive,

02:19:58.500 --> 02:20:02.220
it's very important that we highlight

02:20:02.220 --> 02:20:05.520
that obsessive compulsive
disorder is very intrusive.

02:20:05.520 --> 02:20:06.780
It involves intrusive thoughts

02:20:06.780 --> 02:20:09.120
and it interrupts with
normal functioning in life.

02:20:09.120 --> 02:20:11.490
Whereas obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder,

02:20:11.490 --> 02:20:14.280
while it can interrupt
normal functioning in life,

02:20:14.280 --> 02:20:16.590
it also can be productive.

02:20:16.590 --> 02:20:19.080
It can enhance functioning
in life, not just in work,

02:20:19.080 --> 02:20:21.120
but perhaps at home as well.

02:20:21.120 --> 02:20:23.430
If you are somebody and
you have family members

02:20:23.430 --> 02:20:25.260
that really place enormous value on having

02:20:25.260 --> 02:20:27.600
a beautiful and highly organized home,

02:20:27.600 --> 02:20:29.940
well, then it could lend
itself well to that.

02:20:29.940 --> 02:20:31.620
it's going to be a matter
of degrees, of course.

02:20:31.620 --> 02:20:33.150
None of these things is an absolute,

02:20:33.150 --> 02:20:34.320
it's going to be on a continuum,

02:20:34.320 --> 02:20:36.690
but I think it is fair to
say that obsessive-compulsive

02:20:36.690 --> 02:20:39.120
disorder, whether or
not in mild, moderate,

02:20:39.120 --> 02:20:43.410
or severe form is impairing
normal functioning,

02:20:43.410 --> 02:20:45.930
whereas obsessive-compulsive
personality disorder,

02:20:45.930 --> 02:20:48.270
there's a range of expressions of that,

02:20:48.270 --> 02:20:49.590
some of which can be adaptive,

02:20:49.590 --> 02:20:51.030
some of which can be maladaptive,

02:20:51.030 --> 02:20:53.670
and again, it's all going
to depend on context.

02:20:53.670 --> 02:20:56.610
Before we conclude, I do
want to touch on something

02:20:56.610 --> 02:20:58.530
that I think a lot of people experience

02:20:58.530 --> 02:21:00.600
and that's superstitions.

02:21:00.600 --> 02:21:02.070
Superstitions are fascinating,

02:21:02.070 --> 02:21:05.640
and there's some fascinating
research on superstitions.

02:21:05.640 --> 02:21:08.640
One particular study that I'm a big fan of

02:21:08.640 --> 02:21:11.250
is the work of Bence Olveczky at Harvard.

02:21:11.250 --> 02:21:14.550
He studies motor sequences
and motor learning,

02:21:14.550 --> 02:21:17.520
and he has beautiful
data on how people learn,

02:21:17.520 --> 02:21:19.500
for instance, a tennis swing

02:21:19.500 --> 02:21:23.160
and the patterns that
they engage in early on

02:21:23.160 --> 02:21:25.290
and then the patterns
of swinging that they,

02:21:25.290 --> 02:21:26.640
swinging the racket that is,

02:21:26.640 --> 02:21:29.730
that they engage in later
as they acquire more skill.

02:21:29.730 --> 02:21:31.710
And basically the takeaway is that

02:21:31.710 --> 02:21:34.860
the amount of error or
variation from swing to swing

02:21:34.860 --> 02:21:38.193
is dramatically reduced
as they acquire skill.

02:21:39.150 --> 02:21:41.340
That's all fine and good,

02:21:41.340 --> 02:21:43.620
and there's some
beautiful mechanistic data

02:21:43.620 --> 02:21:46.140
that he and others have discovered

02:21:46.140 --> 02:21:48.240
to support how that comes to be,

02:21:48.240 --> 02:21:50.940
but they also explore animal models,

02:21:50.940 --> 02:21:54.870
in particular, rats pressing
sequences of buttons

02:21:54.870 --> 02:21:57.330
and levers to obtain a reward.

02:21:57.330 --> 02:21:59.520
Believe it or not, rats are pretty smart.

02:21:59.520 --> 02:22:00.960
I've seen this with my own eyes.

02:22:00.960 --> 02:22:04.470
You can teach a rat to press
a lever for a pellet of food.

02:22:04.470 --> 02:22:07.350
Rats can also learn to press levers

02:22:07.350 --> 02:22:10.140
in a particular sequence in order to gain

02:22:10.140 --> 02:22:11.310
a piece of food.

02:22:11.310 --> 02:22:14.400
And they can actually learn to press

02:22:14.400 --> 02:22:17.280
an enormous number of levers
in very particular sequences

02:22:17.280 --> 02:22:19.200
in order to obtain pellets of food.

02:22:19.200 --> 02:22:21.570
You can also give them
little buttons to press

02:22:21.570 --> 02:22:25.920
or even a paddle to, or I
should say a pedal, excuse me,

02:22:25.920 --> 02:22:27.390
to stomp on with their foot

02:22:27.390 --> 02:22:29.190
in order to obtain a pellet of food.

02:22:29.190 --> 02:22:32.100
Basically rats can learn
exactly what they need to do

02:22:32.100 --> 02:22:34.770
in order to obtain a piece of food,

02:22:34.770 --> 02:22:35.670
especially if they're made

02:22:35.670 --> 02:22:37.590
a little bit hungry first.

02:22:37.590 --> 02:22:40.080
Bence's lab has published beautiful data

02:22:40.080 --> 02:22:43.650
showing that as animals and humans

02:22:43.650 --> 02:22:45.780
come to learn a particular motor sequence,

02:22:45.780 --> 02:22:49.080
very often they will
introduce motor patterns

02:22:49.080 --> 02:22:52.860
in that sequence that are
irrelevant to the outcome

02:22:52.860 --> 02:22:54.600
and yet that persist.

02:22:54.600 --> 02:22:56.190
If you've ever watched a game of baseball,

02:22:56.190 --> 02:22:58.080
you've seen this before.

02:22:58.080 --> 02:23:00.760
Oftentimes the pitcher
up on the mound will

02:23:01.740 --> 02:23:03.960
bring the ball to their chin.

02:23:03.960 --> 02:23:05.550
They'll look over their shoulder,

02:23:05.550 --> 02:23:07.830
they'll look back over the other shoulder,

02:23:07.830 --> 02:23:11.790
and then they will, of course,
reel back and pitch the ball.

02:23:11.790 --> 02:23:13.020
But if you watch closely,

02:23:13.020 --> 02:23:15.480
oftentimes there are components
in the motor sequence,

02:23:15.480 --> 02:23:18.000
which are completely
unrelated to the pitch.

02:23:18.000 --> 02:23:19.650
They're not looking necessarily to see

02:23:19.650 --> 02:23:21.150
if someone's stealing a base.

02:23:21.150 --> 02:23:23.190
They're not necessarily looking

02:23:23.190 --> 02:23:26.010
down at home plate where the batter is.

02:23:26.010 --> 02:23:27.600
They're also doing things like

02:23:27.600 --> 02:23:28.680
touching the back of their ear

02:23:28.680 --> 02:23:30.510
before they bring the ball to their chin

02:23:30.510 --> 02:23:31.650
or adjusting their hat.

02:23:31.650 --> 02:23:33.270
And if you watch individual pictures,

02:23:33.270 --> 02:23:35.310
what you'll find is that
they'll do the same sequence

02:23:35.310 --> 02:23:38.310
of completely irrelevant motor patterns

02:23:38.310 --> 02:23:40.590
before each and every single pitch.

02:23:40.590 --> 02:23:43.860
Similarly, rats that have
been trained to, for instance,

02:23:43.860 --> 02:23:47.070
hit two levers and step on a pedal

02:23:47.070 --> 02:23:49.810
with their left hind foot, and then

02:23:50.700 --> 02:23:53.220
tap a button up above
that is the red button,

02:23:53.220 --> 02:23:55.680
will do that to gain a piece of food.

02:23:55.680 --> 02:23:57.720
But sometimes they'll
also introduce a pattern

02:23:57.720 --> 02:24:00.510
into that motor sequence where they will

02:24:00.510 --> 02:24:01.680
shake their tail a little bit,

02:24:01.680 --> 02:24:02.820
or they'll turn their head a little bit,

02:24:02.820 --> 02:24:05.670
or they'll move their ears
a little bit, et cetera.

02:24:05.670 --> 02:24:07.440
Motor patterns that have nothing to do

02:24:07.440 --> 02:24:10.980
with obtaining the
particular outcome in mind.

02:24:10.980 --> 02:24:12.780
In other words, you could eliminate

02:24:12.780 --> 02:24:14.430
certain components of the motor sequence

02:24:14.430 --> 02:24:16.620
and it would not matter, the
rat would still get the pellet,

02:24:16.620 --> 02:24:19.560
the pitcher would still be able to pitch,

02:24:19.560 --> 02:24:21.710
and yet that get
introduced because somehow

02:24:22.770 --> 02:24:25.710
because they were
performed again and again

02:24:25.710 --> 02:24:28.830
prior to successful trials,

02:24:28.830 --> 02:24:32.430
the rat or the human baseball pitcher

02:24:32.430 --> 02:24:34.470
comes to believe in some way

02:24:34.470 --> 02:24:37.200
that it was involved in
generating the outcome,

02:24:37.200 --> 02:24:39.120
hence superstition, right?

02:24:39.120 --> 02:24:41.400
I confess I have a few superstitions.

02:24:41.400 --> 02:24:43.470
I occasionally will knock on wood.

02:24:43.470 --> 02:24:45.030
I'll say something that I want to happen,

02:24:45.030 --> 02:24:47.100
and I'll say, oh, knock on
wood, and I'll just do it.

02:24:47.100 --> 02:24:49.650
And occasionally I'll
challenge myself and think,

02:24:49.650 --> 02:24:51.450
ah, I don't want to knock,
don't knock on wood,

02:24:51.450 --> 02:24:53.310
Andrew, don't do that.

02:24:53.310 --> 02:24:55.350
I don't think anyone
wants to be superstitious,

02:24:55.350 --> 02:24:56.250
I certainly don't.

02:24:56.250 --> 02:24:57.210
And so every once in a while,

02:24:57.210 --> 02:25:00.900
I'll just challenge it, and I
won't actually knock on wood.

02:25:00.900 --> 02:25:02.700
I'm admitting this to you to kind of

02:25:03.750 --> 02:25:05.490
I guess normalize some of this.

02:25:05.490 --> 02:25:08.280
Some people have superstitions

02:25:08.280 --> 02:25:12.030
that border on or even become compulsions.

02:25:12.030 --> 02:25:13.320
They really come to believe

02:25:13.320 --> 02:25:14.940
that if they don't knock on wood,

02:25:14.940 --> 02:25:16.350
that something terrible
is going to happen,

02:25:16.350 --> 02:25:18.240
maybe something in particular.

02:25:18.240 --> 02:25:19.890
Or in the case of the baseball pitcher,

02:25:19.890 --> 02:25:21.630
they come to believe
that if they don't touch

02:25:21.630 --> 02:25:24.573
their right ear before they
reel back on the pitch,

02:25:25.710 --> 02:25:27.870
that the pitch won't be any good

02:25:27.870 --> 02:25:29.130
or that they're going to lose the game.

02:25:29.130 --> 02:25:30.570
Well, I don't know what
their thought process is.

02:25:30.570 --> 02:25:32.760
Now, I also don't know
what the rat is thinking,

02:25:32.760 --> 02:25:36.210
but the rat is clearly doing something

02:25:36.210 --> 02:25:40.050
or thinking something is
related to the final outcome.

02:25:40.050 --> 02:25:42.270
I don't know of any studies
where they've intervened

02:25:42.270 --> 02:25:45.570
with the particular
superstition-like behaviors

02:25:45.570 --> 02:25:47.730
of the rat to see whether or not the rat

02:25:47.730 --> 02:25:50.760
somehow doesn't continue
to do the motor sequence

02:25:50.760 --> 02:25:51.593
to get the pellet.

02:25:51.593 --> 02:25:52.710
We don't know the rats, they're rats.

02:25:52.710 --> 02:25:54.300
I don't speak rat, most people don't,

02:25:54.300 --> 02:25:55.650
or if you speak to a rat,

02:25:55.650 --> 02:25:57.210
if it speaks back, it's not in English.

02:25:57.210 --> 02:26:01.500
Anyway, the point is that
superstitions are beliefs

02:26:01.500 --> 02:26:05.890
that we, on an individual
scale, come to believe

02:26:06.810 --> 02:26:09.510
are linked to the
probability of an outcome

02:26:09.510 --> 02:26:12.870
when in fact we know, we actually know

02:26:12.870 --> 02:26:16.200
in our rational minds, they
have no real relationship

02:26:16.200 --> 02:26:17.033
to the outcome.

02:26:18.060 --> 02:26:20.190
Superstitions can become full-blown

02:26:20.190 --> 02:26:21.940
compulsions and obsessions

02:26:22.800 --> 02:26:25.200
when we repeat them often enough

02:26:25.200 --> 02:26:26.580
that they become automatic.

02:26:26.580 --> 02:26:28.890
And I think this is what
we observe most of the time

02:26:28.890 --> 02:26:31.410
when we see a pitcher touching their ear,

02:26:31.410 --> 02:26:33.720
or for instance, in
tennis, you see this a lot,

02:26:33.720 --> 02:26:36.600
you'll see someone
they'll slap their shoes.

02:26:36.600 --> 02:26:38.040
Often, I see this, they'll like slap

02:26:38.040 --> 02:26:39.570
the undersides of their soles.

02:26:39.570 --> 02:26:41.700
They may tell themselves
that this is, I don't know,

02:26:41.700 --> 02:26:43.560
maybe moving out some
of the dust or something

02:26:43.560 --> 02:26:44.550
in the bottoms of their soles,

02:26:44.550 --> 02:26:46.560
that gives them more
traction and they want that

02:26:46.560 --> 02:26:48.930
to be ready for the serve
or something like that.

02:26:48.930 --> 02:26:50.340
And maybe there's some truth to that,

02:26:50.340 --> 02:26:52.377
but here, what we're referring to

02:26:52.377 --> 02:26:55.470
are behaviors that really
have no rational relationship

02:26:55.470 --> 02:26:58.383
to the outcome, and yet we
perform in a compulsive way.

02:26:59.610 --> 02:27:03.360
People with OCD, yes, tend
to have more superstitions.

02:27:03.360 --> 02:27:05.550
People with more superstitions, yes,

02:27:05.550 --> 02:27:08.547
tend to have a tendency towards OCD

02:27:08.547 --> 02:27:10.740
and I should mention, obsessive-compulsive

02:27:10.740 --> 02:27:12.150
personality disorder.

02:27:12.150 --> 02:27:15.780
If you think way back to the
first part of this episode,

02:27:15.780 --> 02:27:18.000
when I was just describing
what the brain does, right?

02:27:18.000 --> 02:27:18.833
What does your brain do?

02:27:18.833 --> 02:27:20.700
Housekeeping functions to keep you alive

02:27:20.700 --> 02:27:22.980
and it's a prediction machine.

02:27:22.980 --> 02:27:25.320
Your neural circuits, you,

02:27:25.320 --> 02:27:28.410
have an enormous amount
of biological investment

02:27:28.410 --> 02:27:30.630
of real estate, literally,
cells and chemicals

02:27:30.630 --> 02:27:34.530
that are there to try and
make your world predictable

02:27:34.530 --> 02:27:36.570
and to try and give you control,

02:27:36.570 --> 02:27:39.390
or at least the sense of
control over that world

02:27:39.390 --> 02:27:42.330
and that's a normal process.

02:27:42.330 --> 02:27:45.900
Low-level superstitions,
moderate superstitions

02:27:45.900 --> 02:27:50.130
represent a kind of a
healthy range, I would say,

02:27:50.130 --> 02:27:55.110
of behaviors that are aimed
at generating predictability

02:27:55.110 --> 02:27:57.240
that don't disrupt normal function.

02:27:57.240 --> 02:27:59.220
Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder,

02:27:59.220 --> 02:28:00.420
provided is not too severe,

02:28:00.420 --> 02:28:02.580
would I think represent the next level

02:28:02.580 --> 02:28:04.440
along that continuum.

02:28:04.440 --> 02:28:06.330
And then obsessive-compulsive disorder,

02:28:06.330 --> 02:28:07.740
as I pointed out earlier,

02:28:07.740 --> 02:28:10.050
is really a case of highly debilitating,

02:28:10.050 --> 02:28:14.220
highly intrusive, really
overtake of neural circuitry

02:28:14.220 --> 02:28:15.570
over our thoughts and behaviors

02:28:15.570 --> 02:28:19.830
that requires very
dedicated, very persistent,

02:28:19.830 --> 02:28:23.820
and very effective
treatments in order to stop

02:28:23.820 --> 02:28:25.200
those obsessions and compulsions

02:28:25.200 --> 02:28:27.600
and the anxiety that links them

02:28:27.600 --> 02:28:30.570
somewhat counterintuitively
by teaching people

02:28:30.570 --> 02:28:33.330
to tolerate that level
of increased anxiety

02:28:33.330 --> 02:28:35.040
and interrupt those patterns.

02:28:35.040 --> 02:28:37.020
And fortunately, as we described earlier,

02:28:37.020 --> 02:28:40.170
such treatments exist,
cognitive behavioral therapy,

02:28:40.170 --> 02:28:43.380
drug treatments like
SSRIs, also drug treatments

02:28:43.380 --> 02:28:46.260
that tap into the glutamate system

02:28:46.260 --> 02:28:49.650
and into perhaps also the dopamine system,

02:28:49.650 --> 02:28:50.880
the so-called neuroleptics.

02:28:50.880 --> 02:28:53.340
And then, as we described,

02:28:53.340 --> 02:28:55.260
there's now an extensive
exploration of things

02:28:55.260 --> 02:28:58.230
like ketamine, psilocybin, cannabis,

02:28:58.230 --> 02:29:00.420
the initial studies don't
seem to hold much promise

02:29:00.420 --> 02:29:02.580
for cannabis and CBD and
the treatment of OCD,

02:29:02.580 --> 02:29:04.740
but who knows, maybe more
studies will come along

02:29:04.740 --> 02:29:06.630
that will change that story.

02:29:06.630 --> 02:29:08.130
And then of course,
brain machine interface

02:29:08.130 --> 02:29:10.140
like transcranial magnetic stimulation.

02:29:10.140 --> 02:29:12.690
And then just to remind you
what I already told you before

02:29:12.690 --> 02:29:15.390
combinations of behavioral
and drug treatments

02:29:15.390 --> 02:29:16.850
and brain machine interface, I think,

02:29:16.850 --> 02:29:18.420
is really where the future lies.

02:29:18.420 --> 02:29:21.840
Fortunately, good treatments exist.

02:29:21.840 --> 02:29:24.990
We cannot say that any
one individual treatment

02:29:24.990 --> 02:29:26.220
works for everybody.

02:29:26.220 --> 02:29:29.430
There are fairly large
percentages of people

02:29:29.430 --> 02:29:31.530
that won't respond to one
set of treatments or another,

02:29:31.530 --> 02:29:34.080
and therefore one has
to try different ones.

02:29:34.080 --> 02:29:37.830
And then there are the
so-called supplementation-based

02:29:37.830 --> 02:29:39.810
or more holistic therapies.

02:29:39.810 --> 02:29:41.330
Today, I've tried to cover each and all

02:29:41.330 --> 02:29:44.910
of these in a fairly
substantial amount of detail.

